const DISCIPLE_TITLE = "The Disciple";
const DISCIPLE_PARAS = ["I was there from the very beginning of it all, though I didn’t know it at the time. I had left my ordinary life behind to follow John the Baptist out in the wilderness by the Jordan River. The air was dry and hot, carrying the scent of dust and river water, the crowds pressing close as John preached with a voice like thunder rolling over the hills. His camel-hair cloak scratched against his skin, his eyes burned with holy urgency. “Repent,” he would cry, “for the kingdom of heaven is at hand!” I felt the weight of my own sins heavy on my shoulders as I watched others go down into the muddy waters—and I sensed, dimly, that the weight was the whole point, that something was wrong in me that washing alone could not reach.", "Then one ordinary afternoon, the sun glinting off the river, a man approached from the north. He was ordinary in appearance—strong build from years of labor, dust on His sandals, a quiet steadiness in His step. John saw Him and stopped everything. His voice cracked with reverence as he pointed: “Behold, the Lamb of God, who takes away the sin of the world!” Those words pierced me like a spear. I had carried lambs to the altar; I knew what a lamb was for. A lamb was for a sinner who could not pay his own debt, a life given in the place of another. I did not grasp it then, but a seed was planted in me that hour—that this Man had come not only to preach the kingdom but to carry away the very thing that had bowed my shoulders. My heart leaped, a strange warmth spreading through my chest. Who was this Jesus of Nazareth? I had to know.", "Two of us—Andrew and I—left John that day and began following Jesus at a distance. The path was rocky, the sun warm on our backs. He turned, saw us, and asked gently, “What are you seeking?” His voice was calm, deep, like still water after a storm. We stumbled over our words: “Rabbi, where are you staying?” He smiled, eyes full of knowing, and said, “Come and see.” We walked with Him to where He was staying as the sun dipped lower, painting the hills in gold and shadow. We sat with Him until the stars came out, talking deep into the night. I cannot remember every word, but I felt seen—truly seen—for the first time. His gaze held no judgment, only light and invitation. It struck me even then that I had not found Him so much as He had let Himself be found; He had turned and asked the first question, as if the seeking had begun on His side long before it began on mine. A fire kindled in my spirit, quiet but growing, as if something ancient and new was awakening inside me. That night, sleep came lightly; my mind hummed with possibility.", "The next morning, Andrew found his brother Simon and brought him to Jesus. I stood close as Jesus looked at Simon—rough fisherman, broad shoulders, calloused hands—and said, “You are Simon the son of John. You shall be called Cephas,” which means Peter, the rock. I saw Simon’s face shift from surprise to something deeper, a mix of awe and uncertainty flickering in his eyes. We all felt it: this man named things not only as they were but as they would become. He looked at a wavering fisherman and called him rock, and I began to wonder whether this was how He meant to save us—not by finding finished men, but by laying hold of unfinished ones and remaking them into what they could never become on their own.", "We traveled together then into Galilee. The roads wound through fields of grain and olive groves, the air sweet with ripening fruit. Jesus found Philip along the way. “Follow me,” He said simply. Philip’s face lit up like dawn breaking, and he hurried to tell Nathanael. I was there when Philip brought him. Nathanael was skeptical under the shade of a fig tree, wiping sweat from his brow. “Can anything good come out of Nazareth?” he muttered. But we went anyway, and I began to notice that the kingdom advanced this way—one person found, then sent to find another, the circle of the rescued widening with every step.", "Jesus saw Nathanael approaching and called out, “Behold, an Israelite indeed, in whom there is no deceit!” Nathanael’s steps faltered. “How do you know me?” Jesus replied softly, yet with power, “Before Philip called you, when you were under the fig tree, I saw you.” The words hung in the warm air. I felt a shiver run down my spine, the hairs on my arms rising. How could He know? He had seen Nathanael before Nathanael had sought Him, known him before he was known. Nathanael dropped to his knees almost at once, voice trembling with wonder: “Rabbi, you are the Son of God! You are the King of Israel!” Jesus placed a hand on his shoulder, His touch steady and reassuring. “Because I said to you, ‘I saw you under the fig tree,’ do you believe? You will see greater things than these. Truly, truly, I say to you, you will see heaven opened, and the angels of God ascending and descending on the Son of Man.”", "My own pulse quickened. The ground beneath my feet felt sacred, as if the veil between heaven and earth had thinned. A holy fear mixed with joy in my belly—like standing before a burning bush that did not consume. Heaven opened, He had said—the very heaven that sin had shut against us—and He Himself the ladder between earth and sky, the meeting place of God and man. If that was true, then He was the one thing we had always needed and never had.", "The following day we went up to Cana for a wedding. The village buzzed with celebration: music drifting on the breeze, tables laden with bread and fish, laughter echoing off stone walls. Families in their finest robes, children darting between legs. Jesus’ mother was there, her face lined with care and wisdom. I watched her approach Him quietly amid the joy. “They have no wine,” she said. Jesus answered with tenderness but firmness, “Woman, what does this have to do with me? My hour has not yet come.” Yet she turned to the servants with calm trust: “Do whatever He tells you.” The word about His hour lodged in me; even in the middle of a feast He seemed to be measuring His steps toward some appointed time none of us could see.", "Six stone jars stood nearby, massive and cool to the touch, used for purification rites. Each could hold twenty or thirty gallons. Jesus spoke to the servants, His voice clear above the crowd: “Fill the jars with water.” They obeyed, drawing from the well until water brimmed at the top, spilling slightly onto the dusty ground. Then, “Now draw some out and take it to the master of the feast.” It did not escape me, even then, that He began with the jars kept for washing away defilement, and turned what they held into the wine of gladness.", "I held my breath, heart thudding against my ribs, as a servant carried the cup across the courtyard. The master tasted it—his eyes widened in delight. He pulled the bridegroom aside, voice loud with surprise: “Everyone serves the good wine first, and when people have drunk freely, then the poor wine. But you have kept the good wine until now!” The aroma reached me: rich, fragrant, better than any wine I had known. My hands shook as I realized what had happened. Water—plain, ordinary water—had become this. I looked at Jesus. He stood quietly, not seeking praise, His face peaceful in the lamplight.", "Awe crashed over me like waves on the shore. My throat tightened, tears stung my eyes unbidden. This was glory revealed, not in thunder but in quiet abundance—and it was the abundance that undid me. The world’s joy always ran out and ended in the lesser thing; here was One who kept the best until last and poured it out freely on people who had done nothing to earn it. I began to suspect that whatever this Man had come to give, He would give like this: not grudgingly, not as wages owed to the deserving, but lavishly, beyond all reckoning. Faith took root deeper in my soul that night, like a seed in fertile soil after rain.", "From Cana we went down to Capernaum by the sea. The journey was dusty, the lake sparkling blue in the distance. Jesus walked with that unhurried strength, and simply being in His presence made the road lighter. We stayed in a simple home near the shore, the sound of waves lapping stones a constant murmur. Evenings, we gathered as lamps flickered, casting warm shadows on the walls. His teachings sank into us: the kingdom breaking in now, among the ordinary. His words were like bread—nourishing, filling. I felt my old life peeling away, layer by layer, and I sensed that following Him would not leave me as He had found me; He meant to remake me, not merely improve me.", "From Capernaum we made our way up to Jerusalem for the Passover. The road climbed steadily through the hills of Judea, the dust clinging to our sandals and the hems of our robes, turning them a pale gray. Olive groves gave way to rocky terraces planted with vines, and the air grew warmer as we drew nearer to the holy city. Jesus walked ahead with that same unhurried strength, His shoulders steady under the weight of His simple cloak. We were going up for the feast of the lamb’s blood—the night Israel had been spared because a death fell on a lamb in her place—and though I did not yet draw the line between the feast and the Man, the two were walking up to Jerusalem together.", "As we approached, the city rose before us on its hill, walls gleaming white in the sun, the Temple rising above all like a crown of gold and marble. The roads thickened with pilgrims—families leading lambs, merchants with their wares, voices lifted in songs of ascent. The smell of dust mixed with woodsmoke and the faint sweetness of blooming almond trees along the way. My heart swelled with the familiar thrill of the feast, but this time it was deeper, sharper, because He was with us—the Lamb of God walking up to the city of the lambs.", "Inside the city, the narrow streets echoed with footsteps and bargaining. We moved toward the Temple mount, where the outer court buzzed like a marketplace. Animals lowed and bleated, coins clinked loudly on tables, merchants shouted their prices for doves and cattle. The air was thick with the scent of dung, incense, and sweat. Jesus stopped suddenly amid the chaos. I saw His face change—still calm, but kindled with a holy fire. His eyes flashed as He took cords and fashioned a whip, the leather twisting firmly in His calloused hands.", "Then He acted. The whip cracked through the air with sharp authority, tables overturned with heavy crashes that scattered coins across the stone pavement like startled sparrows. Doves fluttered wildly from their cages. “Take these things away!” His voice rang out clear and commanding, echoing off the colonnade walls. “Do not make my Father’s house a house of trade!” Merchants scrambled, their faces twisted in anger and fear, grabbing what they could as animals bolted through the crowd. I stood rooted nearby, my pulse pounding in my ears, the dust rising around my ankles. A holy dread and awe gripped me—my knees felt weak, yet my spirit soared. This was no ordinary man. The zeal of His Father’s house burned in Him like a living flame. I had imagined salvation, when I imagined it at all, as something gentle; now I saw that it began with a refusal to let what was unclean stand in God’s presence. He loved His Father’s house too fiercely to leave it defiled—and I sensed the same love would not be content to leave us defiled either.", "Some of the leaders pressed forward, their robes brushing the ground, voices sharp with challenge. “What sign do you show us for doing these things?” Jesus stood tall amid the overturned tables and answered with steady power, “Destroy this temple, and in three days I will raise it up.” They scoffed, gesturing at the massive stones around us, but His words sank deep into me, mysterious and weighty. I felt a trembling inside, as if the very ground of my understanding was shifting. Later, much later, I would grasp that He spoke of His body—of a death and a rising that would become the true temple, the real meeting place between God and sinners. In that moment I only knew I had seen glory and authority walk among the courts of God, and that He had laid some cornerstone I would not see finished for a long time.", "We remained in Jerusalem for the days of the Passover feast, and the city continued to pulse around us like a living thing. The narrow streets were crowded with pilgrims from every corner of Israel and beyond, their voices rising in a constant murmur of prayer and bargaining. The air carried the mingled scents of roasted lambs, burning incense from the altars, and the sharp tang of animals penned near the Temple. Jesus moved among the people with quiet authority, and I stayed close, my sandals brushing the worn stones that had known the feet of kings and prophets before us.", "In those days He performed many signs—miracles that left me breathless. I saw a woman whose hands had been twisted with pain for years reach out to Him; at His gentle word her fingers straightened, supple and strong again. She wept openly, clutching her healed hands to her chest as if afraid they might vanish. Another time, a man blind from birth was led forward by his relatives. Jesus spoke to him with kindness, laid hands on his eyes, and suddenly the man cried out, seeing the bright sky and the faces around him for the first time. His shouts of joy echoed off the Temple walls, drawing a crowd. Each healing stirred a deep trembling in my soul, and slowly I began to see the signs for what they were: not displays of power for their own sake, but small undoings of the ruin sin had worked in the world—previews of what God meant to do for the whole broken race of Adam. Being near Jesus felt like standing close to a blazing fire: it warmed and purified, yet it also exposed every shadow within me. I felt small, yet strangely cherished.", "Many began to believe in His name because of these signs, pressing closer in the Temple courts, their faces alight with wonder. Yet I noticed something in Jesus’ eyes—a deep knowing that went beyond our excitement. He did not entrust Himself fully to them, for He knew what was in every human heart. That awareness in Him both comforted and unsettled me; it made me realize He saw not only our hopes but our hidden failings, our fleeting enthusiasms. And still He had come. He knew exactly the kind of people He had set out to save, and He had not turned back. I felt exposed in His presence, yet never rejected, and I began to learn the strange shape of His mercy: He saw the worst of us first, and loved us anyway.", "One night, as the city quieted and the oil lamps cast long shadows in the upper rooms where we stayed, a man named Nicodemus came to Jesus under cover of darkness. He was a ruler of the Jews, a Pharisee, his fine robes whispering against the stone floor as he entered. I sat quietly in the corner, watching. The air was still and cool, carrying the faint scent of olive oil from the lamps. Nicodemus spoke with respect, yet I could sense the careful caution in his voice: “Rabbi, we know that you are a teacher come from God, for no one can do these signs that you do unless God is with him.”", "Jesus looked at him steadily, the lamp light playing across His face, and answered with words that pierced the quiet like a shaft of light: “Truly, truly, I say to you, unless one is born again he cannot see the kingdom of God.” Nicodemus blinked, confusion crossing his scholarly features. “How can a man be born when he is old? Can he enter a second time into his mother’s womb and be born?” I felt the weight of that question in my own chest—how indeed? Here was a man who had kept the law his whole life, and Jesus told him that all his keeping was not enough, that he needed not improvement but a beginning he could not give himself. The idea seemed impossible, yet coming from Jesus it carried a mysterious pull.", "Jesus leaned forward slightly, His voice calm and deep, carrying the authority of one who had seen heaven itself. “Truly, truly, I say to you, unless one is born of water and the Spirit, he cannot enter the kingdom of God. That which is born of the flesh is flesh, and that which is born of the Spirit is spirit. Do not marvel that I said to you, ‘You must be born again.’ The wind blows where it wishes, and you hear its sound, but you do not know where it comes from or where it goes. So it is with everyone who is born of the Spirit.” Salvation, then, was not something a man would achieve but something that would be done to him, as hidden and sovereign as the wind.", "I sat motionless, the words sinking into me like rain into dry earth. A strange warmth spread through my limbs, a sense of something vast opening up before me. The night breeze stirred outside, rustling through the narrow street, and I thought of the wind Jesus spoke of—unseen yet powerful, beyond our control. My mind reeled, yet my spirit leaped in recognition. This was no ordinary teaching; it reached into the hidden places of the heart. Nicodemus listened, his face a mixture of awe and struggle, asking more questions as the night deepened. Jesus answered with patience and depth, speaking of the Son of Man lifted up as Moses had lifted the serpent in the wilderness, so that whoever looked to Him in faith might live—and of God so loving the world that He gave His only Son, that whoever believes in Him should not perish but have eternal life. I had thought God’s love was a wage paid to the righteous. Now I heard that it reached down to a perishing world of sinners, and that it would cost the Father His own Son, lifted up like the bronze serpent for everyone bitten by the ancient poison to look upon and be healed.", "As the conversation unfolded, I felt my old understanding crumbling further, like clay jars being reshaped by the potter’s hands. Being with Jesus in that quiet upper room was like standing on holy ground; every word He spoke carried life, every glance invited me deeper. A quiet joy mingled with reverent fear in my heart—this man was opening the kingdom to us, not through rituals or bloodlines or careful obedience, but through a new birth and a Son lifted up, a gift only God could give. Nothing in me had purchased it. The night air felt charged, alive with possibility, and I knew, deep in my bones, that this was the best news I had ever heard.", "After the long night with Nicodemus, the hours slipping away under the flickering lamps until the sky outside began to pale with dawn, Jesus rose with quiet purpose. We left Jerusalem soon after, heading into the Judean countryside. The road led us out through the city gates, the morning air cool and fresh against our faces, carrying the scent of dew on wildflowers and the distant smoke from village hearth fires. The hills rolled gently before us, terraced with vineyards and dotted with sheep grazing under the watchful eyes of shepherds. Jesus walked with that steady, unhurried gait, His presence like a calm center amid the stirring world. Being with Him on that path made the journey feel sacred, every footfall echoing something deeper than mere travel. My sandals pressed into the soft earth, and I felt the night’s words still burning in my chest like embers that refused to cool—that salvation is a gift, that it comes by a birth from above and a Son given for the world.", "We came to a place near the Jordan where there was plenty of water, and there Jesus began to baptize. I stood waist-deep in the cool, flowing river beside Him, the current tugging gently at my legs, the sun warming my shoulders as it climbed higher. The water was clear and refreshing, sparkling as it moved over smooth stones. People came in small groups—humble farmers with calloused hands, women with infants strapped to their backs, even some from the city still marked by the dust of pilgrimage. Jesus would speak to each one with kindness and authority, His voice carrying over the murmur of the river. As they went down into the water and rose again, dripping and renewed, I saw tears on many faces, heard their quiet sobs of release. A holy hush would fall over the scene each time. I felt it too: a cleansing not just of the body but of something reaching into the hidden corners of the soul. This was the new birth made visible—the kingdom breaking in, just as He had said, gentle as water yet powerful enough to remake a life from the inside out.", "Not far away, at Aenon near Salim, John the Baptist was still baptizing. The news reached us through travelers: some of John’s own disciples were troubled, coming to him with furrowed brows and questioning voices. “Rabbi,” they said, “the one who was with you across the Jordan, to whom you bore witness—look, He is baptizing, and all are going to Him.” I could imagine John’s response, that fierce yet humble fire in his eyes. He had always pointed away from himself. Later, when the reports came back to us, they carried his words: “A person cannot receive even one thing unless it is given him from heaven. You yourselves bear me witness that I said, ‘I am not the Christ, but I have been sent before Him.’ He who has the bride is the bridegroom. The friend of the bridegroom, who stands and hears him, rejoices greatly at the bridegroom’s voice. Therefore this joy of mine is now complete. He must increase, but I must decrease.” I understood that the whole story was bending toward this one Man—that John, like the prophets before him, had only ever been pointing past himself to the Bridegroom who had finally come for His bride.", "Those words reached me like a deep, confirming peace. I pictured John standing by the waters he knew so well, his voice hoarse from preaching, yet radiant with joy at his own decrease. To diminish so that Christ might fill the whole horizon—I sensed that this surrender was not loss but the very pattern of life with Him. The countryside around us seemed brighter, the wildflowers more vivid, the sky a deeper blue, as if all creation leaned in to listen to the Bridegroom’s voice.", "But word soon spread to the Pharisees in Jerusalem that Jesus was making and baptizing more disciples than John. I sensed the gathering tension, like storm clouds building on the horizon, though Jesus remained untroubled. In His wisdom, He decided we should leave Judea and return to Galilee. The journey northward was longer this time, the paths winding through Samaria. The sun beat down mercilessly at midday, dust coating our throats and turning our robes pale. We passed fields of waving grain and ancient wells shaded by trees. My feet grew weary, yet walking in step with Him renewed my strength. He spoke little on the road, but His silence was full—comforting, like the steady presence of a father leading through unknown hills. He had set His face toward Samaria on purpose, though I did not yet know it, for there was someone there He meant to find.", "We came at last to Sychar in Samaria, weary from the heat. Jesus sat down beside Jacob’s well outside the village while we went into the town to buy food. The well was deep and ancient, its stones cool to the touch, the water far below glistening faintly. The sun was high, the air still and hot. I glanced back at Him as we left—sitting there alone in the shade, dust on His feet, choosing to rest in the one region every devout Jew hurried through. Even then I should have guessed He had come there for a reason. My spirit felt stretched and expectant, alive with the quiet knowledge that following this Man meant stepping continually into mystery and grace.", "I returned with the others from the village of Sychar, our arms laden with bread still warm from the ovens, fresh figs, and a skin of wine. The midday heat had begun to ease slightly, but sweat still trickled down my back beneath my robe, and the dust of the road clung to my skin. As we approached Jacob’s well, I saw Jesus still seated there on the low stone rim, His posture relaxed yet attentive. A Samaritan woman stood before Him, her water jar resting on the ground. She was alone—unusual for that hour—and carried herself with a guarded weariness, her veil slightly askew, her eyes cautious, the look of someone accustomed to coming when the other women would not.", "I slowed my steps, the others beside me doing the same. We stayed back a respectful distance, close enough to hear but not intrude. The air around the well felt charged, thicker than the ordinary heat, as if heaven itself had drawn near to this dusty patch of earth where a Jewish rabbi was speaking, against every custom, to a Samaritan woman of broken reputation. The woman’s voice carried clearly on the still air: “How is it that you, a Jew, ask for a drink from me, a woman of Samaria?” There was surprise in her tone, mixed with a faint edge of defiance. Jews and Samaritans did not share vessels or pleasant words; the old wound between our peoples ran deep. Yet Jesus crossed that wound as if it were nothing, His voice gentle but piercing, like cool water offered on a scorching day.", "“If you knew the gift of God, and who it is that is saying to you, ‘Give me a drink,’ you would have asked Him, and He would have given you living water.” He had come asking a favor, and ended by offering a gift—stooping low to lift her high.", "She gestured toward the well, her brow furrowed. “Sir, you have nothing to draw with, and the well is deep. Where do you get that living water? Are you greater than our father Jacob?” Her words held both challenge and curiosity. I felt my own heart quicken, remembering the night with Nicodemus—Jesus speaking of new birth, of unseen realities breaking into the ordinary. Here it was again, unfolding before us in the bright sunlight, only now offered not to a ruler of Israel by night but to a Samaritan woman at noon.", "Jesus answered her with patient depth, His eyes steady on hers. “Everyone who drinks of this water will be thirsty again, but whoever drinks of the water that I will give him will never be thirsty forever. The water that I will give him will become in him a spring of water welling up to eternal life.” It was the same gift He had named to Nicodemus—life from within, springing up from God’s own giving—and it was being held out freely to a woman no rabbi I knew would have stopped for.", "The woman’s posture shifted. She leaned forward slightly, her voice softening. “Sir, give me this water, so that I will not be thirsty or have to come here to draw.” There was longing beneath her words, a hunger I recognized in my own soul. Then Jesus touched the hidden place in her life: “Go, call your husband, and come here.”", "She stiffened. “I have no husband.” Jesus nodded, His voice full of knowing yet free of condemnation. “You are right in saying, ‘I have no husband’; for you have had five husbands, and the one you now have is not your husband. What you have said is true.” He had done with her exactly what He did with everyone—seen straight to the wound she kept covered. But He named her sin not to shame her into leaving; He named it because there is no living water for a thirst we will not admit, and He meant to give her the water.", "I watched her face as the truth settled over her. Shock, then a dawning recognition—her eyes widened, color rising in her cheeks. She saw Him truly in that moment. “Sir, I perceive that you are a prophet.” Her voice trembled as she spoke of worship on this mountain or in Jerusalem. Jesus replied with words that seemed to open the sky itself: “Woman, believe me, the hour is coming when neither on this mountain nor in Jerusalem will you worship the Father… God is spirit, and those who worship Him must worship in spirit and truth.” The walls of place and people that men had built around God were coming down; He was opening a way for any heart, anywhere, to come home.", "The woman’s response came like a confession and a hope all at once: “I know that Messiah is coming. When He comes, He will tell us all things.” Jesus looked at her with such tenderness and authority that it stirred my own spirit to its depths. “I who speak to you am He.”", "My breath caught. A holy awe washed over me, raising the hairs on my arms despite the warmth of the day. This Samaritan woman—outsider, sinner by her own admission—was the first I had ever heard Him tell plainly who He was. Not a ruler of Israel, not a priest in the Temple, but her, at a well, in the heat of the day. I saw then that the plan of salvation was tilted toward exactly the ones the world had written off. She left her jar behind, forgotten, and hurried back toward the village, her steps light and urgent, calling out to the people there.", "When she returned leading a crowd, their faces a mixture of skepticism and curiosity, the sun had shifted lower in the sky, casting long golden shadows across the fields. They begged Jesus to stay with them. For two days we remained in Sychar, welcomed into homes where the air smelled of fresh bread and olive oil. Jesus taught in the village square, His voice carrying over the gathered people like a shepherd calling his flock. Many believed because of the woman’s testimony, and even more after hearing Him themselves. “It is no longer because of what you said that we believe,” they told her, “for we have heard for ourselves, and we know that this is indeed the Savior of the world.” The Savior of the world—not of Israel only. I turned the title over in my heart: the Lamb who takes away the sin of the world, the Son given for the world, and now the Savior of the world, confessed first by Samaritans. The circle He had come to gather was wider than I had ever dared to imagine.", "I sat among them, listening, feeling the weight of it all settle deeper into my bones. The journey from Jerusalem, the heat of Samaria, the unexpected grace at this well—it all wove together into a tapestry I could scarcely comprehend. Being with Jesus was to watch barriers crumble: Jew and Samaritan, prophet and outcast, the thirsty soul and the Living Water. Each day in His presence stripped away another of my old certainties about who deserved God and who did not, and replaced it with wonder. He had not come to confirm the worthy in their worth; He had come seeking the thirsty, and there was no thirst so disreputable that He would pass it by.", "After those two full days in Sychar, where the words of Jesus had taken root in hearts long hardened by division and shame, we departed the village amid a small crowd that followed us to the edge of the fields. The morning air was cool and sweet with the scent of dew on barley stalks, the sun just cresting the hills and painting everything in soft gold. The Samaritan woman walked among the others for a short distance, her face transformed—no longer guarded, but radiant with a quiet joy that made her steps lighter. I felt a deep stirring in my own chest as we left, a sense of expansion, as though my soul had been enlarged by watching grace cross every barrier of race and reputation. Jesus had given living water not only to her, but somehow to all of us who watched—and I began to understand that this was the very thing He had come to do.", "The road north into Galilee stretched before us, climbing gradually through Samaria’s valleys before descending again. The journey took us through rugged terrain where ancient olive trees twisted like prophets in prayer, their leaves shimmering silver in the breeze. Dust rose around our ankles with each step, and the midday heat pressed down heavily, yet walking beside Jesus made the miles bearable. His presence was like a steady anchor—unhurried, strong, radiating a peace that seemed to quiet even the weariness in my legs and the questions in my mind. I watched Him as He walked, the simple fold of His robe against His shoulder, the calm set of His jaw. As we went He preached a single blazing word wherever the people gathered—“The time is fulfilled, and the kingdom of God is at hand; repent and believe in the gospel”—and I began to hear that call not as a demand to earn the kingdom but as an open hand inviting empty ones to receive it. Being near Him filled me with both reverence and comfort, as if I were a child following a father through unknown country. My heart felt full, yet hungry for more.", "We crossed back into Galilee, and the people there received Him gladly. Word had spread from the Passover in Jerusalem—of the signs He had done, the Temple cleansed, the sick healed. Villages we passed through buzzed with quiet excitement; men paused their plowing, women at the wells turned to watch us. The air carried the familiar smells of home—freshly turned soil, woodsmoke from cooking fires, the faint salt of the distant lake. I felt a swell of awe at how the news of Him had traveled ahead like heralds, and a deeper wonder still that the One they were running to greet had come to do for them what they could not have dreamed to ask.", "We came at length to Nazareth, the town where Jesus had grown from boy to man. The streets were narrow and familiar to Him, the smell of sawdust and warm bread drifting from the houses, the hills rising brown and quiet around the little valley. Word had gone before us of all He had done in Capernaum, and the people watched Him with a mixture of pride and wariness, the way one watches a son who has gone away and come home changed.", "On the Sabbath we went into the synagogue, as was His custom. The room was close and warm, the murmur of greetings fading as He stood to read. The attendant handed Him the scroll of the prophet Isaiah, and I watched His hands unroll it with care, searching out the place. The whole assembly grew still. His voice filled the small room, steady and clear: “The Spirit of the Lord is upon me, because He has anointed me to proclaim good news to the poor. He has sent me to proclaim liberty to the captives and recovering of sight to the blind, to set at liberty those who are oppressed, to proclaim the year of the Lord’s favor.” Here, in His own chosen words, was the shape of His mission: not to the strong but to the poor, the captive, the blind, the oppressed—to people who had nothing to bring but their need.", "He rolled up the scroll, gave it back to the attendant, and sat down. Every eye was fixed on Him. Into that waiting silence He said, “Today this Scripture has been fulfilled in your hearing.” I felt the words settle over the room like the hush before dawn, every face turned toward Him, waiting. The long-promised year of favor had not come on some distant horizon; it had walked into their synagogue and sat down.", "At first they marveled at the gracious words that came from His mouth. But the wonder soured even as I watched it. “Is not this Joseph’s son?” someone said, low, and others took it up, their faces beginning to harden. They had known Him as the carpenter’s boy; they could not bear that He should speak as one sent from God. Jesus answered their unspoken demand without flinching: “Doubtless you will quote to me this proverb, ‘Physician, heal yourself.’ What we have heard you did at Capernaum, do here also in your hometown.” Then He spoke of Elijah, sent in the famine not to the widows of Israel but to a widow of Sidon, and of Elisha, who cleansed no leper in Israel but Naaman the Syrian. He was telling them the very thing I had been slowly learning at the well: that God’s saving mercy spills past the borders of the deserving and reaches the outsider who will receive it over the insider who will not.", "The room turned like a tide. I felt the heat rise before I fully grasped the words—shoulders squaring, voices climbing, hands reaching. They rose up together and drove Him out of the town, pressing and shoving Him toward the brow of the hill on which Nazareth was built, meaning to throw Him down headlong. My heart hammered against my ribs; I could not reach Him through the crush of bodies. And then—I cannot fully account for what I saw—He passed through the midst of them and went on His way. The hands that had grasped at Him closed on nothing. Their fury broke against Him like water against a stone and ran off into the dust.", "I followed Him down the road, my breath ragged, my thoughts reeling. The very words that had stirred my own heart had kindled something near to murder in theirs. I began to understand that the light He carried did not only warm; it divided. The grace that was good news to the poor was an offense to those who were sure they had no need of it. To welcome Him was life. To insist on one’s own sufficiency was to be left standing on the edge of a hill with empty hands, not knowing how He had slipped away.", "We came again to Cana, the very place where water had first become wine. The village looked the same—stone houses clustered together, children playing in the narrow lanes, the scent of baking bread drifting from open doors. But everything felt different now, charged with memory. A certain nobleman from Capernaum, an official in Herod’s service, sought Jesus out. His face was drawn with desperation, eyes hollow from sleepless nights. He approached with urgent steps, his fine cloak dusty from the hard ride, and begged Him, “Sir, come down to Capernaum, for my son is at the point of death.”", "I stood close enough to see the tremor in the man’s hands as he spoke. His voice cracked with fatherly fear—the kind that strips away all pride. Jesus looked at him, and at the small crowd gathering, and said with measured authority, “Unless you see signs and wonders you will not believe.” The words hung heavy in the warm air. The nobleman’s face tightened, but he pressed on, almost pleading, “Sir, come down before my child dies.”", "Then Jesus spoke words that pierced the moment like sunlight breaking through clouds: “Go; your son will live.” The nobleman’s eyes met Jesus’, and in that instant I saw belief take hold—raw, desperate trust. He did not argue or demand proof. He simply turned and began the journey back to Capernaum, his steps hurried yet steadier, as if carrying a fragile hope. He had nothing to lean on but the bare word of Jesus, and on that word he set out for home.", "The next day, as we remained in Cana, servants from the nobleman’s household found us. Their faces shone with astonishment. They told us the boy had begun to recover at the exact hour Jesus had spoken the word—the fever breaking suddenly, strength returning to his small limbs as if life itself had been commanded to return. The father had asked them the hour, and when they told him, he knew. He and his entire household believed.", "I stood there in the village square, the sun warm on my face, my heart pounding with the same awe I had felt at the wedding. Another sign. Not with jars and wine this time, but across distance, through a father’s faith. I felt tears prick at my eyes unbidden, a trembling deep in my spirit. This Man spoke, and death itself yielded. And I saw something in that father I knew I would need: he had been saved his son not by anything he could see or do, but by taking Jesus at His word and walking home on it. That, I began to understand, was the whole posture of faith—to receive the promise and stake everything on the One who made it. The road behind us and the road ahead seemed bathed in the same light—His light—and I followed, more certain with every step that I had left everything for the only thing worth having.", "After the nobleman and his servants departed with joy lighting their faces, we did not linger long in Cana. Jesus led us down toward the Sea of Galilee once more, the road descending gently through terraced hills where vines heavy with young grapes clung to the slopes. The afternoon sun warmed our backs, and the air carried the sweet scent of ripening fruit mixed with the faint, salty promise of the lake ahead. My feet knew this path by now, yet each journey with Him felt fresh, as though the land itself responded to His presence. Dust rose softly with our steps, and I walked close behind Him, watching the steady rhythm of His shoulders. A deep contentment settled in me, mingled with expectancy. Every mile seemed to whisper that greater things were unfolding.", "We reached the shore near Capernaum as the sun dipped lower, painting the water in hues of gold and deepening blue. Fishing boats bobbed gently at the water’s edge, their wooden hulls creaking softly against the pebbles. The smell of the lake—fresh water, fish, and wet nets—filled my nostrils, familiar and comforting. Several of us had been fishermen before following Him; the sight stirred old memories in my chest. Jesus stopped near the water, the gentle waves lapping at His feet, and the crowds soon found Him. They pressed in eagerly, hungry for His teaching, their voices a low murmur of hope and need.", "Simon Peter was there with his brother Andrew, along with James and his brother John, the sons of Zebedee. They had returned to their boats after a long, fruitless night on the water. I saw the weariness in their faces as they washed their nets—broad shoulders slumped, hands raw from the ropes. Jesus stepped into Simon’s boat and asked him to push out a little from the land. From there, seated in the boat with the water rocking gently beneath Him, He taught the crowds on the shore. His voice carried clearly over the water, rich and full of authority, speaking of the kingdom as a treasure hidden in a field, as a net cast into the sea. I stood on the shore among the people, my heart swelling within me. The words sank deep, nourishing places I had not known were empty. Being near Him while He taught was like standing under a steady rain after drought—reviving, cleansing.", "When He finished, He turned to Simon. “Put out into the deep and let down your nets for a catch.” Simon hesitated, his voice rough with exhaustion. “Master, we toiled all night and took nothing! But at your word I will let down the nets.” I watched as they rowed out, the oars dipping rhythmically into the sparkling water. The sun was lower now, casting long reflections across the lake. Then it happened. The nets went down, and suddenly the water churned. Fish—hundreds upon hundreds—filled the nets so quickly and so abundantly that the cords strained and the boats began to sink under the weight. Shouts rose from the boats—astonishment, laughter mixed with alarm. Simon and the others waved frantically for help from the other boat. James and John rowed over swiftly, their faces alive with wonder as they hauled in the overflowing catch together. The boats rode low in the water, silver fish flashing and slapping against the wood, their scales catching the dying light like living jewels.", "I waded out to help, the cool lake water rising around my knees, my hands plunging into the heaving nets. The fish were heavy, thrashing with life, their wet bodies cool and slippery. My arms ached from the effort, yet my spirit soared. Simon fell to his knees right there in the boat, right in the midst of the miracle, water sloshing around him. His voice broke as he looked at Jesus: “Depart from me, for I am a sinful man, O Lord!” The raw confession pierced me. I felt it too—a holy fear rising in my chest, my own unworthiness laid bare before such power and kindness. And here I saw something I would see again and again until I finally understood it: the sinner’s instinct is to flee from holiness, but Jesus’ answer is to draw the sinner near. He looked at Simon with eyes full of compassion and said, “Do not be afraid; from now on you will be catching men.” He did not wait for Simon to make himself worthy; He laid hold of an unworthy man and gave him a calling.", "That moment on the water changed everything. We brought the boats to land, the abundant catch still shimmering in the hulls, and they left it all—the nets, the boats, the fish, their old lives. Simon, Andrew, James, and John followed Him that day with a wholehearted surrender I felt echoing in my own soul. Being with Jesus meant watching the ordinary burst open into the extraordinary: empty nets filled, weary men transformed into fishers of men. I felt my old world slipping further away, like sand through open fingers, replaced by something vast and eternal. The lakeshore that evening, with its gentle waves and the scent of fish and salt, became holy ground beneath our feet. I walked behind Him as the stars began to appear overhead, my spirit quiet yet alive, understanding a little more that grace does not find us fit—it lays hold of us as we are and makes us follow.", "We made our way into Capernaum that same evening, the miraculous catch of fish still fresh on our hands and in our minds. The town nestled close to the shore, its dark basalt stone houses glowing softly in the fading light, the air thick with the smells of cooking fires, baked bread, and the ever-present tang of the lake. My clothes were damp from the water, my arms weary from hauling nets, yet a fire burned steadily in my chest—an excitement laced with holy fear that refused to let me rest.", "On the Sabbath, we went together into the synagogue. The building was modest but filled with reverence: stone walls cool to the touch, the scent of worn scrolls and olive oil lamps heavy in the air. Men sat on the floor or low benches, their prayer shawls draped over their shoulders, murmuring familiar blessings. Jesus stood to teach, and from the first word, silence fell like a blanket. His voice was clear and full of authority, not like the scribes who quoted endlessly without power. He spoke of the kingdom as if He had walked its streets Himself. I sat near the back, my heart pounding in rhythm with His words, feeling them sink into me like good seed into fertile soil. This was no ordinary rabbi. The air itself seemed to tremble with the weight of what He said.", "Suddenly, a man cried out from the midst of the congregation—a tortured, unnatural shriek that tore through the holy quiet. He convulsed, eyes wild, foam at the corners of his mouth. “What have you to do with us, Jesus of Nazareth?” the voice snarled through him, harsh and demonic. “Have you come to destroy us? I know who you are—the Holy One of God!” The sound of it sent a chill racing down my spine; I felt the hairs on my arms rise. The man’s body jerked violently as the unclean spirit fought within him.", "Jesus turned toward him without fear, His face calm yet commanding. “Be silent,” He said firmly, “and come out of him!” The man convulsed once more, a loud cry echoing off the stone walls, and then the spirit left him. The man collapsed, then rose, blinking in confusion but whole—his eyes clear, his body at peace. Whispers rippled through the synagogue, growing into astonished murmurs. “What is this?” people asked one another. “A new teaching with authority! He commands even the unclean spirits, and they obey Him!” I sat motionless, my breath shallow, a deep awe pressing on my chest like a physical weight. I had imagined our bondage as a matter of bad habits and broken laws; now I saw there was a darker captivity beneath it, real powers that held men in chains—and that this Man had walked into the world to break them. To save sinners was not only to forgive them but to wrench them out of the grip of the enemy who claimed them. The demons knew who He was when men did not, and they trembled, for His coming was their undoing.", "We left the synagogue and went straight to the house of Simon and Andrew. The modest home smelled of fresh fish and herbs, the midday sun streaming through the open doorway. Simon’s mother-in-law lay in the inner room, burning with a severe fever. Her face was flushed and drawn with pain, her breathing labored. Jesus went to her bedside without hesitation. He took her hand gently in His own—those strong, carpenter’s hands—and lifted her up. At once the fever left her. Color returned to her cheeks in a healthy glow. She stood, fully restored, and immediately began serving us, moving about the house with renewed strength, preparing food as if she had never been ill. I watched in silent wonder, my throat tight with emotion. One touch from Him, and sickness fled like shadows before the dawn—and the very strength He gave her, she poured straight back out in service to Him. It struck me as the whole movement of salvation in small: He lays hold of us where we lie helpless, raises us by His own power, and the raising itself frees us to serve the One who gave us back our life.", "As evening came and the sun dipped below the hills, painting the lake in fiery oranges and purples, the whole city gathered at the door. The news had spread like wind over the water. People brought the sick and those oppressed by demons—men, women, and children crowding the narrow street, their faces etched with desperation and hope. The air filled with the sounds of weeping, pleading voices, and the shuffling of many feet. Torches flickered, casting dancing shadows on the walls. Jesus went out among them, laying His hands on each one. I moved through the crowd helping calm the people and keep order. One by one, after a touch from Jesus, they were healed. Demons cried out as they fled, their shrieks cutting through the night before falling silent. The healed ones laughed and wept for joy, embracing their families. I felt overwhelmed by the sheer abundance of mercy pouring out through Him. He turned no one away for being too broken or too unclean; He rationed nothing. Being with Jesus in that moment was like watching the very heart of God beating for His people—compassion flowing freely, powerfully, without limit, the way I was beginning to believe He would one day pour out salvation itself.", "That night, long after the crowds had gone and the town quieted under a canopy of stars, I lay on the roof of the house, the cool night breeze brushing my face. The events of the day replayed in my mind: the synagogue, the demon, the fever leaving a woman’s body, the sea of faces made whole. A profound sense of privilege filled me, mixed with the growing awareness of how much I still did not understand. Yet I knew this with certainty—I would follow this Man anywhere. In His presence, the broken world seemed to be mending, one touch, one word at a time, as though the long ruin were being quietly rolled back.", "The night was deep and still when I finally drifted into sleep on the rooftop, the stars wheeling slowly overhead like silent witnesses. The cool breeze from the lake carried the faint rhythm of lapping waves, and my body ached from the long day, yet my spirit remained strangely alert, as if it refused to fully rest in the afterglow of all we had seen.", "Before the sky had fully lightened, while the town still slept and the first faint gray of dawn touched the eastern hills, I stirred at the sound of movement below. Jesus had risen. I watched from the edge of the roof as He slipped quietly out of the house, His figure a shadow against the dim light, heading alone toward the desolate places outside Capernaum. The hills were rocky and barren there, dotted with scrub and wild olive trees. I did not follow immediately, sensing this was His sacred time with the Father. But later, when Simon and the others woke and found Him gone, we went searching. The morning air was crisp and cool against my skin, dew still clinging to the sparse grass underfoot. My sandals grew damp as we climbed the rocky path, hearts pounding not just from the effort but from a growing hunger to be near Him again.", "We found Him in a quiet hollow among the hills, kneeling in prayer. The first rays of sun were breaking over the ridge, bathing Him in soft golden light. He rose when He saw us, dust on the knees of His robe, His face peaceful yet purposeful. Simon, breathless, spoke for all of us: “Everyone is looking for you!” The urgency in his voice was plain—the crowds from last night were already stirring back in the town, hungry for more. But Jesus looked out over the lake shimmering in the distance and said calmly, “Let us go on to the next towns, that I may preach there also. For that is why I came out.” His words carried a quiet resolve that settled something deep inside me. He would not be held even by a town full of grateful people, because the good news had to run to as many as would receive it, and He meant to carry it to them all.", "We traveled from village to village throughout Galilee in the days that followed. The roads wound through fertile valleys and over gentle hills, the air sweet with the scent of blooming wildflowers and ripening grain. Dust rose around our feet in soft clouds, and the sun warmed our shoulders as we walked. Jesus preached in their synagogues—simple gatherings of farmers, fishermen, and craftsmen who listened with rapt attention. His teaching was like nothing I had ever heard: full of life and fire, yet tender as a shepherd with a lamb. He spoke of the kingdom of God as if it were a seed planted now, growing secretly yet powerfully among us. I walked behind Him, feeling the weight of each word settle into my bones. My old ways of thinking—narrow, bound by tradition and fear—felt as though they were loosening, cracking open like dry earth after rain.", "One afternoon we came to a leper who approached us on the outskirts of a village. The man was a pitiful sight: his skin ravaged by the disease, white patches and open sores visible where his ragged clothes did not cover him. He kept his distance as the law required, yet fell to his knees in the dust, his voice breaking with desperate hope. “Lord, if you will, you can make me clean.” The air around us seemed to still. I felt a surge of pity and awe—pity for the man’s suffering, awe at his raw faith. Jesus did not draw back. Instead, He reached out with compassion shining in His eyes and touched him—actually touched the unclean flesh. “I will,” He said gently, “be clean.” At once the leprosy left the man. His skin became whole and smooth right before our eyes, like a dead branch suddenly bursting with green life. The man wept openly, trembling as he touched his restored face and arms. Jesus instructed him quietly to show himself to the priest and offer the gift Moses commanded, but the news spread quickly anyway. My heart hammered in my chest as I witnessed it. Under the law the unclean defiled the clean, and no one reached across that line; yet Jesus crossed it without flinching, and instead of catching the man’s defilement, He gave the man His own cleanness. I began to see how He would save us—not by keeping a safe distance from our uncleanness, but by laying His hand on it and taking it into Himself, so that what touched Him came away clean.", "The crowds grew wherever we went, pressing in with their needs, yet Jesus often withdrew again to lonely places to pray. I began to understand that His power flowed from that hidden communion with the Father. Following Him was teaching me to listen, to watch, to wait. Each day brought new wonders, new teachings that sank deeper into my soul, and a growing certainty that I was walking in the company of the One Israel had waited for through long centuries of darkness. My spirit felt both hushed and exhilarated, like a sail filled with a strong, steady wind carrying me toward an unseen shore.", "After the healing of the leper, word of Jesus spread like wildfire through the hills and villages of Galilee. We returned to Capernaum, the familiar path now feeling almost like home despite how much had changed within me. The lake glittered under the midday sun as we approached, its surface rippling gently in the breeze, carrying the ever-present smells of fish and salt water. My robe was dusty from the road, my feet sore, but a quiet strength seemed to flow from simply walking near Him. Jesus moved with purpose yet without haste, His presence like a deep river—calm on the surface, yet carrying immense power beneath.", "We entered the town, and the crowds quickly found us again. They pressed around the house where we stayed, filling the narrow street until there was no room even at the door. The air grew thick with the scent of many bodies, dust, and expectation. Jesus was teaching inside, His voice steady and full of life, when four men arrived carrying their paralyzed friend on a mat. The poor man lay motionless, his limbs thin and useless, his eyes filled with a quiet desperation that pierced my heart. His friends, faces streaked with sweat and determination, could not push through the crowd. So they climbed the outer stairs to the roof, their sandals scraping against the clay tiles. I stepped outside and looked up, shielding my eyes from the sun, as they began tearing away the thatch and beams with urgent hands. Dirt and pieces of roofing fell into the room below in small showers. The people inside murmured in surprise, but Jesus simply looked up with calm authority.", "The mat was lowered carefully through the opening, ropes creaking, until the paralyzed man lay directly before Jesus on the floor. Dust still drifted down around him like a veil. I watched from the doorway, my chest tight with anticipation and pity. Jesus looked at the man, then at his friends whose faith had torn through every obstacle, and said words that silenced the room: “Son, your sins are forgiven.” The man had been carried there for his legs, and Jesus spoke first to his sins, as though the deeper paralysis—the one beneath the visible one—was the guilt that bound him to death. A ripple of shock went through the scribes sitting there—teachers of the law with stern faces and crossed arms. I could see the offense in their eyes as they thought to themselves, “Why does this man speak like this? He is blaspheming! Who can forgive sins but God alone?”", "Jesus knew their thoughts. He turned to them, His gaze steady and piercing, and asked, “Why do you question these things in your hearts? Which is easier, to say to the paralytic, ‘Your sins are forgiven,’ or to say, ‘Rise, take up your bed and walk’? But that you may know that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins—” He looked back at the man on the mat, His voice ringing with power—“I say to you, rise, pick up your bed, and go home.”", "The moment that followed is burned into my memory. The man’s eyes widened. Strength flowed visibly into his wasted limbs. He stirred, then sat up. A gasp rose from everyone watching. Slowly, deliberately, he swung his legs over the edge of the mat, planted his feet on the floor, and stood. His face broke into a smile of pure astonishment and joy. He rolled up his mat, tucked it under his arm, and walked out through the crowd, praising God with a loud voice. The people parted before him, stunned into silence at first, then erupting in awe. “We never saw anything like this!” they cried. “Glory to God!”", "My knees felt weak as I watched him go. Tears stung my eyes unbidden. I had seen water turned to wine, demons cast out, lepers cleansed—but this was different. Forgiveness and healing had flowed together from the same source, and at last I understood what I had only half-seen before. He had not come merely to mend bodies; the healings were signs, pointing to the greater thing—that the Son of Man had authority on earth to forgive sins. This was the heart of why He had come. My own sins, hidden though they were, seemed exposed in the light of His authority, yet instead of shame I felt hope—deep, cleansing hope. In that dusty room, with sunlight slanting through the broken roof, I saw that my deepest need was not for my circumstances to be mended but for my guilt to be lifted, and that here, in flesh and blood, stood the One with authority to lift it.", "We continued ministering in Capernaum and the surrounding region in the days that followed. The crowds never seemed to thin. Jesus moved among them with tireless compassion, yet He still slipped away at times to pray in the lonely places before dawn. I began to treasure those quiet mornings when I could watch Him return, His face refreshed as if He had drunk from a hidden spring. Each day with Him peeled away another layer of my old self, leaving me more awake, more alive, more hungry for the kingdom He carried within Him like a priceless treasure.", "After the paralytic rose and walked out carrying his mat, the atmosphere in Capernaum remained charged for days. People talked of nothing else. Jesus continued teaching in homes and open places, and I stayed near Him, listening closely to every word. One afternoon, as we passed the tax booth by the lakeshore, Jesus stopped. The collector there was Levi, son of Alphaeus—a man most of us avoided because of his work for the Romans. He sat surrounded by coins and records, his face set in the careful mask of someone used to suspicion.", "Jesus looked directly at him and said simply, “Follow me.” Levi stood at once. He left the booth, the money, the ledgers—everything—just as he was. I saw the surprise in his eyes turn quickly to resolve. That same evening, Levi hosted a meal in his house. Many other tax collectors and people known for loose living came. They filled the rooms, eating and talking together around the low tables. I sat among them, watching Jesus recline at the table as if He belonged there, speaking with them naturally and without any sign of discomfort.", "The Pharisees noticed. Some of their scribes stood outside, murmuring loudly enough for us to hear: “Why does He eat with tax collectors and sinners?” Their tone was sharp with disapproval. Jesus heard them and answered clearly, “Those who are well have no need of a physician, but those who are sick. I came not to call the righteous, but sinners.” His words hung in the air, steady and kind, and they gathered up everything I had been slowly learning into a single sentence. The well had no need of Him; the sick did. He had not come for the people who imagined themselves righteous, but for the ones who knew they were sick unto death. I had once thought of myself as better than men like Levi—and in that very thought I had nearly disqualified myself from the mercy I needed most. The kingdom reached exactly the places we counted unworthy, because those were the only places that knew they were ill. It humbled me deeply and made me grateful, for the first time, to know my own sickness.", "Not long after, some people came asking why Jesus’ disciples did not fast as the disciples of John and the Pharisees did. Jesus replied, “Can the wedding guests fast while the bridegroom is with them? As long as they have the bridegroom with them, they cannot fast. The days will come when the bridegroom is taken away from them, and then they will fast in that day.” He continued with images of new cloth and old garments, new wine and old wineskins—warning that His way could not be forced into the old patterns without tearing everything apart. I listened carefully, sensing that what He brought could not simply be stitched onto the old religion of earning and deserving; it was a new thing, and it would burst the old wineskins wide open. The Bridegroom had come to His feast, and the only fitting answer to His presence was joy. His words made even the coming sorrow He hinted at—the day the bridegroom would be taken away—feel like part of some larger mercy I could not yet trace.", "When a feast of the Jews drew near, we went up again to Jerusalem. The city was crowded as before, the streets thick with pilgrims, the smoke of the altar rising into a pale sky. But Jesus led us first to a place I had not seen—a pool near the Sheep Gate, called in our tongue Bethesda, ringed by five covered colonnades. Beneath those roofs lay a multitude of the sick: the blind, the lame, the withered, stretched out on mats in the shade, waiting. The air hung heavy with the smell of sickness and the low sound of suffering, and something in my chest tightened to see so much hopelessness gathered into one place. I would have hurried past it; He walked straight in, as if such a place were exactly what He had come for.", "One man among them had been an invalid for thirty-eight years—longer than I had been alive. He lay where perhaps he had lain for much of that time, his body wasted, his eyes gone dull with the long habit of disappointment. Jesus stopped before him. “Do you want to be healed?” He asked. The man did not answer Yes; he answered out of his weariness: “Sir, I have no one to put me into the pool when the water is stirred, and while I am going another steps down before me.” He had no one, and no strength to save himself—and that, I was beginning to see, was precisely the condition Jesus came to meet. He did not wait for the man to drag himself to the water. Jesus looked at him with that steady gaze I had come to know, and said, “Rise, take up your bed and walk.” At once—at once—the strength I had watched pour into others poured into him. He rose, rolled up the mat that had been his prison, and walked. I stood gaping as he passed me, carrying the bed that for so long had carried him.", "But it was the Sabbath. The leaders saw the man with his mat and rebuked him rather than rejoice with him: “It is not lawful for you to take up your bed.” When they learned who had healed him, their anger turned toward Jesus. The wariness I had first felt among the Pharisees by the Jordan hardened now into open hostility, plain on their faces in the bright Temple light. It unsettled me that the same act could draw a helpless man to his feet and harden the hearts of the watching—as if the mercy itself were sorting us.", "Jesus did not retreat from it. He answered them with words that lifted the veil higher than I was ready for: “My Father is working until now, and I am working.” I heard the breath go out of the men around me. To call God His own Father, making Himself equal with God—this they could not abide. Yet He pressed on, and I drank in every word though I could scarcely hold it: that the Son does only what He sees the Father doing; that as the Father raises the dead and gives them life, so also the Son gives life to whom He will; that an hour was coming, and now was, when the dead would hear the voice of the Son of God and those who heard would live. He had just raised a man from thirty-eight years of living death, and now He claimed that the same voice would one day call the truly dead out of their graves. The healing at the pool was only a small picture of the life He had come to give.", "When they demanded proof, He spoke of witnesses. John the Baptist had borne witness to the truth, He said—a burning and shining lamp in whose light they had been willing, for a while, to rejoice. The very works He was doing bore witness that the Father had sent Him. The Father Himself bore witness. And the Scriptures they searched so carefully, supposing that in them they had life, were themselves pointing to Him—yet they would not come to Him to have that life. “Moses wrote of me,” He said. “But if you do not believe his writings, how will you believe my words?” It struck me that a man could know every letter of the law and still miss the Life the law was pointing to; the Scriptures were a road, and He was the home at the end of it.", "I walked out from under the colonnades into the open courts with my thoughts turning over and over. I had watched a man stand upright after thirty-eight years on his back, and I had heard the One who raised him claim the right to raise the dead and to judge the living. The leaders had heard the same things and reached only for stones. I understood, dimly, that I was being asked to settle what they would not: whether the voice that had called a cripple to his feet was the same voice that would one day call me, and all the dead, up out of the dust. And if it was, then life itself was a gift held out in His hand, to be received and not earned.", "One Sabbath we were walking through the fields. The disciples picked heads of grain as we went, rubbing them in our hands to eat. Some Pharisees challenged Him immediately: “Look, why are they doing what is not lawful on the Sabbath?” Jesus answered them by reminding them of David eating the bread of the Presence and of the Sabbath being made for man, not man for the Sabbath. Then He declared, “The Son of Man is lord even of the Sabbath.” I turned it over as we walked: the day of rest had been given as a gift to weary people, not laid on them as another burden—and the One walking beside us was Lord of it, the true rest these fields and feasts had only ever pointed toward.", "Another Sabbath, in the synagogue, a man with a withered hand was present. The religious leaders watched Jesus closely to see if He would heal on the Sabbath. Jesus called the man forward and asked the critics plainly, “Is it lawful on the Sabbath to do good or to do harm, to save life or to kill?” They remained silent. He looked around at them with sorrow and anger at their hardness, then said to the man, “Stretch out your hand.” The man did, and it was restored, whole and strong like the other. The leaders went out at once and began plotting against Him. I felt the tension rising, yet Jesus remained calm and purposeful. It sobered me to see that the very mercy that restored a withered hand was driving His enemies toward His death—that even now His saving work and the cross it would cost Him were quietly bound together. It stirred a quiet resolve in me to stay close, no matter what might come.", "After the growing tension in the synagogue, Jesus withdrew with us toward the sea. Many followed from Galilee, Judea, Jerusalem, Idumea, and even from beyond the Jordan and the regions around Tyre and Sidon. The crowds were large, pressing in to touch Him because power went out from Him and healed them all. I stayed near, watching as people with various afflictions came forward—some carried on mats, others limping or led by the hand. Jesus received each one with steady attention. I saw relief wash over their faces as diseases left them and unclean spirits departed with cries. His calm authority never wavered. The mercy seemed inexhaustible, flowing out to whoever would press near and reach for Him, and I felt a deep sense of privilege mixed with responsibility, as though I were witnessing something sacred that demanded my full attention and loyalty.", "One day He went up on the mountain and called certain ones of us to Himself. I climbed with the others, the path steady beneath my feet. When we reached a level place, He stopped and looked at each of us in turn. His gaze was knowing and purposeful. From the larger group of disciples, He chose twelve whom He named apostles, that they might be with Him and that He might send them out to preach. Simon, whom He called Peter, and Andrew his brother. James and John, the sons of Zebedee, whom He named Boanerges, that is, sons of thunder. Then Philip, Bartholomew, Matthew (who had been Levi the tax collector), Thomas, James the son of Alphaeus, Thaddaeus, Simon the Zealot, and Judas Iscariot, who would later betray Him.", "I was among those called close in this way. When Jesus looked at me and included me in that number, I felt a quiet weight settle on my shoulders—not burdensome, but real. He had not chosen us for what we brought; He chose us first to be with Him, and only then to be sent, as if the being-with had to come before any work could flow from it. It was as if He was entrusting me with something far beyond my own ability. I felt both small and strengthened in His presence, aware of my shortcomings yet drawn forward by His grace. And the name He spoke last—Judas, who would betray Him—lodged in me like a splinter, a sober reminder that being near Him was not the same as being saved by Him; even the closeness was a gift to be received, not a possession to be presumed upon.", "He began to teach the larger crowd that had gathered on the level ground. His words came with gentle authority: “Blessed are you who are poor, for yours is the kingdom of God. Blessed are you who are hungry now, for you shall be satisfied. Blessed are you who weep now, for you shall laugh.” He spoke of love for enemies, of not judging others, of good fruit coming from a good heart. “Why do you call me ‘Lord, Lord,’ and not do what I tell you?” He asked at one point. The teaching was direct, practical, and searching. I felt it examining my own life, exposing where my thoughts and actions fell short. Yet there was mercy in the challenge. He pronounced His blessing not on the strong and full but on the poor, the hungry, the weeping—on people who had nothing in their hands and knew it. The righteousness He described was so far beyond me that it drove me, not to despair, but to lean harder on Him; I saw that the same lips that set the impossible standard were the lips that had been forgiving sinners all along. Sitting there among the apostles and the people, I found myself wanting to follow more closely, even as I recognized how much I still needed Him to change.", "After He finished teaching, we descended with Him. A centurion’s servant was ill and near death. The centurion, a man of authority, sent elders to ask Jesus to come and heal his servant. When Jesus drew near the house, the centurion sent friends with a message: “Lord, do not trouble yourself, for I am not worthy to have you come under my roof. Therefore I did not presume to come to you. But say the word, and let my servant be healed.” I stood nearby and saw the respect and faith in the man’s approach, even through messengers. Jesus marveled at him and said to the crowd, “I tell you, not even in Israel have I found such faith.” When the messengers returned, they found the servant well. A Gentile soldier, an outsider to the covenant, had grasped what many in Israel had not—that Jesus needed only to speak and it was done—and his faith in the bare word was counted greater than all the privilege of birth.", "These events deepened my conviction. Each act of mercy, each teaching, each quiet look from Jesus confirmed that I was exactly where I needed to be. Being with Him was reshaping my understanding of God’s kingdom—a kingdom that welcomed the outsider, counted faith above status, and called ordinary men like me by sheer grace into extraordinary purpose. I was beginning to see that the only thing He ever asked of the people He saved was that they trust Him, and that even this trust seemed to be something He drew out of them rather than something they manufactured.", "We continued traveling through the towns and villages of Galilee. One day we approached Nain, a small town not far from Capernaum. As we drew near the gate, a funeral procession came out toward us. The air was heavy with sorrow. A young man had died, the only son of a widow. She walked behind the bier, her face etched with grief so deep it seemed to hollow her out. The mourners followed, their laments low and steady. I felt the weight of her loss settle in my own chest—a mother burying her child, now utterly alone in the world.", "Jesus stopped. His eyes rested on the widow with compassion so clear and strong it seemed to fill the space between them. No one had asked Him; her need alone moved Him. He spoke to her gently, “Do not weep.” Then He stepped forward and touched the bier. The bearers stood still. In a voice calm and full of authority He said, “Young man, I say to you, arise.” The young man sat up at once and began to speak. The procession turned to stunned silence, then to joy and fear. The widow embraced her son, weeping now with relief and wonder. Those around us glorified God, saying, “A great prophet has arisen among us!” and “God has visited His people!” I stood there, heart pounding, unable to look away. The power in Jesus’ word had reached into death itself and pulled life back. Their cry stayed with me—God has visited His people—for I was beginning to grasp that this was the heart of it: God had not stayed far off, content to pity us from heaven, but had come down into our funerals to undo death from the inside. If He could speak life into a corpse, then the salvation He carried went all the way down to the grave, and even there it would not be stopped.", "Later, messengers came from John the Baptist, who was now in prison. They asked Jesus directly, “Are you the one who is to come, or shall we look for another?” John’s doubt, even from prison, touched me. Jesus answered them by pointing to what they could see: the blind receiving sight, the lame walking, lepers cleansed, the deaf hearing, the dead raised, the poor having good news preached to them. Then He spoke of John with honor, calling him more than a prophet, the messenger who prepared the way. I listened carefully, feeling the tension between expectation and reality. Being with Jesus meant learning that the kingdom came not always in the dramatic deliverance we imagined—not yet the breaking of every prison—but in these quiet, unstoppable acts of mercy that were themselves the proof of who He was.", "One evening, a Pharisee named Simon invited Jesus to dinner at his house. We went, and I sat at the edge of the room, observing. A woman from the city, known for her sinful life, entered while we were reclining at the table. She carried an alabaster flask of ointment. She stood behind Jesus at His feet, weeping. Her tears fell on His feet, and she wiped them with her hair, then kissed them and anointed them with the ointment. The fragrance filled the room—rich and sweet. Simon thought to himself that if Jesus were a prophet, He would know what kind of woman this was and reject her. But Jesus turned to him and told a parable about two debtors, one owing much and one little, both forgiven. “Which will love the creditor more?” He asked. Simon answered correctly, the one forgiven more. Jesus then looked at the woman and said to Simon, “Her sins, which are many, are forgiven, for she loved much. But he who is forgiven little, loves little.” To her He said gently, “Your sins are forgiven… Your faith has saved you; go in peace.”", "I watched her leave with a lightness in her step I had rarely seen. Her tears had been of sorrow turning to joy. Jesus’ words exposed Simon’s cold correctness and the woman’s grateful love, and in doing so they turned my whole understanding of God right way around. I had assumed people were forgiven because they first loved God enough; but Jesus showed it ran the other way—she loved much because she had been forgiven much, and Simon loved little because he imagined he had little to be forgiven. Her flood of love was not the price of her pardon but the proof of it. I felt convicted in my own heart. How much had I been forgiven? Did I love accordingly? Sitting in that room, I sensed Jesus seeing straight into each of us, not with condemnation but with truth that healed, inviting a deeper and more honest love.", "These days with Him were steadily changing me. I saw power over death, mercy toward the doubtful, forgiveness for the broken. I felt more awake to my own need and more drawn to His kindness. Following Him meant walking in a light that revealed and restored at the same time—a light that showed me my sin only to wash it.", "We continued moving through Galilee with Jesus, and a group of women began traveling with us as well—women who had been healed of evil spirits and infirmities. Among them was Mary, called Magdalene, from whom seven demons had gone out. She walked with a quiet dignity now, her presence steady and grateful. There was also Joanna, wife of Chuza, Herod’s household manager, and Susanna, and several others. These women provided for us out of their own resources, and I saw in them a devotion that humbled me. They listened to every teaching with focused attention, their faces often shining with relief and love. Being among them reminded me that the kingdom welcomed and restored hearts the world had given up on—that the same grace which had cast seven demons out of Mary now gave her back a dignity no one could take, and turned the rescued into the most devoted of all.", "One day by the lake, a great crowd gathered again. Jesus got into a boat and sat down to teach, while the people stood on the shore. He told them many things in parables. I sat in the boat near Him, the gentle rocking of the water beneath us, and listened as He spoke of a sower going out to sow seed. Some fell along the path and was trampled or eaten by birds. Some fell on rock and sprang up but withered because it had no root. Some fell among thorns and was choked. But other seed fell on good soil and produced a crop a hundredfold. “He who has ears to hear, let him hear,” Jesus said. Later, when we were alone, we asked Him about the meaning. He explained patiently: the seed is the word of God, and the different soils represent different hearts. I felt the parable searching my own soul. The same good news, I realized, fell on every kind of heart, and what made the difference was not the seed but the soil that received it—so that even my believing was something to ask Him for, a softening of hard ground I could not work in myself. Was my heart good soil? I wanted it to be, and I began to pray that He would make it so.", "He told other parables that day—the kingdom like a lamp set on a stand, not hidden under a bed; like a man who sows seed and then sleeps while it grows by itself; like a mustard seed that becomes the largest of garden plants. Each one opened my understanding a little more. I felt my mind stretching, my heart softening. The picture that lingered was the seed growing secretly while the farmer slept: the kingdom was not something I would build by my own striving but something God was quietly growing, from the smallest beginning, toward a harvest only He could bring. Sitting there with the other apostles, I experienced a growing sense of being let in on secrets of the kingdom hidden for ages—a quiet joy, mixed with awareness of how much I had been given.", "That evening, after the crowds had gone, Jesus said to us, “Let us go across to the other side of the lake.” We set out in the boat while He fell asleep on a cushion in the stern. A fierce wind suddenly arose, and waves began breaking into the boat, filling it with water. The boat tossed violently. I gripped the side, my heart racing with fear as the water rose around my ankles. The other disciples shouted, waking Jesus: “Teacher, do you not care that we are perishing?” I felt the same panic—the wind howling, the waves crashing, the boat tilting dangerously. We had seen so many miracles, yet in that moment raw fear took hold, and I understood how quickly I still reached for terror instead of trust.", "Jesus woke and rebuked the wind and the waves: “Peace! Be still!” At once the wind ceased, and there was a great calm. The water became smooth as glass. He looked at us and said, “Why are you so afraid? Have you still no faith?” I sat there, soaked and trembling, filled with both relief and a deeper awe. Who is this, that even the wind and sea obey Him? My fear turned to reverent wonder. The same word that forgave sins and raised the dead now governed the chaos of wind and water, and I saw that there was no realm—not sickness, not death, not the raging deep, not the storms within me—beyond the reach of His command. In the sudden stillness, with only the gentle lapping of water against the boat, I felt small yet safe, learning that to be saved was, in part, simply this: to be in the boat with Him and to trust that He cared.", "We reached the other side, in the country of the Gerasenes. As soon as Jesus stepped out of the boat, a man with an unclean spirit met us. He lived among the tombs, wild and uncontrollable. Chains had not been able to bind him. He ran to Jesus and fell down before Him, crying out, “What have you to do with me, Jesus, Son of the Most High God? I adjure you by God, do not torment me.” The man’s eyes were tormented, his body marked by self-inflicted wounds. Jesus asked the spirit its name, and it answered, “Legion, for we are many.” After a brief exchange, Jesus commanded the spirits to come out. They entered a herd of pigs feeding nearby, and the herd rushed down the steep bank into the lake and drowned. He had crossed a whole stormy sea, it seemed, for this one ruined man whom no chain could hold—and what no chain could do, His word did in a moment.", "The man was left sitting there, clothed and in his right mind. When the people of the region came and saw what had happened, they were afraid and begged Jesus to leave their region. The healed man wanted to follow us, but Jesus told him gently, “Go home to your friends and tell them how much the Lord has done for you, and how He has had mercy on you.” The man went away and began proclaiming what Jesus had done for him. I watched him go, amazed at the transformation. Here was the whole pattern again: a man in hopeless bondage, set free by a word, and then sent to tell of the mercy he had been shown. The first work He gave the rescued man was simply to testify to his rescue—and I understood that this was how the good news would travel, from one freed captive to the next. Being with Jesus meant seeing captivity turned to freedom even in places that feared and rejected the Liberator, and my own resolve to stay close deepened with every mile.", "We crossed back over the lake, the water now calm and reflective under the sky. The boat moved steadily, the oars dipping with a quiet rhythm, and I sat near Jesus, still awestruck by the sudden stillness He had commanded. My clothes were damp, my body tired from the fear that had gripped me moments before, yet a new layer of trust was forming inside—like a root going deeper into soil I had not known was there. The other side felt familiar again as we approached Capernaum, the town’s lights beginning to appear in the gathering dusk.", "As soon as we stepped ashore, a large crowd was waiting. They pressed around Jesus eagerly, voices overlapping in excitement and need. I stayed close to Him, helping to make a way through the people. Among them came Jairus, one of the rulers of the synagogue. He was a man of standing, yet in that moment he fell at Jesus’ feet, his face tight with desperation. “My little daughter is at the point of death,” he pleaded. “Come and lay your hands on her, so that she may be made well and live.” His voice broke with fatherly love and fear. Jesus agreed at once, and we began moving toward Jairus’ house. It struck me that a ruler of the synagogue and the demon-ridden man of the tombs had been brought to the very same place—on their faces before Him, with nothing to offer but their need. I walked just behind, sensing the urgency in the air, my own heart beating faster in sympathy for the man.", "In the midst of the pressing crowd, a woman approached from behind. She had suffered from a flow of blood for twelve years—long years of isolation and exhaustion under the weight of her condition, unclean by the law and shut out from the worship of her people. She had spent all she had on physicians, yet only grew worse. Quietly, almost secretly, she reached out and touched the fringe of Jesus’ garment. I did not see it happen at first, but Jesus stopped suddenly and asked, “Who touched my garments?” The disciples looked around in confusion; the crowd was so dense it seemed impossible to know. But the woman, trembling with fear and hope, came forward and fell down before Him. She told Him the whole truth, her voice shaking. I watched her closely—her face worn yet filled with a fragile courage.", "Jesus looked at her with kindness that seemed to wrap around her like a warm covering. “Daughter,” He said gently, “your faith has made you well. Go in peace, and be healed of your disease.” He would not let her slip away healed but unknown; He called her out so He could call her daughter—not merely mend her body but name her, restore her to a place among His people. At that moment she knew she was healed. I saw the relief flood her expression, her shoulders straightening as if a heavy burden had been lifted after twelve long years. Her quiet act of faith touched me deeply. In the chaos of the crowd, she had reached out in desperation and found mercy. It made me reflect on my own hesitations, my own need to reach for Him more boldly, trusting that even a trembling touch of faith would not go unanswered.", "While Jesus was still speaking to her, messengers came from Jairus’ house with heavy news. “Your daughter is dead,” they told him. “Why trouble the Teacher any further?” Jairus’ face crumpled with grief. But Jesus turned to him immediately and said with calm strength, “Do not fear, only believe.” Those words steadied something in me as well—a quiet command to keep trusting precisely when everything said to give up. We continued on to the house, the atmosphere now thick with sorrow. At the door, there was already weeping and wailing. Jesus allowed only Peter, James, John, and me to go inside with Him and the girl’s parents.", "Inside, the child lay on her bed—twelve years old, still and pale. The room felt heavy with finality. Jesus took her by the hand and said softly, “Talitha cumi,” which means, “Little girl, I say to you, arise.” Her eyes opened. She rose at once and began to walk. Her parents were overcome with amazement and joy, tears flowing freely. Jesus told them quietly to give her something to eat, showing care even in the miracle. I stood there, my breath caught in my throat, a profound sense of wonder filling me. Death had yielded again to His word, and this time I had been close enough to see His hand close around the cold little hand and warm it back to life. He spoke to death as gently as a mother wakes a sleeping child, and it obeyed Him. In that quiet room I felt the nearness of God in a way that left me hushed inside, certain that the One who could take death by the hand had come to do exactly that for all of us.", "These events continued to shape me day by day. Each miracle revealed more of who He was, and each moment in His presence made me more aware of my own heart’s need for His touch. I followed Him with a deeper commitment, storing these things in my memory like treasures I would one day understand more fully—glimpses, I was beginning to sense, of a single great rescue He was moving toward, of which every healing was a foretaste.", "We left Jairus’ house as the news of his daughter’s return to life began spreading through the streets of Capernaum like quiet ripples on the lake. I walked beside the others, my steps measured, my mind still replaying the moment Jesus took the girl’s small hand and spoke those gentle Aramaic words. The tenderness in His voice, the immediate response in her eyes as life returned—it lingered in me, stirring a mixture of awe and quiet introspection. I felt more aware than ever of how fragile life was, and how, in His hands, even death became something He could simply reach into and reverse.", "In the days that followed, Jesus continued teaching and healing, but the pace was growing more demanding. The crowds seemed to multiply, drawn by reports of what He had done. One Sabbath He returned to His hometown of Nazareth. We entered the synagogue with Him, the familiar space filled with people who had known Him since childhood. I sat among the local men, watching their faces as Jesus stood to read and then began to teach. His words carried the same authority I had come to recognize, full of insight and truth. For a time the people listened with interest, murmuring about the wisdom He displayed.", "But then the tone shifted. Someone whispered, “Is not this the carpenter, the son of Mary?” Others nodded, mentioning His brothers and sisters. I saw skepticism harden into offense on their faces. They could not reconcile the ordinary man they thought they knew with the power they had heard about. Jesus looked at them with quiet sadness and said, “A prophet is not without honor, except in his hometown and among his relatives and in his own house.” Their unbelief was so strong that He could do no mighty work there, except healing a few sick people by laying His hands on them. As we left Nazareth, I felt a heaviness in my chest. I had seen so much belief and transformation elsewhere; here, familiarity had blinded them. It taught me something sobering about the human heart—that salvation could come and stand in a man’s own synagogue, and he could shrug and call it the carpenter’s boy. The gift had to be received with the empty hands of faith, and these were too full of what they thought they already knew. Jesus’ calm acceptance of their rejection strengthened my own resolve to see Him clearly, beyond any preconceptions.", "Not long after, Jesus called the twelve of us together. He gave us authority over unclean spirits and sent us out two by two. I went with one of the others, carrying nothing but a staff—no bread, no bag, no money. His instructions were clear: stay in one house where we were welcomed, shake the dust off our feet if a town would not receive us. We preached that people should repent, cast out demons, and anointed many sick with oil so they recovered. As we traveled from village to village, I felt a new sense of purpose and dependence. The mercy I had only watched Him pour out was now, by His authority, flowing through my own hands and words—not because I had become worthy of it, but because He had chosen to spread His rescue through ordinary, sent men. Speaking in His name, seeing lives touched, was both exhilarating and humbling. I returned to Jesus later with stories of what had happened, my heart full of gratitude for being entrusted with such work.", "Around this time, reports reached us about John the Baptist. Herod had imprisoned him and, at the urging of Herodias, had him beheaded. When the disciples of John brought the news to Jesus, I saw a deep sorrow pass over His face. He had honored John publicly, calling him the greatest among those born of women. The death of the forerunner sent a cold thread of foreboding through me; if the one sent to prepare the way was cut down by the powers of this world, what would those powers do to the One he had prepared the way for? We withdrew for a time to a desolate place to rest, but the crowds followed. Seeing the great multitude, Jesus had compassion on them because they were like sheep without a shepherd—and I understood that this was what had drawn Him out of heaven in the first place: a shepherd coming after a flock that had no one to lead it home. He began teaching them many things.", "As the day grew late, the disciples suggested sending the people away to buy food. But Jesus said, “You give them something to eat.” We had only five loaves and two fish. I watched as He took the bread and fish, looked up to heaven, blessed and broke them, then gave them to us to distribute. The food multiplied in His hands. I walked among the groups of fifty and a hundred, handing out pieces that never seemed to run out. Men, women, and children ate until they were satisfied. When we gathered the leftovers, there were twelve baskets full. I handled those fragments with care, my hands still amazed at what they had witnessed. Five thousand men, plus women and children, fed from so little. He took bread, blessed it, broke it, and gave it—and though I did not see it then, the same hands would soon take bread again, bless and break it, and call it His body given for us. In that moment I felt only a profound sense of abundance and care: Jesus provided not just enough, but more than enough, meeting the hunger of the crowds and the doubt of His followers alike with open-handed generosity. My heart swelled with worship and a deeper trust.", "These experiences continued to form me. Each day with Jesus brought fresh revelations of His compassion, His authority, and His intimate knowledge of our hearts. I followed more closely, learning to trust even when the way seemed impossible, storing every memory like bread that would sustain me through whatever lay ahead.", "We withdrew after the great feeding, but the crowds lingered with excitement, some even wanting to make Jesus king by force. He sent us ahead in the boat while He dismissed the people Himself. I climbed into the boat with the others as evening fell, the water dark and still beneath us. Jesus went up on the mountain alone to pray. I watched His figure grow smaller against the hillside until the gathering dusk hid Him. It struck me that the crowds wanted to seize Him and crown Him for the bread He gave, while He slipped away to pray—as if the kingdom He had come to bring could not be hurried into being by force, but only given in His own way and His own hour. Sitting at the oars, I felt a quiet reverence mixed with weariness. There was so much I did not yet understand about Him.", "We rowed across the lake toward Capernaum. The wind rose suddenly, strong and contrary. Waves began to batter the boat, and we strained at the oars, our arms burning with effort. The darkness was thick, the boat rocking heavily. I gripped the wood tightly, my heart beating fast with the familiar fear that had gripped us before. We had rowed for hours, making little progress, when we saw something that stopped our breath—a figure walking on the water, coming toward us. It looked like a ghost in the dim light. Terror filled the boat. We cried out in alarm.", "But then His voice reached us over the wind: “Take heart; it is I. Do not be afraid.” It was Jesus. My fear shifted at once into awe. Peter called out, “Lord, if it is you, command me to come to you on the water.” Jesus said, “Come.” Peter stepped out of the boat and began walking on the waves toward Him. For a few moments it seemed possible—his eyes fixed on Jesus. Then he looked at the wind and the waves, and doubt took him. He began to sink, crying, “Lord, save me!” Jesus reached out His hand immediately and caught him. “O you of little faith,” He said gently, “why did you doubt?” Together they climbed into the boat, and the wind ceased at once. The water grew calm around us. I have never forgotten the shortness of that prayer—“Lord, save me!”—three words gasped out by a sinking man, and the hand that caught him before he had even finished. I began to suspect that this was the whole of what any of us could do, and the whole of what He asked: to cry out as we went under, and to be caught.", "We were all overcome. I knelt there in the boat, my clothes wet, my body trembling not just from cold but from the encounter. “Truly you are the Son of God,” we said to Him. In that moment, after the terror and the rescue, I felt a deeper surrender taking root in me. I had seen Him command storms before, but this—walking on the sea itself—revealed His mastery over creation in a new way. My heart was full of worship and a quiet awareness of my own frailty, and of how completely my safety lay not in my own grip on the boat but in His grip on me.", "When we reached the land at Gennesaret, people recognized Him at once. They brought the sick on mats to the marketplaces, begging only to touch the fringe of His garment. And as many as touched it were made well. I moved among them, helping where I could, watching the steady flow of mercy. Even the faintest reach toward Him—a fingertip on the hem of His robe—was met with healing, as if no approach to Him in faith was ever too small to be answered. Each healing deepened the conviction growing inside me: this was no ordinary teacher.", "Later, in Capernaum, the crowds found us again, still seeking Him after the feeding. Jesus taught in the synagogue there, and His words took a serious turn. “I am the bread of life,” He declared. “Your fathers ate the manna in the wilderness, and they died. This is the bread that comes down from heaven, so that one may eat of it and not die. I am the living bread that came down from heaven. If anyone eats of this bread, he will live forever. And the bread that I will give for the life of the world is my flesh.” The crowds had chased Him for another meal; He offered them Himself, and spoke for the first time so plainly of a body that would be given for the life of the world. I did not understand it, but I felt the ground of everything shift beneath the words—as though every loaf He had ever broken had been pointing here.", "The people began to grumble. “How can this man give us his flesh to eat?” they asked. Even some of the disciples found it hard. Jesus did not soften the teaching. He spoke of eating His flesh and drinking His blood as the way to abide in Him and have life. Many turned away that day, walking no longer with Him. I watched them leave, feeling the weight of the moment. Jesus turned to the twelve of us and asked quietly, “Do you want to go away as well?” Simon Peter answered for us: “Lord, to whom shall we go? You have the words of eternal life, and we have believed, and have come to know, that you are the Holy One of God.”", "His question searched me deeply. I felt the pull of the hard saying, yet something stronger held me—the recognition that life itself was in Him, and nowhere else. Where would I go? Back to a religion of washings and rules that could not reach the rot inside me, back to a world that ran out of wine and buried its only sons? There was no other well. In that synagogue, surrounded by the familiar smells of bread and oil lamps, I chose again to stay. My heart was committed, even when understanding lagged far behind.", "After that difficult day in the synagogue at Capernaum, many who had followed Jesus turned back and no longer walked with Him. The air in the room felt heavier as they left, their footsteps echoing on the stone floor, voices murmuring in confusion and offense. I remained seated among the twelve, my hands resting on my knees, feeling the weight of the moment. Jesus’ words about His flesh and blood had tested us all. They were strange, even shocking, yet something in them rang with a truth I could not deny. When Peter spoke for us—“Lord, to whom shall we go? You have the words of eternal life”—I nodded inwardly. Those words became my own quiet resolution. I had seen too much, heard too much, tasted too deeply of the life He gave, to walk away now. My heart was bound to Him, even when my understanding faltered.", "We left Capernaum and continued through Galilee, though Jesus no longer moved as openly. He taught us privately as we traveled, focusing on the deeper matters of the heart. One day some Pharisees and scribes from Jerusalem came to question Him. They noticed that some of us ate with hands that were not ceremonially washed according to the tradition of the elders. Their tone was sharp, accusatory. “Why do your disciples not walk according to the tradition of the elders, but eat with defiled hands?”", "Jesus answered them directly, His voice steady and clear. He quoted the prophet Isaiah: “This people honors me with their lips, but their heart is far from me; in vain do they worship me, teaching as doctrines the commandments of men.” Then He turned the challenge back on them, speaking of how they set aside God’s commandments for their own traditions—like the practice of Corban, where a person could declare resources devoted to God and thus avoid helping their parents. I listened closely, feeling the force of His words. He was exposing the difference between outward appearance and inner reality. It convicted me. How often had I focused on external rules while neglecting the weightier matters of justice, mercy, and faithfulness? His teaching felt like a mirror held up to my soul—uncomfortable, yet cleansing.", "He called the crowd to Him again and said, “Hear me, all of you, and understand: there is nothing outside a person that by going into him can defile him, but the things that come out of a person are what defile him.” Later, when we were alone, He explained further: evil thoughts, sexual immorality, theft, murder, adultery, coveting, wickedness, deceit, sensuality, envy, slander, pride, foolishness—all these come from within and defile a person. The words sank into me deeply. I saw then why no amount of washing could ever make me clean: the defilement welled up from inside, from the heart itself, and no ritual could reach that far down. If I were ever to be made clean, it would have to be by someone who could remake the heart—and I was beginning to know who that someone was. Being near Jesus meant allowing Him to search the hidden places of my heart. There was no hiding, but also no rejection—only the call to be made new.", "From Galilee we moved northward toward the region of Tyre and Sidon. Jesus seemed to want quieter time with us. A Syrophoenician woman, a Gentile, came seeking Him. Her daughter was severely oppressed by an unclean spirit. She begged Him for help, her voice full of desperate motherly love. At first Jesus was silent, testing the moment. The disciples urged Him to send her away. But she persisted, saying, “Yes, Lord; yet even the dogs under the table eat the children’s crumbs.” Her humility and faith moved Him. “For this saying,” He told her, “you may go your way; the demon has left your daughter.” When she returned home, she found her child well. I watched the exchange with growing respect. She had asked for nothing but a crumb, claiming no right to the children’s bread—and that very humility was the door her faith walked through. Jesus’ mercy reached past the borders of Israel to a Gentile mother who would take even the leftovers of grace, and it enlarged my own vision of how wide the kingdom would prove to be.", "We returned through the Decapolis, and there people brought to Him a man who was deaf and had a speech impediment. They begged Jesus to lay His hand on him. Taking the man aside privately, away from the crowd, Jesus put His fingers into the man’s ears, spat and touched his tongue, then looked up to heaven, sighed, and said, “Ephphatha,” that is, “Be opened.” At once the man’s ears were opened, his tongue was released, and he spoke plainly. The people were astonished beyond measure. I stood nearby, witnessing the intimate care Jesus showed—drawing the man aside, using touch in a way that seemed so human, yet carried divine power. It moved me that the One who could speak galaxies into being stooped to put His own fingers in a deaf man’s ears, as if salvation always came this near, this personal.", "These journeys and teachings continued to shape me. I felt myself changing—less quick to judge by outward appearances, more aware of the condition of the heart, more open to the breadth of God’s mercy. Following Jesus meant walking in growing light, even when the path included hard words and unexpected turns. My commitment to Him had become the central reality of my life.", "That country has stayed with me, and the healing of the deaf man most of all, so let me tell it as I saw it up close. We had come back through the Decapolis after leaving the regions of Tyre and Sidon. The landscape opened up into a different world—ten Greek cities scattered across the hills and plains east of the Jordan and the Sea of Galilee. It was Gentile territory, more Hellenistic in feel than the villages of Galilee. Columns from pagan temples rose in the distance against the sky, and the air carried the faint scent of incense from distant altars mixed with the drier smell of pastureland and olive groves. The people here spoke Greek more readily, and their faces showed a blend of cultures—some with the sharper features of traders from the east, others tanned and sturdy like farmers of the Decapolis. It felt foreign to me, yet Jesus moved through it with the same steady purpose He carried everywhere. Being in this region reminded me how far His reach already extended, beyond the familiar boundaries of Israel—into the very places we had been taught to count as outside the mercy of God.", "The crowds found us again, bringing the sick and the broken. The deaf man stands out in my memory above the rest. His speech was so impaired he could barely make understandable sounds. His friends led him forward, their eyes pleading as they begged Jesus to lay a hand on him. Jesus did not heal him in the open press of the crowd. Instead, He took the man aside privately, away from the noise and staring eyes. I followed at a short distance and watched closely.", "Jesus put His fingers into the man’s ears, then spat and touched the man’s tongue with the moisture. It was an intimate, almost earthy gesture—human and direct. Then He looked up to heaven, sighed deeply as if carrying the man’s burden in His own spirit, and spoke one clear word: “Ephphatha.” That is, “Be opened.” At once the man’s ears were opened. His face changed in an instant—eyes widening as sound flooded in. His tongue loosened, and he began speaking plainly, words tumbling out with growing excitement. I stood there, my own breath catching, feeling a surge of holy wonder move through me. That sigh stayed with me longest: He did not heal at arm’s length but seemed to take the man’s closed-up silence into Himself before He opened it, as though every rescue cost Him something. Jesus had not needed dramatic displays; a touch, a sigh, a word—and barriers that had shut a man off from the world fell away. I felt humbled witnessing it, aware again of how much I myself needed opening in places I could not reach on my own.", "The people were astonished beyond measure. They brought more and more to Him—lame, blind, crippled, mute—and He healed them all. For three days the crowd stayed with us, listening to His teaching and receiving His care. Their faces, once marked by pain and isolation, began to shine with hope. I moved among them, helping distribute what little we had, my heart full with a growing sense of the kingdom’s reach. This was not only for Israel. Here in the Decapolis, among Gentiles who had known other gods and other ways, Jesus was revealing the mercy of the true God—and I remembered the Samaritan well, and the centurion, and the Gentile mother content with crumbs, and saw that they were all of a piece. The Savior of the world had meant exactly that.", "When the three days drew to a close and the people grew hungry, Jesus called us aside. “I have compassion on the crowd,” He said, “because they have been with me now three days and have nothing to eat. And I am unwilling to send them away hungry, lest they faint on the way.” We had only seven loaves and a few small fish. The memory of the earlier feeding near Capernaum was still fresh, yet we still struggled to believe. Jesus took the bread, gave thanks, broke it, and gave it to us to distribute. Once again, the food multiplied in His hands. I walked among the groups, handing out pieces that never seemed to end. Four thousand men ate, along with women and children, until all were satisfied. When we gathered the leftovers, there were seven baskets full. He had broken bread for the children of Israel on the green grass of Galilee; now He broke it just the same for a hungry Gentile crowd in pagan country. The same blessed, broken bread, given to outsiders—His grace kept the same shape no matter whose hands received it.", "After this, He sent the crowds away and got into the boat with us, heading back toward the western shore. As I sat in the boat watching the Decapolis hills recede behind us, I reflected on all I had seen. The region had shown me that the life He carried was not confined to one people or place. My spirit felt enlarged, my commitment to Him deepened. Every new place, every new healing, was slowly teaching me that the salvation He brought was meant for the whole world, and that following Him meant being willing to go wherever that salvation needed to be carried.", "We sailed back across the lake from the Decapolis, the boat moving steadily under a quiet sky. The hills on the eastern shore slowly faded behind us, their pagan temples and open plains giving way once more to the familiar shoreline of Galilee. I sat near the stern, the gentle rocking of the water beneath me, my mind still turning over the days we had spent among the Gentiles. The abundance of the seven loaves, the healed man speaking clearly for the first time, the astonished faces of the crowd—all of it settled deep in me like a quiet confirmation. Jesus’ mercy was wider than I had imagined.", "When we reached the other side and came to Dalmanutha, the Pharisees and Sadducees soon found us. They approached as a group, their robes clean and their expressions tight with challenge. “Give us a sign from heaven,” they demanded, “to prove you are who you claim.” Their voices carried suspicion and a desire to test Him. I watched Jesus closely. He sighed, a sound heavy with sorrow rather than anger, and answered them, “Why does this generation seek a sign? Truly, no sign will be given to this generation.” He turned away from them without further words. I felt the tension in the air, the growing opposition that seemed to follow us now like a shadow. It troubled me that men could stand in the presence of the Savior, watch Him heal and feed thousands, and still demand He prove Himself—as if the trouble were never the lack of evidence but a heart unwilling to come.", "We got back into the boat and crossed once more. As we rowed, Jesus warned us, “Watch out; beware of the leaven of the Pharisees and the leaven of Herod.” We thought He was speaking about bread, since we had forgotten to bring any. But He knew our discussion and gently rebuked us: “Why are you discussing the fact that you have no bread? Do you not yet perceive or understand? Are your hearts hardened? Having eyes, do you not see, and having ears, do you not hear?” His words pierced me. He reminded us of the two feedings—the five thousand and the four thousand—and how much was left over each time. Slowly it dawned on us that He was warning against the teaching of the Pharisees, a subtle corruption of self-righteousness that could spread silently like yeast through a whole loaf. I sat quietly after that, recognizing in myself how easily I could be soured by the very pride that refused to come to Him empty-handed. His patience with us, even in our slowness, stirred a deeper affection in my heart.", "We came to Bethsaida. There, some people brought a blind man to Jesus and begged Him to touch him. Jesus took the man by the hand and led him out of the village, away from the noise. I followed at a respectful distance and watched. He spat on the man’s eyes, laid His hands on him, and asked, “Do you see anything?” The man looked up and said, “I see people, but they look like trees walking.” His sight was partial, unclear. Jesus laid His hands on him once more. Then the man’s eyes were opened fully, and he saw everything clearly. Jesus sent him home, telling him not to enter the village. I stood there, moved by the tenderness of the healing—the gradual restoration, the patient second touch. It mirrored my own slow seeing exactly: I, too, had glimpsed Him in blurred and partial ways, mistaking trees for men, and I, too, needed His hand to come back again and again before I could see who He truly was. Gratitude filled me, and a quiet humility that He did not grow weary of touching half-blind eyes a second time.", "From there we traveled north toward the region of Caesarea Philippi. The hills grew steeper, the air cooler as we climbed. One day, when we were alone with Him, Jesus asked us, “Who do people say that I am?” We answered with the various opinions we had heard—John the Baptist, Elijah, one of the prophets. Then He looked at us directly and asked, “But who do you say that I am?” Peter answered without hesitation, his voice strong: “You are the Christ, the Son of the living God.” I felt something shift inside me as the words were spoken. They gathered up everything I had seen since the Jordan—the Lamb, the wine, the new birth, the forgiven sins, the bread of life, the wind made still, the dead raised—into a single confession. This was who He was. Jesus blessed Peter, speaking of the rock and the church He would build, and of the keys of the kingdom. The moment carried a weight and a glory I could scarcely take in.", "Yet right after, Jesus began to teach us plainly that He must go to Jerusalem, suffer many things, be killed, and rise again on the third day. The words stunned me. We had just confessed Him the Christ, the Son of the living God—and the first thing He told us about the Christ was that He would be killed. Peter even pulled Him aside to rebuke Him, but Jesus turned and said, “Get behind me, Satan! You are a hindrance to me. For you are not setting your mind on the things of God, but on the things of man.” I felt shaken. The rebuke was sharp because the temptation was real: every part of me, like Peter, wanted a Christ who would conquer, not one who would die. Yet there was no mistaking how firmly Jesus set His face toward it. The death was not a tragedy that might befall Him; it was the very thing He had come to do, the road to which all the rest had been leading. My heart wrestled with fear and confusion, yet underneath it the conviction held: this was the One we had waited for, and if His way ran through suffering, then suffering must somehow be the shape of our rescue.", "We continued northward into the region near Caesarea Philippi, the hills rising higher and the air growing cooler as we walked. The pagan temples and shrines to false gods dotted the landscape in the distance, a constant reminder of the spiritual darkness that still covered much of the world. Yet Jesus moved among it all with quiet confidence, as though He carried a light no shadow could overcome. He told us plainly that if anyone would come after Him, he must deny himself, take up his cross, and follow—that whoever sought to save his life would lose it, but whoever lost his life for His sake would find it. The words landed heavily so soon after His talk of suffering and death. I began to see that the cross He was walking toward was not His alone to carry; the life He gave would reshape ours into the same cross-bearing pattern, and that losing everything to follow Him was, strangely, the only way to gain anything that lasted. Six days after Peter’s confession, Jesus took Peter, James, John, and me up a high mountain to pray. The climb was steady, the path rocky beneath our sandals, but being with Him made the effort feel purposeful. My legs tired, yet my spirit felt drawn upward, expectant without knowing why.", "As the evening deepened, Jesus took Peter, James, and John and began to climb a high mountain alone with them to pray. The rest of us He left at the foot of it, and we made a rough camp among the rocks below, watching the three figures grow smaller against the darkening slope until the night swallowed them entirely. I remember the strange ache of being left behind, the longing to follow tangled together with a quiet trust that He knew what He was doing. We kept a low fire and spoke little. The mountain loomed over us, black against the stars, and once or twice I thought I saw a faint light high on the heights, though I could not say whether it was the moon on bare stone or something I had no word for. None of us slept easily that night.", "It was not until they came down that we learned what had happened up there, and Peter could hardly tell it without his voice shaking. He said that as Jesus prayed, the appearance of His face had changed and begun to shine like the sun, and His clothing turned a dazzling white, whiter than anyone on earth could bleach it. While the three of them stared, two men appeared and stood speaking with Him—Moses and Elijah, the law and the prophets themselves—and they spoke with Him of His departure, which He was about to accomplish at Jerusalem. Peter admitted, almost ashamed, that he had blurted out an offer to put up three shelters, one for each of them, not knowing what he was saying, for they were terrified. John told the rest of it more quietly. A bright cloud had come and overshadowed them, he said, and out of the cloud a voice had spoken: “This is my beloved Son, my Chosen One; listen to Him.” At the sound of it the three had fallen on their faces in dread, until Jesus came and touched them and said, “Rise, and have no fear.” When they lifted their eyes, He was standing alone again, just as they had always known Him.", "They told us these things in low voices as we walked, for Jesus had charged the three to tell no one what they had seen until the Son of Man had risen from the dead. We turned that last phrase over among ourselves, uncertain what rising He meant, and why glory and suffering should be spoken in the same breath. But the thing that lodged deepest in me was what Moses and Elijah had come to discuss with Him: not His glory, but His departure—His death at Jerusalem. The law and the prophets had stood in blazing light only to speak of the cross, as if the whole of Scripture had always been leaning toward that one dark hour, and the radiant Son had come down the mountain precisely in order to die. I had not stood inside that cloud, and some part of me wished that I had; yet hearing it from them, watching the awe still trembling in their faces, was its own kind of witness. The Jesus who walked the dusty road at my side, who would soon eat with us and sleep beneath the same open sky, was the very One whose face had blazed like the noon sun—and that One was walking, on purpose, toward His death for us. I walked the rest of the way in silence, carrying a glory I had only heard described, and somehow it steadied me for the road ahead.", "Even before they had come down from the mountain, a large crowd had gathered around the rest of us at its foot. A man came forward out of the crowd, his face etched with desperation. He had brought his only son, who was severely afflicted by an unclean spirit. The boy would suddenly cry out, foam at the mouth, grind his teeth, and be thrown to the ground in violent convulsions. The father had begged us to cast it out, but we could not, and some scribes had begun to argue with us while the crowd looked on; I felt the sting of our helplessness keenly. When Jesus came down and saw the commotion, the father turned to Him and pleaded, “If you can do anything, have compassion on us and help us.” Jesus replied with both challenge and invitation: “If you can believe, all things are possible to one who believes.” The father cried out with tears, “I believe; help my unbelief!”", "That cry became one of my own truest prayers, for I heard my whole heart in it. The man did not pretend to a faith he did not have, nor did he give up for lack of it; he brought even his unbelief to Jesus and asked Him to mend it. I saw in that moment that faith itself was not a coin I produced to purchase healing, but a gift I could ask for from the very One I was struggling to trust. I watched as Jesus rebuked the unclean spirit, commanding it to come out and never enter the boy again. The spirit cried out and convulsed the boy violently one last time before leaving him. The child lay still, as if dead, but Jesus took him by the hand and lifted him up, and he stood whole. The crowd was amazed. Later, when we were alone, the disciples asked Jesus why they had failed. He told them, “This kind cannot be driven out by anything but prayer.” His answer humbled me again. Set side by side—the glory the three had seen on the heights, and the failure we had just met at the foot of the mountain—these showed me that our own strength was never the point; the rescue was His, and it came down to us through dependence, not ability. My heart remained fixed on Him, even as the road toward Jerusalem began to feel heavier with unspoken cost.", "We continued our journey, moving more privately now through Galilee. Jesus wanted to avoid the crowds for a time, for He was teaching us the deeper things that lay ahead. As we walked the familiar roads between villages, the sun warm on our shoulders and the scent of olive groves and grain fields around us, He began speaking plainly once more: “The Son of Man is going to be delivered into the hands of men, and they will kill Him, and when He is killed, after three days He will rise.”", "The words fell on us heavily. I walked just behind Him, my sandals pressing into the dusty path, and felt a coldness settle in my stomach despite the warmth of the day. We did not understand what He meant, and we were afraid to ask Him. A quiet fear gripped my heart—not the holy awe of the mountain, but a deeper dread. Yet I noticed that He spoke of being killed and of rising again in the same breath, the way Moses and Elijah had joined glory and departure on the mountain; and somewhere beneath my fear a small, stubborn hope began to grow that the death He kept naming was not the end of the story but the heart of it. I had seen too many impossible things to doubt His power, even if I could not yet grasp why power should choose to suffer.", "We returned to Capernaum and gathered in the house where we often stayed. The familiar smell of baked bread and fish lingered in the air. Some tax collectors approached Peter at the door, asking, “Does your teacher not pay the temple tax?” Peter answered quickly that He did. When he came inside, Jesus spoke to him first: “What do you think, Simon? From whom do kings of the earth take toll or tax? From their sons or from others?” Peter replied, “From others.” Jesus nodded. “Then the sons are free. However, not to give offense to them, go to the sea and cast a hook, and take the first fish that comes up. Open its mouth and you will find a shekel. Take that and give it to them for me and for yourself.”", "I listened in quiet amazement as Peter later returned with the coin exactly as Jesus had said. The ordinary and the miraculous wove together so naturally in His presence. But what stayed with me was the heart of His question: as the Son of the Temple’s God He owed nothing, He was free—yet He chose to place Himself under the tax all the same. I was beginning to see a pattern in Him that I could not yet name: the One who owed nothing kept putting Himself under what He did not owe, for the sake of others. He was Lord even over the fish in the sea and the laws of men, yet again and again He stepped beneath them.", "Later that same day, an argument arose among us. We had been discussing who was the greatest in the kingdom. The question had started innocently enough on the road but had grown heated. Jesus knew our thoughts. He sat down, called us to Him, and took a little child in His arms. The child stood quietly against Him, small and trusting. Jesus said, “Whoever receives one such child in my name receives me, and whoever receives me receives not me but Him who sent me.” Then He added, “If anyone would be first, he must be last of all and servant of all.”", "His words cut through our pride like a clean blade. I felt ashamed as I looked at the child’s innocent face. We had been striving for position, yet Jesus pointed us toward humility and service. And I could not help seeing that He was describing Himself: the One who was first of all had made Himself last of all, the Lord becoming servant, even now setting His face toward the lowest place of all on a cross. The greatness of the kingdom ran in exactly the opposite direction from the greatness of the world, and the road down was the road He Himself was walking to save us. I resolved in my heart to learn this way, even though I knew it would be difficult.", "John then spoke up about something that had been bothering him. “Teacher, we saw someone casting out demons in your name, and we tried to stop him, because he was not following us.” Jesus replied gently but firmly, “Do not stop him, for no one who does a mighty work in my name will be able soon afterward to speak evil of me. For the one who is not against us is for us.” The answer broadened my understanding once more. The kingdom was larger than our small circle, and its saving power was not ours to hoard. Jealousy had no place in it.", "These teachings settled deeply into me as we prepared to move toward Jerusalem. The road ahead felt more solemn now. I walked with a quieter spirit, storing His words like treasure, aware that following Him meant laying down my pride, my fears, and my limited understanding. Yet in His presence, even the hard lessons carried hope. He was leading us, and I would continue to follow, step by step, wherever the path took us.", "We remained in Capernaum a little longer, the familiar rhythm of life by the lake continuing around us while everything within us felt increasingly unsettled. One evening in the house, after the children had been sent to bed and the oil lamps burned low, Peter came to Jesus with a question that had been weighing on him. “Lord,” he asked, “how often will my brother sin against me, and I forgive him? As many as seven times?”", "Jesus looked at him with that steady, penetrating gaze I had come to know so well. “I do not say to you seven times, but seventy-seven times.” Then He told us a parable about a king who settled accounts with his servants. One servant owed an enormous debt—ten thousand talents—and begged for patience. The king, moved with compassion, forgave the entire debt. But that same servant went out and seized a fellow servant who owed him only a hundred denarii, refusing to show the same mercy. The king’s anger burned when he heard of it, and he delivered the unforgiving servant to the jailers until the debt was paid.", "As Jesus finished the story, the room was quiet except for the soft crackle of the lamps. I felt the parable sink like a stone into deep water inside me. How easily I could see myself in that first servant—owing a debt I could never repay in a hundred lifetimes, forgiven it all out of sheer compassion, and then turning to choke a brother over some trifling hundred denarii. I understood for the first time that this was the whole arithmetic of the kingdom: I had been forgiven an unpayable debt, and every grudge I clung to was a refusal to let that mercy flow through me. A heart that had truly received such pardon could not hold its own pardons back; if it did, it showed it had never grasped the size of what it had been given. I sat there, hands folded, letting the truth work its way deeper, grateful for the gentle yet firm way He taught us.", "Not many days later, we left Capernaum and set our faces toward Jerusalem. The road south was well-traveled, winding through the hills of Samaria. The air grew warmer as we descended, carrying the scent of ripening figs and dust from the dry paths. Jesus sent messengers ahead to prepare the way, but when we entered a Samaritan village, the people would not receive us because our faces were set toward Jerusalem. The rejection stung. James and John, their tempers flaring like the “sons of thunder” they were, turned to Jesus and asked, “Lord, do you want us to tell fire to come down from heaven and consume them?” Their zeal was fierce, but Jesus turned and rebuked them. “You do not know what manner of spirit you are of; for the Son of Man came not to destroy people’s lives but to save them.”", "I walked beside them afterward in silence, turning that sentence over and over: the Son of Man came not to destroy but to save. It was as plain a statement of His whole purpose as I had ever heard, and it cut against everything in me that wanted Him to call down fire on those who spurned Him. He was bent on rescue, not ruin—even toward a village that had just shut its door in His face. The road felt heavier after that, the dust clinging more stubbornly to our robes, yet His presence remained the steady center that kept us moving forward, and I held that one word close: He had come to save.", "Later, Jesus appointed seventy-two others and sent them on ahead of Him, two by two, into every town and place where He Himself was about to go. I watched as they received their instructions: “The harvest is plentiful, but the laborers are few. Pray therefore the Lord of the harvest to send out laborers into His harvest.” They were to take nothing for the journey—no money bag, no sandals, no staff—and to heal the sick and proclaim that the kingdom of God had come near. As they departed in pairs along different roads, I felt a stirring of both excitement and responsibility. The work was expanding beyond the twelve of us. The harvest He spoke of was a field full of people to be gathered home, and He was sending laborers out into it ahead of His own coming—as if the saving He did could never be content to stay in one pair of hands.", "When the seventy-two returned, their faces were alight with joy. “Lord,” they said, “even the demons are subject to us in your name!” Jesus rejoiced in the Holy Spirit and praised the Father for hiding these things from the wise and revealing them to little children. Then He turned to us and said privately, “Blessed are the eyes that see what you see! For many prophets and kings desired to see what you see, and did not see it.” He told the seventy-two not to rejoice chiefly that the spirits obeyed them, but that their names were written in heaven—and I understood that the greatest wonder was never the power that flowed through us but the mercy that had claimed us, our names kept safe in heaven by grace we had not earned.", "Hearing those words filled me with a profound sense of privilege. I had walked with Him, heard from the three of the glory on the mountain, witnessed countless healings, heard teachings that would echo through the ages. Yet the road to Jerusalem now carried an undercurrent of gathering storm clouds. My heart held both gratitude and growing apprehension. Being with Jesus meant embracing the joy of the kingdom while also preparing for the cost—His cost, I was beginning to fear, more than ours. I continued to follow, step by step, storing every moment in my heart like seeds for a future harvest I could not yet fully see.", "We pressed on toward Jerusalem, the road growing more crowded with pilgrims as the days passed. One afternoon, as the sun beat down on the dusty path lined with rocky hills and scattered olive trees, a lawyer stood up to test Jesus. His voice carried the careful precision of one trained in the law: “Teacher, what shall I do to inherit eternal life?”", "Jesus answered by turning the question back to him: “What is written in the Law? How do you read it?” The lawyer replied smoothly, “You shall love the Lord your God with all your heart and with all your soul and with all your strength and with all your mind, and your neighbor as yourself.” Jesus nodded and said, “You have answered correctly; do this, and you will live.” I noticed He did not soften the law’s demand in the least—do this, and you will live—as if to let the man feel the full, impossible weight of a love he had never once managed to give.", "But the man, wanting to justify himself, asked further, “And who is my neighbor?” Jesus then told a story that has stayed with me ever since. A man was going down from Jerusalem to Jericho and fell among robbers. They stripped him, beat him, and left him half dead on the side of the road. A priest came by, saw him, and passed on the other side. Then a Levite came to the place, looked, and passed by as well. But a Samaritan—a man from the people we often despised—came upon him. When he saw the wounded man, he had compassion. He bound up his wounds, pouring on oil and wine, set him on his own animal, brought him to an inn, and took care of him. The next day he gave the innkeeper two denarii and promised to repay whatever more was spent.", "Jesus looked at the lawyer and asked, “Which of these three, do you think, proved to be a neighbor to the man who fell among the robbers?” The lawyer answered quietly, “The one who showed mercy on him.” Jesus said simply, “Go and do likewise.”", "I walked beside the others, the story working its way into my heart like good medicine. The Samaritan—unexpected, even unwelcome in our eyes—had become the true neighbor, spending his own oil and wine and silver on a stranger left for dead. The longer I carried the picture, the more it seemed to me a portrait of the Lord Himself: the despised One who comes to the very people who scorn Him, finds us robbed and beaten and half dead by the road, and pays out of His own purse the whole cost of our healing. The lawyer had asked what he must do to inherit eternal life; Jesus showed him a love so costly that no one keeps it—no one, except the One telling the story. I felt a quiet conviction stirring within me, and beneath it a deeper wonder at the Neighbor who had stopped for me.", "Not long after, we came to a village where a woman named Martha welcomed us into her home. She was busy with much serving—preparing food, moving quickly between tasks, her face flushed with effort. Her sister Mary sat at Jesus’ feet, listening to His teaching. Martha, growing frustrated, came and said, “Lord, do you not care that my sister has left me to serve alone? Tell her to help me.”", "Jesus answered with gentleness but clarity: “Martha, Martha, you are anxious and troubled about many things, but one thing is necessary. Mary has chosen the good portion, which will not be taken away from her.”", "I watched the exchange carefully. Martha’s diligence was good, yet Mary had grasped what mattered most: before anything could be done for Him, something had first to be received from Him. The one thing necessary was Jesus Himself—to sit at His feet and take in His words. I felt the gentle correction land on my own restless heart, which was always readier to work for Him than to receive from Him. In the simple house, with its smell of baking bread and herbs, I learned again that all true service must flow out of having first sat still and let Him fill me.", "These teachings traveled with me as we moved onward. The road to Jerusalem now felt both urgent and solemn. Every parable, every correction, every moment in His presence was shaping me further, stripping away old ways of thinking and replacing them with the realities of the kingdom. My spirit remained attentive and yielded, walking step by step with the One whose words carried both challenge and life.", "We continued our journey southward, the hills of Judea rising gradually before us as we drew nearer to Jerusalem. The air grew drier, carrying the sharp scent of wild thyme crushed under our sandals and the distant smoke of cooking fires from villages nestled in the valleys. One evening, after a long day of walking, we stopped in a place where Jesus often went apart to pray. We found Him there again, alone for a time, kneeling in the fading light. When He finished, one of us asked Him, “Lord, teach us to pray, as John taught his disciples.”", "Jesus sat with us under the gathering stars and gave us words that have since become the very breath of my life: “When you pray, say: Father, hallowed be your name. Your kingdom come. Give us each day our daily bread, and forgive us our sins, for we ourselves forgive everyone who is indebted to us. And lead us not into temptation.” The first word undid me—Father. He was teaching us to speak to the Most High the way He spoke to Him, as if His own sonship were being opened to make room for us, as if the whole point of the rescue were to bring beggars and sinners into a family and let us call God by that name. And the forgiveness of our sins sat right in the middle of it, asked for daily, as plainly as bread.", "The simplicity of it moved me deeply. No long, elaborate phrases—just a child speaking to a Father. I felt my own prayers, which had often been wordy and anxious, grow quieter within me. He continued teaching: “Ask, and it will be given to you; seek, and you will find; knock, and it will be opened to you.” His voice was low and steady in the cool night air. I sat close, feeling the weight of His words press gently against my heart. Prayer was not a performance, but relationship—persistent, trusting, humble—the speech of children newly brought home. In that moment, with the soft wind stirring the grass around us, I sensed my spirit being reshaped once more, drawn closer to the Father through the Son.", "The following days brought fresh challenges. We were in a house when Jesus cast out a demon that had made a man mute. The man spoke immediately, and the crowd marveled. But some accused Him, saying, “He casts out demons by Beelzebul, the prince of demons.” Others demanded a sign from heaven. Jesus answered them with calm authority, explaining that a kingdom divided against itself cannot stand. “But if it is by the finger of God that I cast out demons,” He said, “then the kingdom of God has come upon you.”", "I listened with my whole being, feeling both the power of His words and the growing resistance around us. His presence always seemed to draw a line—some were drawn closer in faith, others pulled away in offense. It struck me that there was no neutral ground near Him: the same finger of God that set captives free hardened those determined not to come. Later, when the crowds had thinned, He warned us about an unclean spirit returning with worse companions to a house left empty and swept. The teaching settled in my chest with sober weight: it was not enough to be cleansed of an old evil; the swept house had to be filled, and only His own life and presence could fill it. To be saved was not merely to be emptied of sin but to be inhabited by Him.", "As we traveled on, Jesus spoke more openly about the cost of following Him. “Foxes have holes, and birds of the air have nests,” He said to one eager man who wanted to follow, “but the Son of Man has nowhere to lay His head.” To another who asked to bury his father first, Jesus replied, “Leave the dead to bury their own dead. But as for you, go and proclaim the kingdom of God.” The words were sharp, yet spoken with love. I felt their edge in my own soul. Following Him meant everything else becoming secondary—not because the kingdom was a hard bargain, but because He was a treasure worth more than all I would lay down for Him. My old life—comforts, obligations, securities—seemed increasingly distant, like shadows fading in strong sunlight.", "These moments on the road were etching themselves into me. Each teaching, each healing, each quiet conversation under the open sky was slowly transforming the way I saw the world. My heart remained full—awed by His authority, humbled by His demands, and strangely at peace in His company. The path toward Jerusalem stretched ahead, more serious now, yet I walked it willingly, step by steady step, with the One who had become the center of my existence.", "We drew closer to Jerusalem, the holy city now appearing on the horizon like a crown set upon the hills. The roads were busier, filled with pilgrims making their way for the coming feasts. I walked with a growing awareness of the weight upon Jesus’ shoulders, though His step remained steady and unhurried. One day, as we rested under the shade of a spreading tree, He spoke again of the coming trials. “Behold, we are going up to Jerusalem, and everything that is written about the Son of Man by the prophets will be accomplished. For He will be delivered over to the Gentiles and will be mocked and shamefully treated and spit upon. And after flogging Him, they will kill Him, and on the third day He will rise.”", "The words landed heavily among us. I felt a tightness in my chest, a mingling of dread and confusion. How could this be? The One who calmed storms, raised the dead, and shone with divine glory on the mountain—how could He speak so calmly of suffering and death? Yet He kept saying it was written, that the prophets had marked out this very road long before, and that He was not being dragged toward Jerusalem but going up to it on purpose. I looked at His face and saw both sorrow and resolve there, like a man who saw the full path ahead and still chose to walk it for reasons I could not yet fathom. I did not understand, but I trusted. My love for Him had grown beyond fear now; it had become the ground I stood on.", "We arrived in the region near Jerusalem, and the tension in the air was unmistakable. Many were talking about Him in quiet corners and along the roads. Some said He was a good man; others said He was leading people astray. Jesus went up to the Feast of Tabernacles, not openly at first, but in a more hidden way. When He finally began teaching in the Temple courts, the people were astonished. “How is it that this man has learning, when He has never studied?” they asked. Jesus answered, “My teaching is not mine, but His who sent me. If anyone’s will is to do God’s will, he will know whether the teaching is from God or whether I am speaking on my own authority.”", "I stood in the outer court, the marble stones cool beneath my feet, the voices of merchants and worshippers echoing around the colonnades. I watched the faces of those listening—some angry, some intrigued, some afraid. The religious leaders sent officers to arrest Him, but they returned empty-handed, saying, “No one ever spoke like this man!” The power in His words was undeniable. They reached into the heart and exposed what was there—and I saw that a man’s response to those words was never really about the words at all, but about whether he was willing to do the will of God or determined to keep his own.", "One morning, as the sun rose higher and light flooded the Temple, the scribes and Pharisees brought a woman caught in adultery and set her before Him. She stood trembling, her head bowed in shame. They asked Jesus what should be done, reminding Him of the law that she should be stoned. I felt the trap they were laying. Jesus bent down and wrote with His finger on the ground. Then He stood and said, “Let him who is without sin among you be the first to throw a stone at her.” One by one, beginning with the older ones, they dropped their stones and left until only the woman remained. Jesus looked at her with kindness and said, “Woman, where are they? Has no one condemned you?” She answered, “No one, Lord.” He replied gently, “Neither do I condemn you; go, and from now on sin no more.”", "I felt the mercy in that moment like a cool wind on a hot day. The woman’s relief was visible—her shoulders softened, her eyes lifted with new hope. He had not pretended her sin was nothing; He told her plainly to leave it. Yet He sent her away forgiven rather than condemned, and only later did I understand the cost of it: the condemnation she had earned did not simply vanish. The One who refused to throw the stone would Himself, before long, stand condemned in her place. His mercy was never cheap; it was being charged, even then, to His own account. It stirred deep gratitude in me, along with a fresh awareness of my own need for that same mercy.", "As the feast continued, Jesus taught boldly in the Temple. “I am the light of the world,” He declared. “Whoever follows me will not walk in darkness, but will have the light of life.” The words rang through the courts with authority. Some believed, but others grew angrier, picking up stones to throw at Him. Yet His hour had not yet come, and He slipped away. I followed close behind, my heart pounding from the nearness of danger, yet more convinced than ever that He was the One sent from the Father. He had named Himself the light of the world, and I could see the proof in the very crowd around Him: the same light that gave the light of life to those who followed drove those who loved the dark into a fury. Being with Him in Jerusalem felt like standing at the very center of God’s story—holy, dangerous, and full of life. My spirit was quiet but resolute. I would stay with Him, no matter what the coming days would bring.", "We remained in Jerusalem as the Feast of Tabernacles drew to a close, the city still buzzing with pilgrims and the lingering scent of leafy booths and incense hanging in the air. One Sabbath, as we walked along a street near the Temple, we passed a man who had been blind from birth. He sat begging by the roadside, his eyes clouded and unseeing, his hands outstretched in quiet desperation. The disciples asked Jesus, “Rabbi, who sinned, this man or his parents, that he was born blind?”", "Jesus answered with a gentleness that cut through our assumptions: “It was not that this man sinned, or his parents, but that the works of God might be displayed in him. We must work the works of Him who sent me while it is day; night is coming, when no one can work. As long as I am in the world, I am the light of the world.” I had assumed the man’s darkness was a verdict on someone’s sin; Jesus saw it instead as a place for the mercy of God to be put on display. It made me wonder how much of what I counted as judgment was waiting, in His hands, to become a showcase for grace.", "I stood nearby, watching closely. Jesus spat on the ground, made mud with the saliva, and spread it on the man’s eyes. The blind man did not flinch or pull away; there was a quiet trust in his posture. Jesus then told him, “Go, wash in the pool of Siloam.” The man rose and felt his way along the street toward the pool, his steps uncertain but determined. I followed at a distance with the others, my heart beating with expectation. When he returned, his eyes were open—clear, seeing, filled with wonder as he looked around at the world for the first time. Colors, faces, the brightness of the sky—he drank it all in with speechless joy. The change in him was immediate and undeniable. People who had known him as the blind beggar began murmuring, some saying it was the same man, others insisting it was only someone like him. But he kept declaring, “I am the man.”", "The Pharisees soon heard of it and brought the healed man before them for questioning. I stood in the outer court, listening as the interrogation unfolded. Their voices were sharp with suspicion. They asked how he had received his sight, and when he told them about the mud and the washing, they divided among themselves. Some said Jesus could not be from God because He had made mud on the Sabbath. Others wondered how a sinner could do such signs. They called in the man’s parents, who confirmed he had been born blind but nervously distanced themselves from how he was healed, fearing the leaders. Then they questioned the man again, pressing him harshly. His answer grew bolder: “One thing I do know, that though I was blind, now I see.” Finally, in quiet defiance, he said, “If this man were not from God, he could do nothing.”", "They cast him out. I watched him leave, his shoulders straight despite the rejection. Later, Jesus found him and asked, “Do you believe in the Son of Man?” The man answered with humble longing, “Who is he, sir, that I may believe in him?” Jesus replied, “You have seen him, and it is he who is speaking to you.” The man fell down before Him and worshiped. In that moment, I felt a deep stirring in my own spirit. The man had moved from physical darkness to light, and now from spiritual seeking to faith—and I noticed that the moment the world cast him out for Jesus’ sake was the very moment Jesus came to find him. Being thrown out of the synagogue had landed him at the feet of the Son of God. His words afterward lingered with me: “For judgment I came into this world, that those who do not see may see, and those who see may become blind.” The Pharisees nearby heard and asked if they were blind too. Jesus answered, “If you were blind, you would have no guilt; but now that you say, ‘We see,’ your guilt remains.” I understood then that the one thing that could shut a man out of this salvation was the proud certainty that he needed no healing.", "Being with Jesus in those days felt like standing in the full glare of light—revealing, sometimes painful, yet full of hope. I saw how He brought sight to the blind in more ways than one, and how the same light exposed the blindness in proud hearts. My own eyes felt opened further, my loyalty to Him deepening even as the shadows around us in Jerusalem lengthened. I walked with Him through the courts and streets, storing these things carefully, knowing the path we were on was leading toward something far greater and more costly than I could yet fully grasp.", "We lingered in Jerusalem after the healing of the blind man, the city still alive with the remnants of the Feast of Tabernacles. The courts of the Temple echoed with discussions and debates, and the air carried the mingled scents of incense, dust, and the faint sweetness of palm branches left from the celebrations. Tension hung over everything like a gathering cloud. Jesus continued to teach in the colonnades, and I stayed near Him, my senses alert to every shift in the mood of the crowd.", "One day He spoke of Himself as the shepherd of the sheep. “Truly, truly, I say to you,” He began, His voice carrying clearly across the open space, “I am the door of the sheep. All who came before me are thieves and robbers, but the sheep did not listen to them. I am the good shepherd. The good shepherd lays down his life for the sheep.”", "I listened with my whole being, feeling the weight and tenderness of those words. The image of a shepherd who would die for his flock stirred something deep within me, and at last it began to draw all His talk of suffering into focus. He had spoken again and again of being killed; now He told us why. He was not being overtaken by His enemies; He was a shepherd laying down His life, on purpose, between the flock and the wolf. I had seen shepherds on the hills—rugged, watchful men who knew each sheep by name—but I had never heard of one who meant to die so the sheep might live. Jesus spoke as One who knew us that intimately and would not flee when danger came. Some in the crowd murmured that He was mad or had a demon, while others defended Him, saying, “These are not the words of one who is demon-possessed. Can a demon open the eyes of the blind?” The division was growing sharper, like a blade slowly cutting through cloth. My own heart felt the strain—loyalty to Him burning brighter even as the opposition hardened around us.", "Later, during the Feast of Dedication in winter, we were walking in the Temple, in Solomon’s Portico. The air was cooler now, and the light had a sharper quality as it fell across the stone columns. The people gathered around Him and asked directly, “How long will you keep us in suspense? If you are the Christ, tell us plainly.”", "Jesus looked at them with steady sorrow and said, “I told you, and you do not believe. The works that I do in my Father’s name bear witness about me… I and the Father are one.”", "The words fell with quiet power, yet they struck like lightning. I felt a holy thrill run through me, mingled with awe. He was claiming unity with the Father Himself—the Shepherd who would die for the sheep was no mere man hired to watch them, but God come down to do it. Some in the crowd picked up stones to throw at Him, their faces twisted with rage. Jesus asked them calmly, “I have shown you many good works from the Father; for which of them are you going to stone me?” They answered that it was for blasphemy, because He, being a man, was making Himself God. He responded with scripture and reminded them of His works, but they tried again to arrest Him. He slipped away from their hands, and we withdrew with Him across the Jordan to the place where John had first been baptizing.", "There, many came to Him and believed. I sat with the others in the quieter countryside, the sound of the river nearby a constant, soothing murmur. Being away from the pressure of Jerusalem brought a measure of relief, yet my spirit remained watchful. The things I had seen and heard were accumulating like stones in a wall—each one strengthening my conviction that this was the Messiah, the Son of God. Even as the shadows lengthened over His path, I felt a deepening peace in simply being near Him, walking where He walked, listening to every word as if my life depended on it. And in many ways, I knew it did.", "We stayed for some time in the region beyond the Jordan, in the place where John had first baptized. The land was quieter here, the river flowing steadily nearby with a low, constant voice that seemed to echo the baptizer’s old call to repentance. Fewer crowds pressed upon us, and the open air allowed space for deeper conversation. Jesus taught us privately in the evenings as we sat together, His voice calm and measured in the cooling twilight. I listened with an attentiveness that had grown sharper over the months, sensing that every word now carried extra weight, like provisions stored carefully before a long journey. It struck me that John had begun his work here pointing to the Lamb of God who takes away the sin of the world; now we had come full circle to the same riverbank, and that Lamb was about to do the very thing John had named.", "In those weeks, as we moved from village to village through the country beyond the Jordan and up toward Judea, He taught without ceasing, and I held His words as a man holds water in cupped hands, afraid to lose a drop. When the crowds pressed in clamoring for a sign, He grew firm. “This generation is an evil generation. It seeks a sign, but no sign will be given it except the sign of Jonah. For as Jonah became a sign to the people of Nineveh, so will the Son of Man be to this generation.” I did not catch His meaning then, but I would: Jonah three days in the belly of the deep and brought up alive was the one sign He would give—His own death and rising, the very thing He kept promising. He spoke of the men of Nineveh and the queen of the South rising in the judgment, because they had repented and listened at far less than now stood before them. Then, more gently, He gestured to a lamp burning on its stand. “No one lights a lamp and puts it in a cellar, but on a stand, that those who enter may see the light. The eye is the lamp of the body. When your eye is healthy, your whole body is full of light.” One of the Pharisees invited Him to dine, and was astonished that He did not first wash. Jesus answered him without flinching, naming their careful tithing of mint and rue while they neglected justice and the love of God, their hunger for the best seats and the greetings of the marketplaces, the burdens they laid on others’ shoulders that they would not touch with a finger. The lawyers bristled, and from that hour they watched Him closely, waiting to catch Him in something He might say.", "To us, His own, He turned with a quieter urgency. “Beware the leaven of the Pharisees, which is hypocrisy. Nothing is covered up that will not be revealed, and what you have whispered in private rooms will be proclaimed on the housetops.” He told us not to fear those who kill the body and after that can do nothing more, but to fear the One who has authority over body and soul together. Yet in the same breath He comforted us: not one sparrow is forgotten before God, and even the hairs of our heads are all numbered. “Everyone who acknowledges me before men, the Son of Man also will acknowledge before the angels of God.” And He promised that when we were dragged before rulers and authorities, the Holy Spirit would teach us in that very hour what we ought to say. I held those words close, not knowing then how soon I would need them, nor how badly I and the others would stumble before we learned to lean on them. There was comfort woven through the warning: the God who could weigh body and soul together was the same God who counted the sparrows and numbered my hairs, and a salvation held by a God like that could not slip out of His hands.", "A man called out from the crowd, “Teacher, tell my brother to divide the inheritance with me.” Jesus refused to be made a judge over their property. “Take care, and be on your guard against all covetousness, for one’s life does not consist in the abundance of his possessions.” Then He told of a rich man whose land produced so plentifully that he resolved to tear down his barns and build larger ones, saying to his soul, “You have ample goods laid up for many years; relax, eat, drink, be merry.” But God said to him, “Fool! This night your soul is required of you, and the things you have prepared, whose will they be?” So it is, He said, with the one who lays up treasure for himself and is not rich toward God. And turning to us, He bade us not be anxious about our lives, what we would eat or what we would put on. “Consider the ravens: they neither sow nor reap nor gather into barns, and yet God feeds them. Consider the lilies, how they grow. If God so clothes the grass of the field, how much more will He clothe you. Seek His kingdom, and these things will be added to you.” The plain birds wheeling overhead and the wildflowers leaning along the path became, in His mouth, sermons I could not unsee afterward. He was teaching us to live as children whose Father could be trusted for everything—which was only possible, I realized, for those whose true treasure was no longer anything that this night’s death could take away.", "He spoke often of readiness in those days. “Stay dressed for action and keep your lamps burning, like men waiting for their master to come home from the wedding feast, so that they may open the door at once when he knocks.” Blessed, He said, are the servants the master finds awake; but the servant who says in his heart, “My master is delayed,” and begins to beat the other servants and to eat and drink and get drunk, will be cut in pieces. To whom much is given, much will be required. He did not soften what His coming would cost. “I came to cast fire on the earth, and how I wish it were already kindled.” He spoke of a baptism He had still to undergo, and of how distressed He was until it was finished—and only much later did I understand He meant the flood of suffering that was rushing toward Him at Jerusalem. “Do you think I have come to give peace on earth? No, but rather division.” Households would be split, He warned, father against son and mother against daughter, for His sake. Then, turning to the crowds, He rebuked their dullness: they could see a cloud rising in the west and say a shower was coming, and feel the south wind and say there would be scorching heat, yet they could not interpret the present time. The warning settled over me like the chill that runs ahead of a storm.", "Some told Him of Galileans whose blood Pilate had mingled with their sacrifices, as if such suffering marked the men as worse sinners than the rest. Jesus refused the cruel arithmetic. “Do you think they were worse offenders than all the others? No, I tell you; but unless you repent, you will all likewise perish.” He recalled the eighteen on whom the tower in Siloam had fallen, and said the same of them. Then He told of a man who had a fig tree planted in his vineyard and came seeking fruit on it for three years and found none. “Cut it down,” he told the keeper of the vineyard; “why should it use up the ground?” But the keeper pleaded, “Sir, let it alone this year also, until I dig around it and put on manure. Then if it should bear fruit next year, well and good; but if not, you can cut it down.” I understood that He was speaking of a patience that was real but would not last forever—and I heard in the keeper’s plea an echo of Jesus Himself, standing between the barren tree and the axe, buying it more time with His own care. I searched my own life for fruit, sobered that the danger was not only the gross sinner’s but mine: not worse offenders, but all alike in need of turning home before the season ran out.", "On a Sabbath He taught in a synagogue where a woman had been bent over for eighteen years, unable to straighten herself at all. He called her to Him and said, “Woman, you are freed from your disability,” and laid His hands on her, and at once she stood up straight and began praising God. The ruler of the synagogue was indignant that He had healed on the Sabbath and told the people to come for healing on the other six days. But Jesus called him a hypocrite. “Does not each of you on the Sabbath untie his ox or his donkey and lead it away to water? And ought not this woman, a daughter of Abraham whom Satan has bound for eighteen long years, be loosed from this bond on the Sabbath day?” His adversaries were put to shame, and all the people rejoiced at the glorious things He did. It moved me that He called her a daughter of Abraham and named her bondage as Satan’s work; He had come to loose exactly such bonds, and would let no rule of men keep Him from setting a captive free. He likened the kingdom of God to a grain of mustard seed that a man sowed in his garden, which grew into a tree where the birds made their nests, and to leaven that a woman hid in three measures of flour until it was all leavened. Small, hidden beginnings, He seemed to say, with an end too large to measure—and I took heart that the rescue He was working, which looked so small and so opposed, would one day fill the whole earth.", "Someone asked Him whether those who are saved would be few. He answered not with a number but with a door. “Strive to enter through the narrow door, for many, I tell you, will seek to enter and will not be able.” He warned that some would stand outside knocking, saying, “We ate and drank in your presence, and you taught in our streets,” only to hear, “I do not know where you come from.” People would come from east and west, from north and south, and recline at table in the kingdom of God, while some who counted themselves first would be last. I felt the urgency of it: the door was narrow, and it was open now, but it would not always be. That same hour certain Pharisees came warning Him to leave, for Herod wanted to kill Him. Jesus was unmoved. “Go and tell that fox that I cast out demons and perform cures today and tomorrow, and the third day I finish my course.” Then His voice bent with grief over the city that lay ahead of us. “O Jerusalem, Jerusalem, the city that kills the prophets and stones those sent to it—how often would I have gathered your children together as a hen gathers her brood under her wings, and you were not willing.” I had never heard such longing braided so tightly with such sorrow. The image undid me—the Lord of glory as a mother bird stretching her wings over her young to take the blow herself—and I wondered whether that was not exactly how He meant to gather us at last, by spreading Himself over us on something like outstretched wings.", "He dined one Sabbath at the house of a ruler who belonged to the Pharisees, and they watched Him carefully. A man was there swollen with dropsy, and Jesus healed him and sent him away, then asked them which of them, if a son or an ox had fallen into a well on the Sabbath, would not at once pull him out. They could not answer Him. Noticing how the guests had chosen the places of honor, He counseled taking the lowest place instead, so the host might come and say, “Friend, move up higher,” for everyone who exalts himself will be humbled, and the one who humbles himself will be exalted. And He told the host to invite not friends and rich neighbors who could repay him, but the poor, the crippled, the lame, and the blind. Then He told of a man who gave a great banquet and invited many, but when the hour came each of them began to make excuses—a field just bought, five yoke of oxen to examine, a wife newly married. In anger the master sent his servant into the streets and lanes to bring in the poor and crippled and blind and lame, and then to the highways and hedges to compel people to come in, that his house might be filled. “For none of those men who were invited shall taste my banquet.” I felt the warning aimed squarely at those of us who assumed our places at His table were secure—and I saw the gospel hidden in it too: the feast of God would not go unfilled for lack of worthy guests, but would be crowded instead with exactly the poor and broken and overlooked who knew they had no claim, dragged in joyfully off the roads by a host determined to fill His house.", "Great crowds traveled with Him now, and He turned and gave them no easy welcome. “Whoever does not bear his own cross and come after me cannot be my disciple.” He asked which of them, intending to build a tower, would not first sit down and count the cost, whether he had enough to finish it, lest he lay a foundation and not be able to complete it, and all who saw it mock him. Or what king, marching out to meet another in war, does not first sit down and weigh whether with ten thousand he can meet the one coming against him with twenty thousand. “So therefore, any one of you who does not renounce all that he has cannot be my disciple. Salt is good, but if it has lost its taste, how shall its saltiness be restored?” The words thinned the crowd the way a hard climb thins a caravan, leaving behind those who only meant to travel a little way. I understood that He wanted followers who knew exactly what they were walking into—and the more I thought on it, the more I saw that He was asking nothing of us He was not Himself about to do in full, for He was the One walking ahead to bear the heaviest cross of all and renounce everything, even His life, for our sake.", "The tax collectors and sinners were all drawing near to hear Him, and the Pharisees and the scribes grumbled, “This man receives sinners and eats with them.” So He answered them with a question that was really a mirror. What man among them, having a hundred sheep and losing one, does not leave the ninety-nine in the open country and go after the one until he finds it, and then lay it on his shoulders rejoicing and call his friends together to celebrate? Or what woman, having ten silver coins and losing one, does not light a lamp and sweep the house and search diligently until she finds it, and then call her neighbors to rejoice with her? “Just so, I tell you, there is joy before the angels of God over one sinner who repents.” Their grumble had been an accusation; He turned it into the very glory of God. I had spent my whole life among people taught to keep their distance from such sinners, and here was the Shepherd telling me that heaven does not wait at a safe distance for the lost to find their own way back—it goes out after the one, carries it home on its own shoulders, and throws a feast when it arrives. The receiving of sinners they sneered at was the deepest joy in heaven.", "Then He told the story I have never been able to forget. A man had two sons, and the younger asked for his share of the estate, then gathered everything and journeyed into a far country, where he squandered it all in reckless living. When a severe famine arose and he had nothing, he hired himself out to feed pigs and longed to fill his belly with the pods they ate, and no one gave him anything. But he came to himself and said, “How many of my father’s hired servants have more than enough bread, while I perish here with hunger. I will arise and go to my father and say to him, Father, I have sinned against heaven and before you; I am no longer worthy to be called your son; treat me as one of your hired servants.” And while he was still a long way off, his father saw him and felt compassion and ran and fell on his neck and kissed him. Before the son could finish the confession he had rehearsed, the father called for the best robe, a ring for his hand, sandals for his feet, and the fattened calf, “for this my son was dead, and is alive again; he was lost, and is found.” But the older son, coming in from the field and hearing music and dancing, was angry and would not go in. His father came out and entreated him, and the son complained that he had served all these years and never disobeyed, yet was never given so much as a young goat to celebrate with his friends. The father’s answer hung in the cooling evening air. “Son, you are always with me, and all that is mine is yours. It was fitting to celebrate and be glad, for this your brother was dead, and is alive; he was lost, and is found.”", "I looked at the tax collectors leaning in close, and at the scribes standing stiff at the edge of the crowd, and I understood there were two sons in the story for a reason. But it was the father I could not get past. I had imagined that a sinner crawling home should expect, at best, a cold acceptance and a long probation. Instead the father saw him while he was still far off—which meant he had been watching the road—and ran, an old man hitching up his robe and running like a fool to reach a ragged, pig-stinking boy and fall on his neck before a word of confession was even out. Here at last was the whole of what I had been slowly learning, gathered into one running figure: this is what God is like, and this is what He had come down to do—not to wait for the worthy, but to run toward the wretched, cut their rehearsed groveling short, and clothe them as sons. And I began to suspect, watching the father go out to plead even with the bitter older son, that there was no kind of lostness, neither the prodigal’s nor the prig’s, that this love would not leave the house to chase.", "He told us, His own disciples, of a manager accused of wasting his master’s goods, who, about to be dismissed, shrewdly reduced the debts of his master’s debtors so they would receive him into their houses afterward. The master commended the dishonest manager for his shrewdness, and Jesus drew the lesson out sharply: “Make friends for yourselves by means of unrighteous wealth, so that when it fails they may receive you into the eternal dwellings.” The one faithful in very little is faithful also in much, and the one dishonest in very little is dishonest in much. “No servant can serve two masters. You cannot serve God and money.” The Pharisees, who loved money, heard all this and ridiculed Him; but He told them that what is exalted among men is an abomination in the sight of God. The Law and the Prophets, He said, were until John; since then the good news of the kingdom of God is preached, and everyone forces his way into it, yet it is easier for heaven and earth to pass away than for one dot of the Law to fail. I sat with that strange pairing—the kingdom flung open as good news to be pressed into, and not one stroke of the Law allowed to fall—and could not yet see how both could stand at once. Only later would I learn that they met in Him: that He would satisfy every dot of the Law in our place so that the door could be thrown open to sinners who could never satisfy it themselves.", "There was a rich man, He said, clothed in purple and fine linen, who feasted sumptuously every day, and at his gate was laid a poor man named Lazarus, covered with sores, who longed to be fed with what fell from the rich man’s table, while even the dogs came and licked his sores. The poor man died and was carried by the angels to Abraham’s side; the rich man also died and was buried, and in torment he lifted up his eyes and saw Abraham far off and Lazarus beside him. He begged that Lazarus might dip the tip of his finger in water and cool his tongue, but Abraham answered that a great chasm had been fixed between them which none could cross, and that in his lifetime the rich man had received his good things while Lazarus received only evil. Then the rich man pleaded that Lazarus be sent to warn his five brothers. “They have Moses and the Prophets,” Abraham said; “let them hear them.” “No, father Abraham, but if someone goes to them from the dead, they will repent.” And the answer came back: “If they do not hear Moses and the Prophets, neither will they be convinced if someone should rise from the dead.” The story sobered me with how final the chasm became once death had fixed it, and how the time to cross it was now, on this side, while there was still a voice to heed. I did not know then how soon a man named Lazarus would in fact rise, nor how stubbornly the hard of heart would still refuse to believe—proof, in the end, that no wonder can save a heart that will not hear.", "He warned us that temptations to sin are sure to come, but woe to the one through whom they come; it would be better to have a millstone hung around the neck and be cast into the sea than to cause one of these little ones to stumble. “Pay attention to yourselves. If your brother sins, rebuke him, and if he repents, forgive him; and if he sins against you seven times in the day, and turns to you seven times saying I repent, you must forgive him.” The apostles, staggered, said to the Lord, “Increase our faith.” He told them that faith like a grain of mustard seed could uproot a mulberry tree and plant it in the sea. Then He sobered us with a picture of plain service: when a servant has plowed or kept sheep and comes in from the field, his master does not thank him but expects to be served first; so we, when we have done all that we were commanded, should say, “We are unworthy servants; we have only done what was our duty.” It pressed the swelling pride out of me, that image of dusty, ordinary obedience that expects no applause. Even my best obedience earned God nothing; whatever I would ever receive from Him would be gift, never wage—and that, I was learning, was a relief and not a loss.", "As we passed along between Samaria and Galilee, ten lepers met Him, standing at a distance as the law required, and lifted their voices together: “Jesus, Master, have mercy on us.” He looked at them and only said, “Go and show yourselves to the priests.” And as they went, they were cleansed—I watched the wonder break across their faces as the disease loosed its grip on their skin right there on the road. But just one of them, when he saw that he was healed, turned back, praising God with a loud voice, and fell on his face at Jesus’ feet, giving Him thanks; and he was a Samaritan. Jesus looked around almost in sorrow. “Were not ten cleansed? Where are the nine? Was no one found to return and give praise to God except this foreigner?” Then to the man at His feet He said, “Rise and go your way; your faith has made you well.” All ten were cleansed, but only the one who came back was told his faith had saved him—and again it was the outsider. I began to see there was a healing of the skin that anyone might receive and walk away from, and a deeper saving that came only to those who turned back to the Healer Himself in faith and thanks. The gratitude of the foreigner, and the silence of the nine who never returned, stayed with me long after the man had gone on his way.", "When the Pharisees asked Him when the kingdom of God would come, He told them it would not come with signs to be observed, for the kingdom of God was already in their midst. But to us He spoke of the days of the Son of Man, which would be like the days of Noah and of Lot—people eating and drinking, marrying, buying and selling, planting and building—until the day judgment fell suddenly upon them. “Remember Lot’s wife,” He said. “Whoever seeks to preserve his life will lose it, and whoever loses his life will keep it.” To teach us to pray always and not lose heart, He told of a widow who kept coming to a judge who neither feared God nor respected man, until he granted her justice simply to be rid of her; and He asked how much more God will give justice to His elect who cry to Him day and night. And for some who trusted in their own righteousness and looked down on everyone else, He told of two men who went up to the temple to pray—a Pharisee who thanked God that he was not like other men, fasting and tithing, and a tax collector who stood far off and would not so much as lift his eyes, but beat his breast and said, “God, be merciful to me, a sinner.” “I tell you, this man went down to his house justified rather than the other. For everyone who exalts himself will be humbled, but the one who humbles himself will be exalted.” That word, justified, lodged in me. The man who brought God a ledger of his goodness went home with nothing; the man who brought only his sin and begged for mercy went home set right with God. I had spent years assuming heaven was won by the first kind of prayer, and Jesus had just told me it was answered only for the second.", "When we crossed at last into the region of Judea, beyond the Jordan, crowds gathered to Him again, and the Pharisees came testing Him, asking whether it was lawful for a man to divorce his wife for any cause. He pointed them back past Moses to the beginning, to the Maker who from the start made them male and female, so that a man leaves father and mother and holds fast to his wife, and the two become one flesh. “What therefore God has joined together, let not man separate.” When they pressed Him about Moses’ certificate of divorce, He said Moses had allowed it because of their hardness of heart, but from the beginning it was not so. He spoke too of how jealously God watches over the little ones, repeating that whoever causes one of them to sin would be better off drowned in the depth of the sea, that their angels always behold the face of His Father, and that it is not the will of the Father that one of these little ones should perish. The weight He gave to the small and the overlooked rearranged something in the way I had been taught to see the world. If it was not the Father’s will that even one little one should perish, then the whole effort He was bending toward Jerusalem was of a piece with that will—a Shepherd going after every last sheep so that none would be lost.", "People began bringing their little children to Him so that He might touch and bless them, and we, thinking to guard His time and strength, rebuked them. Jesus was indignant with us. “Let the little children come to me, and do not hinder them, for to such belongs the kingdom of God. Truly, I say to you, whoever does not receive the kingdom of God like a child shall not enter it.” Then He took them up in His arms, laid His hands on them, and blessed them one by one. I watched Him crouch down in the dust to a child’s small height, unhurried, attentive, and I was ashamed of how quickly I had counted them an interruption. The kingdom, it seemed, belonged most surely to those who could bring nothing in their hands but trust—who received it as a gift, the way a child receives, because they had no power to earn it and no thought of trying.", "As He was setting out on the road, a young man ran up and knelt before Him. “Good Teacher, what must I do to inherit eternal life?” Jesus told him he knew the commandments, and the man said he had kept them all from his youth. Then Jesus, looking at him, loved him, and said, “You lack one thing: go, sell all that you have and give to the poor, and you will have treasure in heaven; and come, follow me.” The man’s face fell, and he went away sorrowful, for he had great possessions. I have never forgotten that Jesus loved him in the very moment of naming the one thing that held him back; the love and the hard word were not opposites. Jesus looked around at us and said, “How difficult it will be for those who have wealth to enter the kingdom of God. It is easier for a camel to go through the eye of a needle than for a rich man to enter the kingdom of God.” We were astonished and asked who then could possibly be saved, and He answered, “What is impossible with man is possible with God.” The words settled something in me that had been unsettled since the beginning: no one—not the rich young man with his clean record, not I with all I had left behind—could thread himself through that needle by his own effort. Salvation was impossible for man and possible only for God, which meant it had to be, from first to last, a thing God did. Peter began to say that we had left everything to follow Him, and Jesus promised that no one who has left house or family or lands for His sake and the gospel’s will fail to receive a hundredfold now, along with persecutions, and in the age to come eternal life. “But many who are first will be last, and the last first.”", "To explain that last saying He told of a master of a house who went out early in the morning to hire laborers for his vineyard, agreeing with them on a denarius for the day. He went out again at the third hour, and the sixth, and the ninth, and even at the eleventh hour, hiring those he still found standing idle. When evening came, he told his foreman to pay them all, beginning with the last hired, and each one received a denarius. Those hired first grumbled when they were paid the same, complaining that they had borne the burden of the day and the scorching heat. But the master answered one of them, “Friend, I am doing you no wrong. Did you not agree with me for a denarius? Take what belongs to you and go. Am I not allowed to do what I choose with what is my own? Or do you begrudge my generosity?” “So the last will be first, and the first last.” I felt the grumbling laborer’s complaint rise in my own chest, recognized in it the very voice of the older brother from the other story, and fell silent. The kingdom did not pay wages by the hour; it gave the same lavish gift to the latecomer and the lifelong laborer alike, because it ran on the master’s generosity, not on what we had earned—and the only thing that could spoil it was the pride that wanted to be paid what it deserved instead of given what it could never deserve.", "As we went up the road toward Jerusalem, Jesus walked on ahead of us, and a kind of dread settled over those who followed. He took the Twelve aside and told them plainly what awaited Him: He would be delivered over to the chief priests and the scribes, condemned to death, and handed to the Gentiles to be mocked and flogged and crucified, and on the third day He would be raised. We did not grasp it; the meaning was hidden from us. And almost in the same breath, the mother of James and John came with her sons and asked that they might sit, one at His right hand and one at His left, in His kingdom. When the other ten heard it, they were indignant. So Jesus called us all together. “You know that the rulers of the Gentiles lord it over them, and their great ones exercise authority over them. It shall not be so among you. Whoever would be great among you must be your servant, and whoever would be first among you must be your slave, even as the Son of Man came not to be served but to serve, and to give His life as a ransom for many.” That word—ransom—broke over me like cold water and stayed with me longer than any other He spoke on that road. A ransom is the price paid to buy back a captive, to set a slave free. He was telling us, as plainly as He ever had, why He was walking toward death: not as a defeat that would befall Him, but as a price He had come to pay, His own life laid down in exchange for the many who could never buy their own freedom. That He could speak of His death and of our squabbling for greatness in a single breath unsettled and humbled me at once; we were grasping for thrones while He was on His way to purchase us with His blood.", "We came to Jericho, and as He was leaving the city a great crowd went with Him. A blind beggar named Bartimaeus sat by the roadside, and when he heard that Jesus of Nazareth was passing by, he began to cry out, “Jesus, Son of David, have mercy on me!” Many rebuked him and told him to be silent, but he cried out all the louder, “Son of David, have mercy on me!” Jesus stopped and said, “Call him.” They told the man to take heart, that Jesus was calling him, and he threw off his cloak and sprang up and came. “What do you want me to do for you?” Jesus asked. “Rabbi, let me recover my sight.” And Jesus said, “Go your way; your faith has made you well.” At once he could see, and he followed Jesus on the road, glorifying God, and all the people who saw it gave praise as well. I had walked past a hundred beggars in my life without slowing; Jesus stopped the whole crowd for the one man no one else would hear. His was the right cry, I thought—not a claim of worth but a plea for mercy, flung out louder the more they tried to silence it—and it was answered. That was how a man came to Him: shouting for mercy over every voice that said to keep quiet.", "In Jericho there was a man named Zacchaeus, a chief tax collector who was rich, and he wanted to see who Jesus was but could not on account of the crowd, for he was small in stature. So he ran on ahead and climbed up into a sycamore tree to see Him, since Jesus was about to pass that way. When He came to the place, Jesus looked up and said, “Zacchaeus, hurry and come down, for I must stay at your house today.” He came down at once and received Him joyfully, while the crowd grumbled that Jesus had gone in to be the guest of a sinner. But Zacchaeus stood and said to the Lord, “Behold, Lord, the half of my goods I give to the poor, and if I have defrauded anyone of anything, I restore it fourfold.” And Jesus said to him, “Today salvation has come to this house, since he also is a son of Abraham. For the Son of Man came to seek and to save the lost.” I thought of Levi rising from his tax booth long before, and saw plainly that Jesus had never once stopped going after the very people the rest of us had quietly written off. Here at last, in one sentence, was the thing my whole journey with Him had been spelling out from the Jordan onward: the Son of Man came to seek and to save the lost. Not to wait to be sought, but to seek; not to inspect the worthy, but to save the lost. Zacchaeus’ sudden open-handedness was not the price of his salvation but the first overflow of it—the prodigal’s robe, the forgiven woman’s tears, all over again, in a small rich man halfway down a tree.", "Because we were now near Jerusalem and many supposed the kingdom of God was about to appear at once, He told us of a nobleman who went into a far country to receive for himself a kingdom and then return, leaving ten servants ten minas with the charge, “Engage in business until I come.” But his citizens hated him and sent word after him that they would not have this man reign over them. When he returned, having received the kingdom, he summoned the servants: the one who had made ten minas more was set over ten cities, and the one who made five over five; but the servant who had hidden his mina away in a cloth, excusing himself by calling his master a severe man, had even that one taken from him and given to the servant who had ten. “To everyone who has, more will be given; but from the one who has not, even what he has will be taken away.” And as for the citizens who would not have him reign, they were to be brought and judged before him. I heard in it a gentle correction of our eagerness—the kingdom was not going to burst into full glory the moment we reached the city; the King would first go away, and we would be left to trade faithfully on what He had entrusted until He returned. The parable fell heavily on all of us, so near the city now, and we went on, the journey narrowing toward Jerusalem, every step weighted with a hope and a fear I could no longer tell apart.", "Then word came from Bethany, a village just outside Jerusalem. A messenger arrived, breathless and troubled, with news from Mary and Martha: “Lord, he whom you love is ill.” Lazarus. Our friend. I remembered him well—quiet, generous, a man whose home had often been a place of welcome. Jesus listened without immediate alarm. “This illness does not lead to death,” He said. “It is for the glory of God, so that the Son of God may be glorified through it.” We stayed two days longer where we were. I felt the tension among the disciples. Some wondered why we did not hurry. I myself wrestled with it—love and delay seemed at odds. Yet in His presence I was learning that His timing was never late, only purposeful, and that He sometimes let a darkness deepen precisely so that the deliverance, when it came, would leave no doubt about who had wrought it.", "Finally He said, “Let us go to Judea again.” The others hesitated, reminding Him of the stones the Jews had recently taken up against Him. Jesus answered, “Are there not twelve hours in the day? If anyone walks in the day, he does not stumble, because he sees the light of this world. But if anyone walks in the night, he stumbles, because the light is not in him.” Then plainly: “Our friend Lazarus has fallen asleep. But I go to awaken him.” When we still did not fully understand, He said it clearly: “Lazarus has died, and for your sake I am glad that I was not there, so that you may believe. But let us go to him.” That He would walk knowingly back toward the men who wanted to kill Him, and do it to wake a dead friend, told me again that His own safety was never the thing He was guarding; He was spending Himself, step by step, on the lives of others.", "We set out, the road climbing back toward Jerusalem. My heart was heavy with sorrow for our friends and with a growing unease about returning to danger. Yet walking beside Jesus steadied me. Thomas voiced what many of us felt when he said, “Let us also go, that we may die with him.” There was courage in those words, even if mixed with resignation. I felt it too—a willingness to follow even into the shadow.", "When we arrived near Bethany, Lazarus had already been in the tomb four days. Martha came out to meet us first, her face drawn with grief but her faith still reaching. “Lord, if you had been here, my brother would not have died. But even now I know that whatever you ask from God, God will give you.” Jesus answered her with words that still echo in my memory: “I am the resurrection and the life. Whoever believes in me, though he die, yet shall he live, and everyone who lives and believes in me shall never die. Do you believe this?” Martha’s reply came with quiet strength: “Yes, Lord; I believe that you are the Christ, the Son of God, who is coming into the world.” He did not say He would bring resurrection or fetch life from somewhere else; He said He was the resurrection and the life. The thing every grieving heart longed for was not a power He wielded but a Person standing in front of Martha in the dust of the road—and I understood that to have Him was already to have life that death could not finally hold.", "Mary soon followed, falling at His feet weeping. The sound of her sobs, joined by the mourning of friends who had come from Jerusalem, stirred deep compassion in Jesus. I saw His face change—troubled, moved with sorrow. He asked where they had laid him, and they led us to the tomb, a cave with a stone against the entrance. Jesus wept. The sight of His tears moved me profoundly. The One who knew He was about to raise Lazarus still wept at the grave—the Lord of life refusing to stand aloof from our grief, weeping with us before He delivered us. It was not distant power, but shared sorrow; He came down into the very ache of death before He undid it. Some around us whispered, “See how He loved him!”", "Then came the moment I will never forget. Jesus told them to take away the stone. Martha hesitated, mentioning the odor after four days. But He reminded her gently of belief. When the stone was rolled aside, Jesus lifted His eyes to heaven and prayed: “Father, I thank you that you have heard me. I knew that you always hear me, but I said this on account of the people standing around, that they may believe that you sent me.” Then, with a loud voice that carried the full weight of authority, He cried out, “Lazarus, come out!”", "The silence that followed was absolute. Then movement—shuffling, the sound of linen wrappings shifting. Lazarus emerged from the darkness of the tomb, bound hand and foot with strips of cloth, his face wrapped in a cloth. He was alive. Whole. The crowd gasped, some falling back in fear, others crying out in joy. Jesus said quietly, “Unbind him, and let him go.”", "I stood transfixed, my legs weak beneath me. Tears streamed down my face unashamedly. Death had yielded once more, but this time it felt intensely personal. Our friend, cold in the tomb four days, now stood blinking in the sunlight. In that moment the glory Jesus had spoken of shone out, and the words at the roadside became flesh before my eyes: He was the resurrection and the life, and He had just proved it on a man four days dead. If His voice could call Lazarus out of a sealed tomb, then no grave—not even the one I sensed waiting for Him in Jerusalem—could be the end of the story. Yet even in the joy I sensed the gathering storm. This sign would not be ignored by those who opposed Him; the very raising of a dead man would, I would soon learn, hasten His own death. I remained close to Jesus, more committed than ever, knowing that whatever came next, I had seen enough to trust Him completely.", "We stayed in Bethany for a short time after Lazarus emerged from the tomb, the village buzzing with wonder and disbelief. People came from Jerusalem and the surrounding towns to see both Jesus and Lazarus, the man who had been dead four days and now sat among us, eating and speaking in a voice still tinged with awe. I watched Lazarus closely—his movements careful at first, as though rediscovering his own body, his eyes often turning toward Jesus with quiet gratitude. The joy in that house was deep, yet I felt an undercurrent of danger. Word of the miracle spread like fire through dry grass, and I sensed the growing threat it posed; the clearest proof of His power to give life was becoming the very reason men resolved to take His.", "Not many days later, reports reached us that the chief priests and Pharisees had gathered the Sanhedrin. They were troubled. “What are we to do?” they asked one another. “This man performs many signs. If we let Him go on like this, everyone will believe in Him, and the Romans will come and take away both our place and our nation.” Caiaphas, the high priest that year, spoke with cold calculation: “It is better for you that one man should die for the people, not that the whole nation should perish.” I felt a chill when I heard these words repeated by those who had been at the council. They did not understand that they were prophesying. He meant only to sacrifice an innocent man to protect their power; yet the words were truer than he knew, for Jesus would indeed die for the people—one man in the place of the many—and not for the nation only, but to gather into one the children of God scattered abroad. The deepest mystery of the whole plan had just been spoken aloud by the man who least believed it. From that day on, they plotted to put Him to death. Jesus therefore no longer walked openly among the Jews but went to the region near the wilderness, to a town called Ephraim, and stayed there with the disciples.", "The days in Ephraim were quieter, the hills rugged and the air crisp. We rested, and Jesus continued teaching us in small, intimate moments. Yet even there, my sleep was sometimes restless. I would lie awake thinking of Lazarus walking out of the tomb, the grave clothes still wrapped around him, and then of the words spoken in the Sanhedrin. The joy and the shadow walked side by side—life pulled out of one grave, and a death being plotted for the One who had pulled it out.", "When the Passover was at hand, Jesus left Ephraim and set His face once more toward Jerusalem. Many from the region went up to the city to purify themselves before the feast, and they looked for Him, wondering if He would come. The chief priests and Pharisees had given orders that anyone who knew where He was should report it, so that they might seize Him. As we drew near to Bethany again, six days before the Passover, a dinner was given in Jesus’ honor at the home of Simon the leper. Lazarus was there, reclining at the table with Him—a living testimony in the flesh. Martha served, as was her way. Then Mary took a pound of very costly ointment of pure nard, anointed Jesus’ feet, and wiped them with her hair. The fragrance filled the entire house—rich, heavy, and sweet.", "I watched from across the room as the scent enveloped us. Judas Iscariot, one of the twelve, objected: “Why was this ointment not sold for three hundred denarii and given to the poor?” His words sounded righteous, but I sensed something false beneath them. Jesus defended Mary gently but firmly: “Leave her alone. She has kept this for the day of my burial. For the poor you always have with you, but you do not always have me.”", "The words struck me deeply. Burial. He was speaking of His own death again, and this time the reality settled heavier in my chest. Mary had understood something the rest of us had not yet fully grasped—she had heard Him say He would die, and believed it, and so she lavished her treasure on Him now while she still could, anointing His body for the grave before the grave had even claimed it. Her act of extravagant love, poured out without calculation, humbled me. Nothing given to Him was ever truly wasted, however the careful world counted it; the only waste was holding back. I felt my own devotion tested—would I give so freely, so recklessly, to the One who was about to give His all for me?", "The next day, the great crowd that had come to the feast heard that Jesus was coming to Jerusalem. They took branches of palm trees and went out to meet Him, crying out, “Hosanna! Blessed is He who comes in the name of the Lord, even the King of Israel!” Jesus found a young donkey and sat on it, just as it was written: “Fear not, daughter of Zion; behold, your king is coming, sitting on a donkey’s colt.” He came as a king, but not the kind the waving crowds wanted—no warhorse, no sword, only a borrowed colt; a King whose throne, I would soon see, was to be a cross, and whose conquest was to be His own death.", "I walked near Him as we descended the Mount of Olives, the crowds swelling on every side. Their shouts rose like thunder, palm branches waving in the air, cloaks spread on the road before Him. My heart lifted with the praise, yet a sorrow lingered beneath it. I remembered His words about rejection and death. Many in the crowd had seen the raising of Lazarus and believed, but I knew not all hearts were true. As we approached the city, Jesus stopped and wept over Jerusalem. “Would that you, even you, had known on this day the things that make for peace! But now they are hidden from your eyes.” His tears on that day of triumph broke something open in me. While the city shouted His welcome, He wept for it, because He saw that the very peace it was singing about—the peace He had come to make—was being held out and refused. He had come to bring exactly what made for peace, at the cost of His own life, to a city that would spend that week putting Him to death.", "We entered the city, the Temple courts filled with excitement and confusion. Some Pharisees in the crowd said to Him, “Teacher, rebuke your disciples.” Jesus replied, “I tell you, if these were silent, the very stones would cry out.”", "That evening, as we returned to Bethany, the weight of all I had seen pressed upon me. The palms, the shouts, the tears, the fragrant ointment still lingering in my memory—everything pointed toward a climax I could not fully see, though I was beginning to feel its shape: that the King had come to His city to be crowned, and that the crowning would look like nothing any of us expected. I walked close to Jesus, my spirit quiet and attentive, more determined than ever to remain with Him through whatever darkness or glory lay ahead.", "We returned to the city the next morning, the echoes of yesterday’s Hosannas still ringing faintly in my ears. The streets felt charged, as though the whole of Jerusalem held its breath. Jesus walked ahead of us toward the Temple with that same steady purpose I had come to recognize so well. As we entered the outer court once more, the familiar chaos met us—tables of money changers, merchants selling doves and oxen, the loud clink of coins and the lowing of animals filling the air.", "I saw Jesus’ face change. A righteous fire kindled in His eyes, yet it was controlled, holy. He made a whip of cords as He had done years earlier, and began driving out the sellers. Tables overturned with heavy crashes, coins scattered across the stone pavement like rain. Animals bolted in confusion. His voice rang out clear and strong above the noise: “It is written, ‘My house shall be called a house of prayer,’ but you have made it a den of robbers!” I had stood in that court at the very beginning, when He first overturned these tables, and I understood it differently now. He was clearing His Father’s house of everything that stood between sinners and God—and within days He would offer His own body as the new and true temple, the one place where the whole world, Jew and Gentile alike, could finally come near to God in prayer.", "I stood a short distance away, my heart pounding. The authority in His voice sent a tremor through me. Merchants scrambled to gather their goods, their faces flushed with anger and fear. The religious leaders watched from the edges, their expressions dark. Children in the court cried out, “Hosanna to the Son of David!” and the chief priests and scribes grew indignant. Jesus healed the blind and lame who came to Him there, and their joyful voices rose like incense. But the leaders’ opposition only deepened. I felt the growing divide like a crack widening in the ground beneath us. Being near Him in that moment filled me with both awe and unease—this was the same zeal I had witnessed at the first Passover, now burning brighter as the hour approached, as if He were cleansing the house one last time before offering Himself within it.", "The following morning, as we left Bethany, Jesus was hungry. He approached a fig tree in full leaf along the path, but when He found no fruit on it, He said, “May no one ever eat fruit from you again.” The words were spoken quietly, yet with power. I noted it, but did not fully understand until later, when I saw the tree withered to its roots and remembered His parable of the fruitless fig tree spared one more year. A nation, like that tree, could be full of the leaves of religion and bear nothing for God—and the season for fruit would not last forever.", "In the Temple courts that day, the chief priests, scribes, and elders confronted Him directly. “By what authority are you doing these things,” they demanded, “or who gave you this authority?” Jesus answered with a question of His own: “Was the baptism of John from heaven or from man? Answer me.” They argued among themselves, trapped by their own fear of the people. Finally they said, “We do not know.” Jesus replied, “Neither will I tell you by what authority I do these things.”", "He then began teaching in parables. One struck me deeply—the story of the vineyard owner who planted a vineyard, leased it to tenants, and sent servants to collect fruit. The tenants beat and killed the servants, and finally killed the beloved son when he was sent. “What will the owner of the vineyard do?” Jesus asked. “He will come and destroy the tenants and give the vineyard to others.” I felt the weight of it, and a cold understanding crept over me: He was telling them, to their faces, exactly what they were about to do. He was the beloved son, sent last of all to a vineyard that had murdered every messenger before Him, and He knew they would kill Him too. Yet He spoke of the stone the builders rejected becoming the cornerstone, as if even His murder were folded into a plan that would build something the builders never imagined. The religious leaders knew He spoke of them. Their faces hardened with anger, yet they feared the crowd and did not seize Him.", "They tried again later, sending some Pharisees and Herodians to trap Him with a question about taxes. “Is it lawful to pay taxes to Caesar, or not?” Jesus asked for a denarius, looked at the image, and answered, “Render to Caesar the things that are Caesar’s, and to God the things that are God’s.” The wisdom and simplicity of His reply left them marveling and silent. I felt a quiet pride in Him, mixed with growing sorrow. They were not seeking truth; they were hunting for any reason to accuse—men circling the Lamb, looking for the pretext that would let them take Him to slaughter.", "Later that day, Sadducees came with a question about the resurrection, presenting a complicated case of a woman married to seven brothers. Jesus answered with calm authority, quoting Scripture: “He is not God of the dead, but of the living.” The crowd was astonished at His teaching. A scribe then asked which commandment was greatest. Jesus replied, “The Lord our God is one Lord. And you shall love the Lord your God with all your heart and with all your soul and with all your mind and with all your strength. The second is this: You shall love your neighbor as yourself. There is no other commandment greater than these.” The scribe agreed, and Jesus told him he was not far from the kingdom of God. For a moment, truth seemed to touch even some of His opponents. I marked the two commandments and felt their full weight—a whole-hearted love for God and neighbor that I had never once managed for a single day. Listening, I understood more sharply why I needed Him: the law named the love I owed and could not pay, and He was walking toward Jerusalem to pay it in my place.", "As evening approached, Jesus sat opposite the treasury and watched people putting money in. Rich people gave large sums out of their abundance, but a poor widow came and put in two small copper coins. “Truly, I say to you,” He told us, “this poor widow has put in more than all of them. For they all contributed out of their abundance, but she out of her poverty has put in everything she had, all she had to live on.”", "I felt the words pierce me. True giving, true worship, came from the heart’s deepest place. She had given not from her surplus but everything she had to live on—and it struck me, even then, that this was the very thing Jesus Himself was days from doing: not a portion offered out of abundance, but His whole life poured out down to the last. In the fading light of the Temple, with the sounds of the city beginning to quiet around us, I sensed the days of public teaching were drawing to a close. My spirit grew more solemn, holding tightly to every moment with Him, knowing the path we walked was leading toward a final, unavoidable hour.", "We left the Temple courts as the sun dipped lower, casting long shadows across the white stones. I walked close behind Jesus, the image of that poor widow still vivid in my mind—her small, trembling hand dropping the two copper coins into the treasury with such quiet finality. Her face had held no bitterness, only simple devotion. Jesus’ words about her gift lingered in my chest like a warm coal. Everything she had. In that moment, I felt the gap between my own cautious offerings and the kind of wholehearted surrender she showed—the same surrender I was about to watch the Master make on a scale I could not yet imagine.", "As we made our way out of the city and up the Mount of Olives, one of the disciples remarked on the grandeur of the Temple buildings—the massive stones, the gleaming gold, the towering architecture that spoke of permanence and glory. Jesus stopped and turned toward the city. His voice was quiet but carried a heavy solemnity: “Do you see these great buildings? There will not be left here one stone upon another that will not be thrown down.”", "We sat down together on the slope overlooking the Temple, the evening light turning the city gold and orange. Peter, James, John, and I gathered closer, our hearts uneasy. “Tell us,” we asked, “when will these things be, and what will be the sign when all these things are about to be accomplished?”", "Jesus began to teach us, and I listened with every part of me, feeling the weight of His words press down like the gathering dusk. He spoke of wars and rumors of wars, nation rising against nation, earthquakes and famines. “But the end is not yet,” He said. He warned of persecution, of being delivered up to councils and beaten in synagogues, of standing before governors and kings for His sake. “And the gospel must first be proclaimed to all nations.” That single line lifted my eyes above the coming troubles: whatever was about to happen to Him in Jerusalem, it would not bury the good news but launch it, until word of this Savior reached every nation on earth. My stomach tightened as He described brother betraying brother, children rising against parents, hatred because of His name. Yet He promised, “The one who endures to the end will be saved.”", "He spoke of the abomination of desolation, of those in Judea fleeing to the mountains, of tribulation so great that if the days had not been cut short, no human being would be saved. I felt a holy fear settle over me as I looked out over Jerusalem, the city that had just welcomed Him with palms now appearing so fragile in the fading light. Then His voice softened with promise: “And then they will see the Son of Man coming in clouds with great power and glory. And then He will send out the angels and gather His elect from the four winds, from the ends of the earth to the ends of heaven.” The One soon to be seized and killed would come again in glory to gather His own from every corner of the earth—and I understood that the rescue He was about to accomplish would not be undone by the grave, but would reach its harvest on that day.", "I sat in silence as the stars began to appear overhead, my hands resting on my knees. The contrast between the beauty of the night and the coming trials stirred a deep trembling inside me. I felt small, inadequate, yet strangely held. He spoke of the fig tree and the signs of summer, urging us to stay awake, to watch and pray. He told of servants entrusted with their master’s goods, of wise and foolish virgins waiting for the bridegroom, of a day when the Son of Man would separate the sheep from the goats and welcome the blessed of His Father into the kingdom prepared from the foundation of the world. “Heaven and earth will pass away,” He said, looking at each of us, “but my words will not pass away.”", "As the night deepened, we returned to Bethany. The road was quiet now, our footsteps the only sound. My mind turned slowly over everything He had said, storing it like precious seed in fertile but troubled soil. The days of teaching in the Temple were ending. Something greater and more terrible was drawing near, and I knew I would need every word, every memory of His presence, to endure it. Yet walking beside Him, even in the gathering dark, I felt a quiet certainty: wherever this path led, I would not leave Him.", "We returned quietly to Bethany that night, the path lit only by the moon and stars. The weight of Jesus’ words on the Mount of Olives pressed heavily upon me as I walked—wars, tribulations, the Temple stones thrown down, the Son of Man coming in glory. It all felt both distant and frighteningly near. My legs moved almost automatically while my mind turned slowly over everything, like a man trying to hold water in his hands. I felt small and unprepared, yet strangely anchored by His calm presence ahead of me on the road, the One who spoke of the end of all things as steadily as a shepherd describing the way home.", "The next day, two days before Passover, we gathered again in the house of Simon the leper. The familiar scent of roasted lamb and herbs filled the air as preparations for the feast began. While we reclined at the table, Mary once more drew near to Jesus, the memory of her earlier costly gift still lingering over the room. The atmosphere was thick with unspoken tension. I could sense the shadow lengthening over us all—the feast of the Passover lamb drawing near, and the true Lamb reclining among us.", "That same evening, Judas Iscariot slipped away. I noticed his absence but thought little of it at first—he often handled our money. Only later did I learn he had gone to the chief priests and offered to betray Jesus for thirty pieces of silver. The knowledge came like a slow knife. One of our own. One who had walked with us, seen the miracles, heard the teachings. It made the coming suffering feel more real, more personal—and it taught me a hard thing: a man could stand for years in the very presence of the Savior and still sell Him for silver, for nearness was never the same as faith.", "On the first day of Unleavened Bread, when the Passover lamb was to be sacrificed, Jesus sent Peter and John ahead into the city. “Go into the city,” He told them, “and a man carrying a jar of water will meet you. Follow him, and wherever he enters, say to the master of the house, ‘The Teacher says, Where is my guest room, where I may eat the Passover with my disciples?’ He will show you a large upper room furnished and ready. Prepare there for us.” They went and found everything exactly as He had said. I marveled again at His complete knowledge of what was to come; the One who could order the smallest detail of the day was not being overtaken by events, but walking with open eyes into the hour He had chosen.", "That evening we gathered in the upper room. The oil lamps cast a warm, flickering light on the walls. The table was set with the Passover elements—the bitter herbs, the unleavened bread, the wine. I reclined near Jesus, my heart full and uneasy. He looked around at each of us with deep affection, then did something that stunned us all.", "He rose from the table, laid aside His outer garments, and tied a towel around His waist. He poured water into a basin and began to wash our feet, drying them with the towel. When He came to me, I felt the cool water on my dusty, calloused feet and His strong, gentle hands. The King of Israel—washing my feet like the lowliest servant. Shame and love flooded through me at once. When He reached Peter, Peter protested, “Lord, do you wash my feet?” Jesus answered, “What I am doing you do not understand now, but afterward you will know.” Peter declared he would never let Him do it. Jesus replied quietly, “If I do not wash you, you have no share with me.” Those words went deeper than the water. I understood, slowly, that the basin was a small picture of a far greater washing He was about to do; unless He cleansed us—not our feet only but our very souls, at a cost He had not yet named—we could have no share with Him at all. Peter then asked for his hands and head as well. Jesus’ humility broke something open inside me. If the Master stooped this low to serve, how could I ever again seek to be first?", "After He had washed our feet and put on His garments, He sat down again and said, “Do you understand what I have done to you? You call me Teacher and Lord, and you are right, for so I am. If I then, your Lord and Teacher, have washed your feet, you also ought to wash one another’s feet. For I have given you an example, that you also should do just as I have done to you.”", "The room was very still except for the soft crackle of the lamps. I felt tears welling up as His words sank in. Love expressed not in grand gestures alone, but in lowly, practical service. My spirit was both humbled and lifted. Being with Him in that upper room felt like standing on holy ground—intimate, tender, and shadowed by the knowledge that this might be our last meal together in this way.", "Then Jesus grew troubled in spirit. “Truly, truly, I say to you,” He said, looking around the table, “one of you will betray me.” We looked at one another in shock and sorrow, each asking, “Is it I, Lord?” When He gave the dipped morsel to Judas, I saw something dark pass across Judas’ face. He left quickly into the night. None of us fully understood at the time, except that Jesus had told him to do quickly what he was going to do. Then, as we ate, Jesus took bread, gave thanks, broke it, and gave it to us. “Take, eat; this is my body, which is given for you. Do this in remembrance of me.” In the same way He took the cup after supper, gave thanks, and gave it to us, and we all drank from it. “This cup is the new covenant in my blood, which is poured out for many for the forgiveness of sins.” I held the broken bread in my hand and could scarcely breathe. Here was everything He had been telling us, gathered into bread and wine: the body given, the blood poured out, the forgiveness of sins. The bread of life He had spoken of in Capernaum, the ransom He had named on the road, the new covenant the prophets had promised—all of it was being placed into our very hands at a Passover table, on the night before He would become the Lamb. I ate and drank, and did not yet grasp it, yet I felt that I was receiving the heart of the whole matter.", "In the quiet that followed, with Judas gone, Jesus spoke words of comfort and promise that I have held onto ever since. “Let not your hearts be troubled. Believe in God; believe also in me. In my Father’s house are many rooms. If it were not so, would I have told you that I go to prepare a place for you? And if I go and prepare a place for you, I will come again and will take you to myself, that where I am you may be also.” He was telling us why He had to leave: not abandonment, but preparation—His going to the cross and beyond it was the opening of the Father’s house to us, the making of a place where sinners could dwell with God forever. When Thomas asked the way, He answered, “I am the way, and the truth, and the life. No one comes to the Father except through me.” There was the whole of it: not a way He would teach us, but a way He Himself was; the only door home, and He was about to fling it open with His own body.", "He spoke of the Holy Spirit, the Helper who would come. He called us His friends, not servants. He prayed for us. And He gave us a new commandment: “That you love one another: just as I have loved you, you also are to love one another. By this all people will know that you are my disciples, if you have love for one another.”", "My heart burned within me as I listened. Love like His—selfless, sacrificial, serving. We were to love one another with the very love He was hours from proving on a cross. The night outside was dark, but in that upper room the light of His presence felt brighter than ever. I did not yet know how soon the darkness would come, but I knew I would follow Him through it.", "We remained in the upper room as the night deepened, the oil lamps burning lower and casting long, flickering shadows across the walls. The air was heavy with the scent of wine, roasted herbs, and the lingering fragrance of the nard Mary had poured out earlier. Jesus looked at each of us with eyes full of both love and sorrow, as though He were already carrying the weight of what was coming.", "“Little children,” He said gently, “yet a little while I am with you. You will seek me, and just as I said to the Jews, so now I also say to you, ‘Where I am going you cannot come.’” His words pierced me. I felt a sudden ache, like the first cold wind of winter. Where was He going that we could not follow? My hands tightened around the edge of the table as I leaned forward, hungry for every moment, every syllable. Only later would I understand: He was going where none of us could go for ourselves—down into death under the weight of our sin, to a place He had to enter alone precisely so that we would never have to.", "Simon Peter, ever bold, asked, “Lord, where are you going?” Jesus answered him quietly, “Where I am going you cannot follow me now, but you will follow afterward.” Peter pressed further, his voice thick with emotion: “Lord, why can I not follow you now? I will lay down my life for you.” I saw Jesus’ face soften with compassion, yet there was pain there too. “Will you lay down your life for me? Truly, truly, I say to you, the rooster will not crow till you have denied me three times.”", "The room fell into stunned silence. Peter’s face flushed with disbelief and hurt. I felt my own heart twist. If Peter—strong, impulsive Peter—could fall, what hope was there for the rest of us? And yet, even in foretelling the failure, Jesus had already provided for it; He had told Peter, \"I have prayed for you that your faith may not fail, and when you have turned again, strengthen your brothers.\" The denial would not be the end of Peter, because the Savior would hold him through it. Jesus did not rebuke him harshly. Instead, He continued speaking words meant to strengthen us for the coming trial.", "“I am the true vine, and my Father is the vinedresser. Every branch in me that does not bear fruit He takes away, and every branch that does bear fruit He prunes, that it may bear more fruit.” His voice was steady, almost tender, as if painting a picture we could hold onto. “Abide in me, and I in you. As the branch cannot bear fruit by itself, unless it abides in the vine, neither can you, unless you abide in me.” I felt those words sink into me like water into dry soil. Abiding. Remaining. Not striving in my own strength, but staying joined to Him and drawing all my life from Him—for, as He said plainly, apart from Him we could do nothing. The whole of the life He had been calling me into was here: not a list of duties to perform for God, but a union with Himself out of which everything else would grow. He warned us that the world would hate us as it hated Him, that we would be persecuted, even cast out of synagogues. Yet He promised the Helper—the Spirit of truth—who would come and guide us into all truth, convict the world of sin, and glorify Him. He told us our sorrow would turn to joy, like a woman’s anguish in birth forgotten in the joy of a child, and bade us take heart, for He had overcome the world.", "My spirit trembled with both fear and hope as He spoke. He was preparing us, like a father speaking to his sons before a long journey. Then Jesus lifted His eyes to heaven and prayed—not just for us at the table, but for all who would believe through our word. “That they may all be one, just as you, Father, are in me, and I in you, that they also may be in us… I in them and you in me, that they may become perfectly one.” I listened with my head bowed, feeling as though I were overhearing something sacred, something eternal. To hear Him pray for people not yet born—for the ones who would believe because we told them what we had seen—made the whole sweep of His rescue suddenly vast: He was not saving the Twelve only, but gathering a countless family across all the years to come, and binding us into the very love the Father had for the Son. His love for us, for me, washed over me like a river. In that upper room, with the night pressing against the windows, I felt known, protected, and called into something far greater than myself.", "After the prayer, Jesus rose. “Arise, let us go from here.” We followed Him out into the cool night air. The streets of Jerusalem were quiet now, the city mostly asleep. We crossed the Kidron Valley, the sound of our footsteps soft on the path, and came to a garden called Gethsemane on the slope of the Mount of Olives. Olive trees stood like silent witnesses in the moonlight, their leaves silver and still. Jesus told most of us to sit while He took Peter, James, and John a little farther. I stayed with the others at first, but the heaviness in the air drew me closer. I watched from a short distance as He fell to the ground and prayed with deep intensity, “Abba, Father, all things are possible for you. Remove this cup from me. Yet not what I will, but what you will.”", "His voice carried such anguish that it tore at my heart. Sweat fell from Him like drops of blood onto the earth. I had seen Him calm storms and raise the dead without a tremor; now I watched Him shake before a cup He had not yet drunk. I did not understand then what that cup held, but I have since: it was the cup of God’s judgment against sin, the wrath I had earned, and He was steeling Himself to drink it to the dregs in my place. He could have refused it; instead He prayed, \"not what I will, but what you will,\" and in that surrender the whole rescue hung. I wanted to go to Him, to comfort Him, but I felt paralyzed by the holiness of the moment. When He returned to us, He found us sleeping. “Could you not watch one hour?” He asked gently. The sorrow in His tone shamed me. I had failed even in this small thing while He bore the weight of the world alone. He prayed again, and again we slept. The third time He came, His voice was calm but resolute: “The hour has come. The Son of Man is betrayed into the hands of sinners. Rise, let us be going. See, my betrayer is at hand.”", "In the distance, I heard the sound of approaching feet and the flickering of torches. My heart began to pound heavily. The long night of teaching and promise was ending, and the hour of darkness had arrived. I stood beside Him, my legs unsteady, yet determined to remain. Whatever came now, I would not leave His side.", "We stood there among the olive trees as the torchlight drew nearer, casting dancing shadows through the branches. The night air was cool and heavy with the scent of crushed olives and damp earth. My heart hammered so loudly I could hear it in my ears. Judas appeared at the head of the crowd—soldiers from the chief priests and Pharisees, armed with swords and clubs, their faces grim in the flickering light. He stepped forward and kissed Jesus on the cheek. “Rabbi,” he said. The betrayal stung like a wound. One of our own. The man who had traveled with us, seen the miracles, shared our bread—now leading them to Him with the sign of friendship.", "Jesus looked at Judas with sorrowful eyes and said, “Judas, would you betray the Son of Man with a kiss?” Then He turned to the armed men. “Whom do you seek?” They answered, “Jesus of Nazareth.” He replied calmly, “I am He.” At those words, the whole group drew back and fell to the ground. I stood frozen, watching powerful soldiers and officers sprawled on the dirt. The authority in His simple declaration was overwhelming. And it told me something I have never forgotten: no one was taking His life from Him. With a word He had laid them flat; He could have walked free through the lot of them. When Peter drew a sword and struck off the ear of the high priest’s servant, Jesus healed the man with a touch—mercy even to the one come to arrest Him—and told Peter to put away his sword, for the cup the Father had given Him, He would drink. He let them bind Him. The Lamb went willingly, because being seized was not His defeat but the very thing He had come to do. It filled me with a strange mixture of awe and fear, and the first dim understanding that everything happening to Him, He was allowing for our sake.", "They rose again, more cautiously this time. Jesus spoke once more, “If you seek me, let these men go.” Even in the moment of His seizing, He was shielding us—securing our freedom as the price of His own surrender, the Shepherd stepping between the flock and the swords so that we could walk away. Peter, sudden and fierce, drew his sword and struck the high priest’s servant, cutting off his right ear. The man cried out in pain. Jesus immediately touched the servant’s ear and healed him—His last healing before the cross given to a man come to arrest Him. Then He turned to Peter with gentle rebuke: “Put your sword into its sheath. Shall I not drink the cup that the Father has given me?” I felt Peter’s shame as my own. We had been ready to fight, but Jesus chose another way—the way of obedience and sacrifice, refusing the rescue of the sword because only by drinking the cup could He rescue us.", "They seized Him then, binding His hands. I followed at a distance with the others as they led Him back across the Kidron Valley toward the city. My legs felt heavy, my spirit reeling. The garden that had been a place of prayer was now the site of betrayal and arrest. I stayed close enough to see but far enough to avoid immediate danger, my heart torn between loyalty and fear. We went first to Annas, the father-in-law of Caiaphas the high priest. Questions were hurled at Jesus about His teaching and His disciples. He answered with quiet dignity: “I have spoken openly to the world. I have always taught in synagogues and in the Temple. Ask those who have heard me what I said to them.”", "One of the officers struck Him across the face. The sound echoed sharply. I winced as if I had been hit myself. Jesus replied calmly, “If I have spoken wrongly, bear witness about the wrong; but if I have spoken rightly, why do you strike me?” The blow and His response burned into my memory. He stood there, bound yet regal, enduring injustice without retaliation—the innocent One already beginning to bear what the guilty deserved. My own courage felt small in comparison.", "From Annas they took Him to Caiaphas, where the council had gathered through the night, hunting for a charge that would hold. I would learn the worst of what passed there soon enough; for now I clung to the edge of the courtyard, sick with dread, watching the torches move behind the walls.", "As I stood warming myself by a fire in the high priest’s courtyard, a servant girl looked at me and said, “You also were with Jesus the Galilean.” Fear gripped me. I denied it. “I do not know what you mean.” Another pressed me, and again I denied. A third time, someone noted my accent: “Certainly you are one of them, for your accent betrays you.” This time I swore and cursed, “I do not know the man!” At that very moment, a rooster crowed. Jesus’ words from the upper room flooded back. I looked across the courtyard and caught His eye. He turned and looked at me. In that gaze was no anger—only sorrow and love. My heart broke. I went out and wept bitterly.", "The night stretched on, dark and heavy. I had failed Him in the moment He needed me most. And yet, even as the shame crushed me, the look He had given me would not let me drown in it—there had been no condemnation in it, only a love that seemed to reach for me through my own denial. I understood, dimly, that if I were ever to be held, it would not be by the strength of my loyalty, which had just shattered, but by His love, which had not. I could not stay away. I would follow, even at a distance, through the trial and whatever came after. Though I had faltered, His love had not. The dawn was coming, and with it the hour of His greatest suffering. I remained, broken but still His.", "I keep going back, in my memory, to what they did to Him in that council, for it is burned into me beyond forgetting. The Sanhedrin had assembled—chief priests, elders, and scribes—the room crowded, the air thick with the smell of oil lamps and incense. False witnesses were brought forward one after another, their testimonies twisted and contradictory. They spoke lies about Jesus destroying the Temple and rebuilding it in three days. These men were not seeking truth; they were hunting for any accusation that would stick. Jesus stood silent through much of it, His hands bound, His posture upright and calm—and I remembered the prophet’s words about a lamb led to the slaughter that opens not its mouth.", "Finally the high priest stood and asked Him directly, his voice sharp with impatience: “Are you the Christ, the Son of the Blessed?” The room fell into a tense hush. Jesus looked at him steadily and answered with clear, solemn power: “I am. And you will see the Son of Man seated at the right hand of Power, and coming with the clouds of heaven.” He would not save His life with a denial as I had just saved mine; He confessed the truth that would cost Him everything.", "A ripple of outrage swept through the council. Caiaphas tore his robes and cried out, “What further witnesses do we need? You have heard his blasphemy!” They condemned Him as deserving death. Then the mockery began. Some spat on Him. Others blindfolded Him, struck Him with their fists, and jeered, “Prophesy! Who is it that struck you?” The sounds of the blows and their mocking laughter cut through me like knives. This was the Man I had watched heal the sick with a touch, feed thousands, and speak words of eternal life. Now He endured their cruelty without resistance, taking in silence the scorn that should have fallen on guilty men like me. The humility and love in His silence overwhelmed me.", "As the night wore on toward morning, the council bound Him more securely and prepared to take Him to Pilate, the Roman governor, since they did not have the authority to carry out a death sentence themselves. I followed as they moved through the dimly lit streets, the first faint gray of dawn touching the eastern sky. My legs felt heavy, my spirit bruised and weary. I had seen His glory on the mountain, heard His teachings in the upper room, watched Him pray in anguish in Gethsemane. And now this—the King of Israel being led like a lamb to slaughter, exactly as the Scriptures had foretold, exactly as He had told us He must.", "Yet even in the darkness of that night, something held me. I could not turn away. The love and loyalty that had grown in me over these years refused to let me abandon Him completely, even as fear gnawed at the edges of my resolve. The path to Pilate’s headquarters lay ahead, and with it the final hours. I continued to follow, heart heavy but still fixed on Him—the One who had become my Teacher, my Lord, my everything.", "I heard, in the midst of it all, what became of Judas. When he saw that Jesus was condemned, he was seized with remorse and came back to the chief priests with the thirty pieces of silver. “I have sinned,” he said, “by betraying innocent blood.” But they only answered, “What is that to us? See to it yourself.” He threw the silver down in the Temple and went away and hanged himself. The news landed on me with a strange and terrible weight, for I had denied the Lord that very night as surely as Judas had sold Him, and the difference between us was not the size of our sin. Judas carried his guilt back to the priests, who could do nothing with it but throw it in his face; he was sorry, bitterly sorry, yet he never carried that sorrow to the only One who could forgive it. I had wept as he despaired—but the look Jesus had given me across the courtyard had drawn my brokenness back toward Him rather than away. I saw then that remorse is not the same as repentance, and that even the worst betrayal is not beyond mercy for the one who will bring it home. The tragedy was not that Judas’s sin was too great to be forgiven, but that he died without ever turning to the One whose blood, even then, was being shed for sinners exactly like him.", "We continued through the streets as the first gray light of dawn broke over the city. The guards led Jesus toward the Praetorium, the governor’s headquarters. My feet moved heavily on the stone pavement, each step echoing the growing dread in my chest. The air was cool and damp, carrying the faint smell of morning bread from nearby houses, but it felt wrong against the horror unfolding before me.", "They brought Him before Pontius Pilate, the Roman governor. Pilate came out onto the pavement, his Roman cloak sharp and official in the early light. He looked at Jesus with a mixture of curiosity and irritation. “Are you the King of the Jews?” he asked. Jesus answered quietly, “Do you say this of your own accord, or did others say it to you?” Their exchange continued, Pilate questioning Him about His kingdom. Jesus replied with calm dignity: “My kingdom is not of this world. If my kingdom were of this world, my servants would have been fighting, that I might not be delivered over to the Jews. But my kingdom is not from the world.” He was a King, but His throne would not be seized by force or defended by swords; it would be won, I would soon see, by surrender.", "I stood among the growing crowd at the edge of the courtyard, my heart swelling with strange pride and sorrow. Even bound and accused, He spoke with a royalty no earthly governor could claim. Pilate seemed unsettled. He found no guilt in Him and sought to release Jesus, but the chief priests and the crowd they had stirred shouted for Barabbas instead—a notorious rebel and murderer. Pilate asked again, “What shall I do with the man you call the King of the Jews?” They cried out louder, “Crucify him!” I watched a guilty man walk free that morning because an innocent One was condemned in his place, and the whole of it struck me later with terrible clarity: Barabbas was the first to be saved by the death of Jesus. The murderer went home to his family while the sinless One took the cross that had been meant for him—and I knew myself to be Barabbas, and so are we all.", "Pilate, still hesitant, had Jesus scourged. I could not see everything clearly from where I stood, but I heard the sickening sound of the whip striking flesh again and again. When they brought Him back out, He was wearing a crown of thorns they had twisted and pressed onto His head, and a purple robe draped mockingly over His bleeding shoulders. Blood ran down His face. Pilate gestured toward Him and declared, “Behold the man!” The crowd roared back with hatred: “Crucify him! Crucify him!”", "My knees nearly gave way. This was the Man I had followed for years—the One whose touch had healed the sick, whose words had fed my soul, whose glory I had seen on the mountain. Now He stood broken and bleeding before a hostile crowd. A deep, aching grief filled me, mixed with helpless anger at the injustice. Yet beneath it a stammering understanding was forming: the prophets had written that He would be wounded for our transgressions and crushed for our iniquities, that the punishment that brought us peace would be upon Him. I was watching it happen. Pilate washed his hands in a basin, declaring himself innocent of His blood, but still handed Jesus over to be crucified.", "The soldiers took Him away to be prepared for execution. They stripped the purple robe from Him and put His own clothes back on His torn back. A heavy wooden crossbeam was laid across His shoulders. I followed as they led Him out of the city toward the place called Golgotha, the Skull. The road was rough, and Jesus stumbled under the weight. At one point, the soldiers seized a man named Simon from Cyrene who was coming in from the country and forced him to carry the cross behind Jesus. He was led outside the city walls to die, I thought—cast out, as the sin offering was carried outside the camp and burned, bearing away what defiled the people.", "A large crowd followed, including many women who mourned and lamented for Him. Jesus turned to them and said, “Daughters of Jerusalem, do not weep for me, but weep for yourselves and for your children.” His concern for them, even in His own suffering, pierced me deeply. How could One so selfless still think of others while walking toward His death? Even here, bleeding toward the cross, He was the Shepherd, grieving for the sheep more than for Himself.", "We reached the hill called Golgotha. The soldiers offered Him wine mixed with myrrh to dull the pain, but He refused it. They laid Him on the cross and drove the nails through His hands and feet. The sound of the hammer striking metal echoed horribly in the morning air. I stood at a distance with the others, my body shaking, tears streaming down my face. They lifted the cross into place between two thieves. Above His head they placed a sign: “Jesus of Nazareth, the King of the Jews.” He was numbered with the transgressors, lifted up between two criminals—and I remembered His words to Nicodemus long ago, that as Moses lifted the serpent in the wilderness, so the Son of Man must be lifted up, that whoever looked to Him might live. Now He was lifted up, and the looking and the living could begin.", "As He hung there, the soldiers divided His garments and cast lots for His tunic. The chief priests and scribes mocked Him from below: “He saved others; let Him save Himself if He is the Christ of God!” Even one of the criminals crucified beside Him railed against Him. But the other rebuked him and turned to Jesus with humble faith: “Jesus, remember me when you come into your kingdom.” Jesus answered him with tenderness, “Truly, I say to you, today you will be with me in paradise.” It undid me, even in my grief. They had jeered that He could not save Himself—and they were right, for to save us He would not save Himself. And there, in His dying hour, with nailed hands and no strength left, He was still saving: a condemned criminal who could do no good work, offer no payment, only gasp out a plea for mercy, was promised paradise that very day. I had watched Him receive sinners for three years; His very last act before death was to receive one more.", "I remained there through the long hours, watching as darkness covered the land from the sixth hour to the ninth. At the ninth hour, Jesus cried out with a loud voice, “My God, my God, why have you forsaken me?” The cry tore through me. He who had always called God Father now cried out as one forsaken—and slowly, in the years since, I have understood why: He was bearing my forsakenness, drinking to the dregs the cup of being cut off from God that my sins had earned, so that I would never have to be forsaken. Then He cried, “It is finished,” and finally, “Father, into your hands I commit my spirit.” With that, He bowed His head and gave up His spirit. It is finished—not He is finished, but the work, the debt, the ransom, all of it paid in full and complete. And in that same moment, we were told, the great curtain of the Temple was torn in two from top to bottom, the veil that had shut sinners out of the Holy Place ripped open by no human hand. The way to God He had spoken of was thrown wide at the instant He died.", "The earth shook. The centurion standing by said in awe, “Truly this man was the Son of God.” I stood there, numb with grief, my heart torn open. The One I had followed, loved, and believed in had died. Yet even in the crushing sorrow, something deeper stirred—a quiet hope I could not yet name. I had been with Him to the end. And somehow, I knew this was not truly the end. A Roman executioner had seen what the council could not, and even at the foot of the cross the gathering of believers had already begun.", "We remained at the foot of the cross as the darkness that had covered the land began to lift. The air felt heavy and still, as though all creation held its breath. I stood with John and a few others at a distance, my eyes fixed on Jesus’ lifeless body hanging there between the two thieves. His head was bowed, His body limp and bloodied. The sight tore at me in a way no words can fully describe. This was the Man who had walked with us, taught us, healed the sick, fed the multitudes, and revealed the glory of God. Now He was gone. A profound emptiness settled in my chest, like a deep well with no bottom. I did not yet understand that the darkest thing I had ever witnessed was the very heart of the rescue—that He was never more the Savior than in the hour He seemed most defeated.", "Joseph of Arimathea, a respected member of the council who had secretly been a disciple of Jesus, went boldly to Pilate and asked for the body. Pilate granted it, surprised that Jesus had died so quickly. Joseph came with Nicodemus, the one who had come to Jesus by night long ago. Together they took the body down with careful reverence. I helped where I could, my hands trembling as we lowered Him. The weight of His lifeless form pressed not only on my arms but on my soul. They wrapped Him in clean linen cloths with spices—myrrh and aloes—according to the burial custom of the Jews. The fragrance was strong and sweet, almost overwhelming in its contrast to the horror of the day; I thought of Mary’s ointment at Bethany, poured out, He had said, for the day of His burial, and saw that she had understood before any of us. They laid Him in Joseph’s own new tomb, cut into the rock nearby, and rolled a great stone against the entrance. The women who had followed Him from Galilee—Mary Magdalene, Mary the mother of James, and Salome—watched where He was laid. Their quiet weeping mingled with the fading light as the Sabbath drew near.", "We returned to the city as evening fell, our steps slow and heavy. The streets felt strangely empty now, the earlier crowds dispersed. I found a place to rest, but sleep evaded me. My mind replayed every moment: the upper room, the garden, the trials, the scourging, the nails. Grief pressed on me like a physical weight. All our hopes seemed buried with Him in that tomb. Yet even in the darkness of that night, small flickers of memory returned—the times He had spoken of rising again on the third day, the way He had called Lazarus out of the grave, the sign of Jonah, the temple He would raise in three days. I dared not hope too much, but neither could I fully despair. Something in me refused to let go completely, as if His own words were keeping a small lamp lit against the dark.", "The Sabbath passed in quiet sorrow. We rested as the law required, but our hearts were far from rest. I stayed mostly with the others, speaking little, each of us lost in our own thoughts and memories. The women prepared spices and ointments to anoint His body after the Sabbath, their devotion unwavering even in grief.", "Then came the first day of the week, very early in the morning while it was still dark. I was awakened by urgent voices. Mary Magdalene had come running, her face a mixture of fear and astonishment. “They have taken the Lord out of the tomb,” she said breathlessly, “and we do not know where they have laid Him.” Peter and I ran together to the tomb. I outran him and reached it first. Stooping to look in, I saw the linen cloths lying there, but the body was gone. Peter arrived and went inside. The face cloth that had been on Jesus’ head was folded up in a place by itself. We stood there in the dim light of the tomb, the stone rolled away, and something began to stir in me—a dawning realization mixed with disbelief. The grave clothes lay undisturbed, as if His body had simply passed out of them; no thief steals a corpse and leaves the wrappings folded. He was not there. And then I remembered everything He had said, and I believed.", "We returned to the others, our minds racing, but the full wonder had not yet broken over me. Later that same day, as some of us gathered behind closed doors for fear of the Jews, Jesus Himself stood among us. “Peace be with you,” He said. My heart nearly stopped. It was Him—alive, bearing the wounds in His hands and side. Joy flooded through me like a river breaking through a dam. He showed us His hands and His side, and we rejoiced with a gladness deeper than any we had known before. The grief of the cross gave way to a wonder that filled every part of me. Death had not held Him. And I understood, all at once, what the whole journey had been moving toward: the cross had not been a defeat that the resurrection reversed, but a victory that the resurrection proclaimed. The ransom had been paid and accepted; the debt was truly finished; the forsakenness was over; the Lamb who was slain was standing in the room, alive forevermore, and the wounds He carried were now the very proof and seal of our salvation.", "In the days that followed, He appeared to us again and again—by the sea where He restored Peter, on the mountain where He commissioned us, in the upper room where He opened our minds to understand the Scriptures. He walked us through Moses and the Prophets and showed how all of it had been pointing to this: that the Christ should suffer and rise, and that forgiveness of sins should be proclaimed in His name to all nations. Everything I had glimpsed in pieces over three years—the Lamb of God, the bread of life, the good shepherd laying down His life, the ransom for many, the seeking of the lost—now stood together in the full light, and I saw it had been one plan all along. My spirit was remade in those encounters. The road that had led through Gethsemane and Golgotha had not ended in darkness, but in glorious light.", "We remained with Him for forty days after His resurrection, and those days were unlike any that had come before. He appeared to us at different times and in different places, always bringing peace and deeper understanding. Sometimes He would suddenly stand among us when the doors were shut, and we would startle at first, our hearts leaping with joy once we recognized Him. He ate with us—broiled fish and honeycomb—to show that He was not a spirit but truly risen in body. I touched His hands and His side where the wounds remained, and the feel of them beneath my fingers filled me with awe. The scars were real, yet they no longer spoke of defeat. They spoke of victory—the marks of the price He had paid, carried now in a body that could never die again.", "One morning by the Sea of Galilee, we had fished all night and caught nothing. As dawn broke, a figure stood on the shore and called out, “Children, do you have any fish?” We said no. He told us to cast the net on the right side of the boat. When we did, the net filled so full we could hardly drag it in. John recognized Him first: “It is the Lord!” Peter jumped into the water and swam to shore. There on the beach, Jesus had prepared a charcoal fire with fish and bread. We ate together in the early morning light, the sound of gentle waves on the pebbles mixing with the crackle of the fire. After breakfast, Jesus restored Peter with three gentle questions of love, undoing his three denials by another charcoal fire. I watched with a full heart, feeling my own failures and restorations in Peter’s—for I, too, had denied Him in my heart that night, and I, too, was being gathered back not by my faithfulness but by His. This was how He treated those who had fallen: not casting them off, but seeking them out and giving them work to do.", "On a mountain in Galilee where He had directed us, Jesus met with more than five hundred brothers and sisters at once. He stood before us all, alive and radiant, and gave us the commission that still burns in my soul: “All authority in heaven and on earth has been given to me. Go therefore and make disciples of all nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit, teaching them to observe all that I have commanded you. And behold, I am with you always, to the end of the age.”", "His voice carried across the hillside with quiet power. I felt the weight and the wonder of those words settle deep within me. We were no longer just followers—we were sent ones, charged to carry to every nation the very rescue I had watched unfold from the Jordan to the empty tomb. The plan had never been for the saving to stop with us; we had been gathered in order to be sent. A holy fear and excitement stirred in my chest. I looked at His face, the same face I had first seen years ago by the river, and knew that everything had been leading to this.", "Then came the day of His ascension. He led us out to the Mount of Olives, near Bethany. The air was clear, the sky wide and blue above the olive trees. He lifted His hands and blessed us. As He did, He was lifted up before our eyes, and a cloud took Him out of our sight. We stood there gazing upward, our hearts full to overflowing. Two men in white robes appeared beside us and said, “Men of Galilee, why do you stand looking into heaven? This Jesus, who was taken up from you into heaven, will come in the same way as you saw Him go into heaven.”", "We returned to Jerusalem with great joy, as He had commanded us to wait for the promise of the Father. Those final moments with Him remain burned into my memory—the blessing, the cloud, the empty place where He had stood. I had walked with Him from the very beginning, from the banks of the Jordan to the cross and beyond the empty tomb. I had seen His glory, felt His compassion, heard His words of life, and witnessed His victory over death. And I had finally understood what John the Baptist had meant on the first day, when he pointed and cried, “Behold, the Lamb of God, who takes away the sin of the world.” It had taken me all those years, and a cross, and an empty tomb, to grasp it; but I grasped it now.", "And now, though He is no longer walking visibly beside us, I know He is with us still, just as He promised. The light that first drew me to Him has never gone out. It burns brighter than ever in my heart, and I will spend the rest of my days telling what I have seen and heard—the story of the Word who became flesh and dwelt among us, full of grace and truth; the story of how God came to seek and to save the lost, and did not stop until the work was finished."];
