const WITNESSES_PARAS = [
`A continuation of the account: the story of the church, from Pentecost onward.`,
`§§ Acts 1:1-11`,
`For forty days after He rose, the Lord came and went among us, and they were the strangest and sweetest days of my life. He would be there when the doors were shut, and gone again, and then walking on a beach at dawn cooking fish over a fire of coals. He ate with us to prove He was no phantom; I saw Him take a piece of broiled fish and a bit of honeycomb and eat it before our eyes, and I touched the place in His side where the spear had gone in. He was the same and not the same—the very Man we had followed three years on the dusty roads of Galilee, and yet risen into a life that death could no longer touch. He opened the Scriptures to us in those days as He had on the Emmaus road, showing us from Moses and the Prophets and the Psalms how the Christ had to suffer and rise, until the whole of our holy writings seemed to turn like a face toward Him.`,
`On the last of those days He led us out toward Bethany, to the Mount of Olives, and we asked Him—still half dreaming of thrones and armies—whether He would now restore the kingdom to Israel. He answered that it was not for us to know the times the Father had fixed by His own authority, but that we would receive power when the Holy Spirit came upon us, and would be His witnesses in Jerusalem, in all Judea and Samaria, and to the end of the earth. I have come to see that those few words were the whole map of everything that followed—Jerusalem first, then the despised Samaritans, then the very ends of the earth, the gospel rolling outward in ever-widening circles until it reached the nations. He was telling us, before it began, how far His salvation meant to travel.`,
`And then, while He was blessing us, with His hands still lifted over our heads, He was taken up, and a cloud received Him out of our sight. We stood staring into the sky long after He was gone, our necks craned, our hearts torn between joy and bereavement, until two men in white robes stood beside us and said, "Men of Galilee, why do you stand looking into heaven? This Jesus, who was taken up from you into heaven, will come in the same way as you saw Him go." It was a promise wrapped in a gentle rebuke: He had not abandoned us, and there was work to do, and He would come again. So we turned from the empty sky and went down the hill back into the city, carrying His blessing and His promise, to wait for the power He had pledged.`,
`I have wondered, in the long years since, why He stayed those forty days at all, coming and going among us, rather than ascending straight from the empty tomb. I think now it was for our sakes, that we might be left in no doubt. He gave us, He said, many proofs that He was alive—the wounds touched, the fish eaten, the Scriptures opened—so that when the time came to give our testimony, even to die for it, we would not be testifying to a hope or a feeling or a dream, but to a thing we had seen and handled. A man may be mistaken about a vision in the night; he is not mistaken about the friend who broke bread with him on a hillside at dawn. The Lord knew that the witness He was about to scatter across the world would be hated and doubted and put to the sword, and He grounded it, before He left, in forty days of plain and unanswerable fact.`,
`§§ Acts 1:12-26`,
`We climbed the stairs to the upper room where we had been staying—the same kind of low, lamp-lit chamber where He had broken bread and washed our feet, perhaps the very one. We were eleven of the chosen now, with the women who had followed Him, and Mary His mother, and His brothers, who had mocked Him once and worshiped Him now, for they too had met Him risen. There is a particular bond among people who have wept the same tears and seen the same impossible morning, and we had it. We had no power of our own yet, only a promise; and so we did the one thing that people with empty hands can do, which is to pray, and we gave ourselves to it day after day, the prayers rising in that small room like the first smoke of an offering.`,
`In those days Peter stood up among the brothers, about a hundred and twenty of us in all. He was changed from the man who had cursed and sworn by the fire that he never knew the Lord; the threefold restoration on the beach had put a new kind of steel in him, the gentle, unbreakable steel that comes not from confidence in oneself but from having been forgiven much and knowing it. He spoke of Judas, who had walked with us and seen everything and sold the Lord for silver, and who had gone to his own terrible end, the field bought with his blood-money standing empty to this day. The Scripture had foretold it, Peter said, and had said too that another should take his office of oversight.`,
`So they put forward two men who had been with us the whole way, from the baptism of John until the day the Lord was taken up—Joseph called Barsabbas, and Matthias. And we prayed, asking the Lord who knows all hearts to show which one He had chosen, and we cast lots, and the lot fell to Matthias, and he was numbered with the eleven apostles. I have thought often about that quiet morning. Before the Spirit had even fallen, before the first sermon was preached, the Lord was already mending what the betrayal had torn, filling the empty place at the table, restoring the broken number of the witnesses to its wholeness. It was as if He wished to show us from the very start that no man's treachery, not even Judas's, could thwart the purpose of God or leave a permanent wound in His work. The traitor had fallen away; the testimony stood whole. And so, made complete again, we settled back to wait for the thing He had promised would come upon us.`,
`I have come to love that quiet, hidden time of waiting and prayer, though we did not love it then, for we were impatient for the power He had promised. But I see now that the Lord does much of His deepest work in His people in just such hidden seasons, when nothing seems to be happening and we are tempted to think ourselves forgotten. We were being made ready. The same men who would soon stand before thousands were first shut up together in an upper room with nothing to do but pray and wait and learn to depend on a promise. I have noticed that He nearly always brings His servants through such a wilderness of waiting before He uses them greatly, as He led Moses to the back of the desert and Paul into Arabia and His own Son into the wilderness; for power that is not first born in prayer and dependence will only puff a man up and destroy him.`,
`§§ Acts 2:1-13`,
`When the day of Pentecost came, we were all together in one place. It was the feast of weeks, the fiftieth day after the Passover when He had died as the true Lamb, the feast when our people brought the firstfruits of the wheat harvest and remembered the giving of the law on Sinai. The city was swollen again with pilgrims from every land under heaven, the streets a river of languages, the air thick with the smell of dust and bread and the smoke of the altar. The morning had begun like any other—the ordinary noises of a waking city below the window, the ordinary light slanting across the floor—and then, all at once, it was not ordinary at all.`,
`There came a sound from heaven like a mighty rushing wind, and it filled the whole house where we were sitting. I felt it before I understood it, a roaring and a pressure that was not wind, for not a cup was stirred on the table and not a hair moved on my head, and yet the sound was everywhere, inside us and around us. Then there appeared to us tongues as of fire, dividing and resting on each one of us. I looked at the faces of my companions and saw the small flames standing above them, steady and bright, and I knew without being told that the Helper He had promised in the upper room had come at last—that He had kept His word and had not left us as orphans. We were all filled with the Holy Spirit and began to speak in other tongues as the Spirit gave us utterance. I heard my own mouth forming words I had never learned, and all around me the others were doing the same, a flood of speech that was not babble but a hundred clear languages.`,
`The sound drew a crowd in the street below, and they were bewildered, because every man heard us declaring the mighty works of God in the tongue of his own birth—Parthians and Medes and Elamites, dwellers in Mesopotamia and Judea and Cappadocia, men from Pontus and Asia, from Phrygia and Egypt and the parts of Libya near Cyrene, visitors from Rome, Cretans and Arabs, each one hearing his mother's language on the lips of Galileans. Some were amazed and perplexed, asking one another what it could mean. Others sneered and said we were filled with new wine, though it was only the third hour of the morning. I have pondered long why He poured out His Spirit in just this way, scattering the gospel into so many tongues in a single hour. At Babel, long ago, God had confused the speech of proud men building a tower to heaven and scattered them across the earth; now, at Pentecost, He gave many tongues to humble men and began to gather the scattered nations back in. It was the Savior of the world keeping the word I had first heard from Samaritans at a well, that He had not come for one people only. The Spirit who fell on us that morning was nothing less than the very life of the risen Christ, applied at last to our own hearts and poured out through our mouths; everything He had purchased on the cross was now being pressed into us and through us, out toward the ends of the earth. We had been a frightened huddle behind locked doors; in one morning we became a fire that could not be hidden.`,
`I have thought, too, on the strangeness of the fire that did not burn. The tongues rested on each of us and we were not consumed, and I remembered Moses at the bush that flamed and was not eaten away, and the pillar of fire that had gone before our fathers in the wilderness. God had always come to His people in fire—but that fire had kept men at a distance, fencing the mountain, warning them not to draw near lest they die. Now the fire came down and sat gently upon ordinary heads, upon fishermen and former tax collectors and women, and did not destroy them but filled them. The God who had once been hidden behind smoke and trembling on Sinai had come, through the torn veil, to dwell in His people themselves. That, I have come to see, is what the whole gospel had been moving toward: not a God who keeps us at the foot of the mountain, but a God who makes His home in us.`,
`§§ Acts 2:14-41`,
`Then Peter stood up with the eleven, lifted his voice, and addressed the crowd, and I stood near him and watched the fisherman become a herald. He told them plainly that we were not drunk, as they supposed, for it was but the third hour of the day, but that this was what the prophet Joel had spoken—that in the last days God would pour out His Spirit on all flesh, on sons and daughters, on young and old, on menservants and maidservants, and that whoever called on the name of the Lord would be saved. And then he did a thing of breathtaking boldness: he preached Jesus to the very city that had killed Him, scarcely fifty days after the deed.`,
`He spoke of Jesus of Nazareth, a man attested by God with mighty works and wonders and signs that God did through Him in their midst, as they themselves knew. And he did not soften the charge: "This Jesus, delivered up according to the definite plan and foreknowledge of God, you crucified and killed by the hands of lawless men." But God raised Him up, Peter declared, loosing the pangs of death, because it was not possible for death to hold Him. He held up the words of David, who had written that God would not abandon His Holy One to corruption—and David, Peter reminded them, was dead and buried, his tomb still among them, so he had spoken not of himself but of the Christ who would rise. I marveled, listening, at how Peter held two truths together in a single breath, the very two I had wrestled so hard to hold myself. The cross was the most wicked thing that men had ever done—and it was the very thing God had planned before the foundation of the world for our salvation. Human malice and divine mercy had met at Golgotha and done the same deed, the blackest sin and the deepest grace folded into one act, and neither cancelled the other. This crucified Jesus, now risen and exalted to the right hand of God and having poured out the Spirit they now saw and heard, God had made both Lord and Christ.`,
`When the people heard this, they were cut to the heart, and they cried out, "Brothers, what shall we do?" I have never forgotten the answer, for it was the whole of what I had learned, now offered freely to the very hands that had driven the nails: "Repent and be baptized every one of you in the name of Jesus Christ for the forgiveness of your sins, and you will receive the gift of the Holy Spirit. For the promise is for you and for your children and for all who are far off, everyone whom the Lord our God calls to Himself." There was no debt too great to be forgiven, not even the blood of the Son of God; the men who had shouted "Crucify Him" not seven weeks before were now being offered the very forgiveness that His blood had bought. And he kept exhorting them, urging them to be saved from that crooked generation. That day about three thousand souls received his word and were baptized. I watched them go down into the water, group after group, and I thought of John standing in the Jordan long ago crying, "Behold, the Lamb of God, who takes away the sin of the world," and I understood that the taking-away had now begun in earnest, that the harvest the Lamb had died to gather was coming in by the thousands.`,
`And I have marveled since at where Peter began. He did not begin with himself, nor with us, nor even with the wonder the crowd had just witnessed; he began with the Scriptures, with Joel and with David, showing that nothing happening before their eyes was new or strange, but the very thing the prophets had foretold for the last days. This is how the Lord had taught us to read, on the Emmaus road and in the upper room—that all the Scriptures spoke of Him. And so the church was born not on the strength of a spectacle but on the foundation of the written word, rightly opened; for a faith built on signs alone will fail when the signs fade, but a faith built on the word of God endures, because that word does not pass away.`,
`§§ Acts 2:42-47`,
`Those first days had a brightness to them that I can scarcely describe to anyone who was not there. We who believed were together and held all things in common, and those who had lands or houses sold them and brought the proceeds to be laid at the apostles' feet and distributed to any who had need. No one called what he owned his own. Day by day we devoted ourselves to four things—the apostles' teaching, the fellowship, the breaking of bread, and the prayers—gathering in the Temple courts in the great open daylight and then in one another's homes as the lamps were lit. We ate together with glad and generous hearts, and the smell of those shared meals, the cheap bread and oil and thin wine of poor men passed from hand to hand, still comes back to me across all these years as the very smell of joy. Awe came upon every soul, for many wonders and signs were being done through the apostles, and a holy gladness lay over the whole company like morning light.`,
`I have come to understand that this open-handedness was not a rule we laid upon ourselves, nor some discipline we gritted our teeth to keep; it was simply the overflow of grace. People who have been forgiven a debt they could never pay cannot easily go on clutching their coins; the mercy that has been poured into them keeps spilling out toward whoever stands near. We had received everything as a gift, and so we found we could hold everything as a gift, with open hands. It was the parable of the unforgiving servant turned right side out at last—a whole community of people who knew the size of what they had been forgiven, and so could not bear to let a brother go hungry while they had bread. And the Lord added to our number day by day those who were being saved, until the little band that had hidden behind locked doors had become a thronging multitude in the heart of the city that had crucified its King.`,
`I have grown old enough now to know how rare and precious those days were, and how soon the first bloom of a thing can fade if it is not guarded. But I have also learned that the four things to which we gave ourselves in that first season are the very things that keep a church alive in every season: the apostles' teaching, that we not drift from the truth; the fellowship, that we not grow cold and solitary; the breaking of bread, that we never forget the body broken and the blood poured out for us; and the prayers, that we lean always on God and not on ourselves. Where these four are kept, the life remains; where any of them is let go, the life begins to ebb, however much else may be busy and impressive. The Lord builds His church not on great schemes but on these small, faithful, daily things.`,
`§§ Acts 3:1-26`,
`One afternoon Peter and John went up to the Temple at the hour of prayer, the ninth hour, and I went up with them. At the gate called Beautiful sat a man lame from his mother's womb, carried there and laid down each day to beg from those entering the Temple, as he had been laid for more than forty years. He saw us about to go in and asked us for alms, his hand out, his eyes already sliding past us to the next pilgrim, expecting at best a coin. Peter fixed his gaze on him, and so did John, and said, "Look at us." So he looked, expecting to receive something from them. And Peter said, "I have no silver and gold, but what I do have I give to you. In the name of Jesus Christ of Nazareth, rise up and walk." And he took him by the right hand and raised him up, and immediately his feet and ankles were made strong. He leaped up and stood and began to walk, and then to leap, and he went into the Temple with us, walking and leaping and praising God. I had seen the Lord Himself raise such men with a word; now I saw that same power flow through Peter's calloused hand—not Peter's power, but the Lord's, working through His name.`,
`All the people came running together to us in astonishment, in the portico called Solomon's. But Peter would not let them stare at us as though we had done this by our own power or our own piety. "Why do you wonder at this," he said, "or why do you stare at us, as though by our own power or godliness we have made him walk?" He preached Jesus to them again—the Author of life, whom they had denied and killed, asking for a murderer to be released instead, and whom God had raised from the dead. It was faith in the name of Jesus, Peter said, that had made this man strong, whom they saw and knew. "Repent therefore, and turn back, that your sins may be blotted out." It struck me freshly, watching the healed man cling to Peter and John in the portico, that everything still ran through the name—not through any goodness in the one healed, who had only begged, not through any holiness in the ones who spoke, who were unlearned fishermen, but through the name of the crucified and risen One alone. The lame man's leaping feet were themselves a sermon written in flesh: this is what the salvation of God does to a man who could never once stand on his own. It lifts him up by a hand he did not deserve, and sets him leaping into the house of God.`,
`I have often returned, in my mind, to the look on that man's face. He had asked for a coin, expecting nothing more, having asked for coins at that gate every day for longer than I had been alive; and he received instead his feet, his ankles, his whole life made new. It has seemed to me a parable of how God so often answers us—we come to Him asking for some small mercy, some easing of our circumstances, a little silver to get us through the day, and He gives us instead the thing we never thought to ask for and could never have imagined, Himself and His salvation, and sends us leaping into His house. We ask for coins; He gives us sons' inheritance. I have learned not to be disappointed when He withholds the small thing I begged for, for it is often because His hand is full of something far greater.`,
`§§ Acts 4:1-31`,
`While we were still speaking to the people, the priests and the captain of the Temple guard and the Sadducees came upon us, greatly annoyed that we were teaching the people and proclaiming in Jesus the resurrection from the dead—the very doctrine the Sadducees denied. They arrested Peter and John and held them overnight, for it was already evening. But many of those who had heard the word believed, and the number of the men came to about five thousand. The seed scattered in that one sermon was already a harvest.`,
`The next day the rulers and elders and scribes gathered in Jerusalem—Annas the high priest was there, and Caiaphas, the very men who had condemned the Lord to death not two months before—and they set Peter and John in their midst and demanded, "By what power or by what name did you do this?" Then Peter, filled with the Holy Spirit, said the thing that I have carried in my heart ever since as the plainest summary of the gospel that I know. He told them the lame man stood before them well by the name of Jesus Christ of Nazareth, whom they had crucified and God had raised. "This Jesus," he said, "is the stone that was rejected by you, the builders, which has become the cornerstone. And there is salvation in no one else, for there is no other name under heaven given among men by which we must be saved." No other name. Not Moses, not the Temple in whose courts they stood, not the law, not their descent from Abraham, not any goodness of our own—only the name they had tried to silence by killing the One who bore it. The rulers saw the boldness of Peter and John and perceived that they were uneducated, common men, and they were astonished, and they recognized that they had been with Jesus. I have found that to be true ever since: being with Him leaves a mark that no schooling can give and no threat can erase, a boldness that does not come from the man but from the One who stands with him.`,
`They could find nothing to say against it, for the man who had been healed was standing right there beside us, and they could not deny the miracle. So they conferred and then charged us to speak no more at all in that name, and threatened us, and let us go, finding no way to punish us because of the people, who were all glorifying God. But when Peter and John were released and came to us and reported what the chief priests and elders had said, we did not pray for safety. We lifted our voices together to God and prayed for boldness—that He would look upon their threats and grant His servants to continue to speak His word with all boldness, while He stretched out His hand to heal, and signs and wonders were performed through the name of His holy servant Jesus. And when we had prayed, the place in which we were gathered together was shaken, and we were all filled afresh with the Holy Spirit and went on speaking the word of God with boldness. I have noticed, across all the years since, that He does not often take the danger away. He gives instead the courage to walk straight into it, which is the better gift by far, for it teaches us to lean on Him and not on calm circumstances, and it leaves us, when the danger has passed, knowing that it was He and not our own nerve who carried us through.`,
`I have noticed, looking back over the whole of it, how the council kept making the same mistake, again and again, that it had made with the Lord. They had supposed that by killing Jesus they would be rid of Him and of this troublesome teaching; and within weeks His followers were filling the very courts where He had been condemned, and the man they had crucified was being preached as Lord by the thousands. Now they thought that by threatening two unlearned fishermen they could stop the word. But you cannot threaten men into silence when they have seen the dead rise; you cannot frighten away those who no longer fear death. The council had power over bodies and prisons and even over life, and it availed them nothing, because the thing they were fighting was not a movement of men but the purpose of God, and against that no scheme of the rulers of this age has ever prevailed.`,
`§§ Acts 4:32-5:11`,
`The whole company of those who believed were of one heart and soul, and no one said that any of his possessions was his own, but they had everything in common. Great grace was upon us all, and there was not a needy person among us, for as many as owned lands or houses sold them and laid the proceeds at the apostles' feet, to be distributed as any had need. Among them was a Levite from Cyprus named Joseph, whom the apostles called Barnabas, which means son of encouragement; he sold a field he owned and laid the money at the apostles' feet, and many did likewise, gladly, freely, in the joy of the grace that had been poured into them.`,
`But a man named Ananias, with the knowledge of his wife Sapphira, sold a piece of property and kept back for himself part of the proceeds, and brought only a portion and laid it at the apostles' feet, while pretending that it was the whole. Peter said, "Ananias, why has Satan filled your heart to lie to the Holy Spirit and to keep back for yourself part of the proceeds of the land? While it remained unsold, did it not remain your own? And after it was sold, was it not at your disposal? Why is it that you have contrived this deed in your heart? You have not lied to men but to God." And when Ananias heard these words, he fell down and breathed his last. About three hours later his wife came in, not knowing what had happened, and told the same lie, and she too fell down and died at Peter's feet. And great fear came upon the whole church and upon all who heard of it.`,
`That day frightened me, and I have pondered it for many years since. The sin was not that they kept back some of their money, for Peter said plainly that the field had been theirs to sell or keep, and the money theirs to give or withhold. The sin was the lie—the pretense of a holiness they did not have, the attempt to purchase a reputation for generosity within the very community that lived by free grace, to be admired as Barnabas was admired, while secretly hedging their hearts. It was the leaven of the Pharisees, the old hypocrisy the Lord had warned against, creeping back into the house of the forgiven. And I came to understand a thing that has steadied and sobered me ever since: that the God who is tender beyond all measure toward the sinner who comes to Him empty-handed, with nothing to plead but mercy, is severe toward the pretense that imagines it can deceive Him while trading on His grace. The same mercy that had received tax collectors and harlots and a dying thief would not be mocked by play-acting at devotion. It made me resolve to come to Him always exactly as I was, with no pretense and no performance, for the only ones He turns away are those who will not admit that they need Him.`,
`I have thought much, over the years, about why this severe judgment came at the very dawn of the church, when grace was so freshly and freely flowing. And I have come to believe it was a mercy, however terrible—a warning set up at the threshold, that we might know forever what kind of God we were dealing with. He is not mocked, and He will not be used. The hypocrisy of Ananias, if it had been let pass, would have spread through the young church like the leaven the Lord had warned us against, until the community of the forgiven became another den of pretenders, every man performing a holiness he did not have. The same God who runs to embrace the returning prodigal will not be flattered by the elder brother's show of duty. And so I learned to fear Him rightly—not with the terror of a slave, but with the awe of a son who knows that the Father who loves him also sees straight through him, and loves him far too much to let him live a lie.`,
`§§ Acts 5:12-42`,
`Many signs and wonders were regularly done among the people by the hands of the apostles, and we were all together in Solomon's portico. None of the rest dared join us out of fear, yet the people held us in high esteem, and more than ever believers were added to the Lord, multitudes of both men and women, so that they even carried the sick out into the streets and laid them on cots and mats, that as Peter came by at least his shadow might fall on some of them. The people gathered also from the towns around Jerusalem, bringing the sick and those afflicted with unclean spirits, and they were all healed. The mercy that had once flowed from the hem of the Lord's garment in Galilee was flowing still, now through the shadow of a fisherman, and not one who came was turned away.`,
`Then the high priest rose up, and all who were with him, the party of the Sadducees, and filled with jealousy they arrested the apostles and put them in the public prison. But during the night an angel of the Lord opened the prison doors and brought them out and said, "Go and stand in the Temple and speak to the people all the words of this Life." So at daybreak they entered the Temple and began to teach. When the council sent to the prison to have them brought, the officers found it securely locked and the guards standing at the doors, but no one inside; and word came that the men they had jailed were standing in the Temple teaching the people. Brought before the council again, and reminded that they had been strictly charged not to teach in this name, Peter and the apostles answered, "We must obey God rather than men. The God of our fathers raised Jesus, whom you killed by hanging Him on a tree. God exalted Him at His right hand as Leader and Savior, to give repentance to Israel and forgiveness of sins."`,
`When the council heard this they were enraged and wanted to kill them. But a Pharisee named Gamaliel, a teacher of the law held in honor by all the people, stood up and counseled caution. He recalled others who had risen up claiming to be somebody and had come to nothing once they were killed and their followers scattered. "So in the present case," he said, "keep away from these men and let them alone, for if this plan or this undertaking is of man, it will fail; but if it is of God, you will not be able to overthrow them. You might even be found opposing God." They took his advice, and after beating the apostles and charging them again to speak no more in the name of Jesus, they let them go. And the apostles left the presence of the council rejoicing that they were counted worthy to suffer dishonor for the name. I marveled at that joy when I heard it—men walking away from a public flogging glad, because they had been allowed to bear a little of what He had borne for them. The Master had said long before that whoever lost his life for His sake would find it, that those persecuted for His name should rejoice and be glad; and here it was, happening before my eyes. Every day, in the Temple and from house to house, they did not cease teaching and preaching that the Christ is Jesus.`,
`It was a strange new thing in the world, this gladness in suffering, and I do not think any of us could have manufactured it. We had not been brave men. We had fled in the garden; I myself had stayed at a distance through much of His trial; Peter had denied Him to a servant girl. And now here were these same men, walking out of a flogging with their backs torn and bleeding, rejoicing—not gritting their teeth and enduring, but rejoicing—that they had been counted worthy to suffer dishonor for the name. The only thing that can account for such a change is the resurrection, and the Spirit poured out from the risen Lord. Cowards do not become martyrs by trying harder. They become martyrs when they have seen, with their own eyes, that the grave is empty and that the One who conquered death is with them.`,
`§§ Acts 6:1-7`,
`As the disciples were increasing in number, a complaint arose, and it was the kind of complaint that can quietly poison a young and joyful thing if it is not met with wisdom and love. The Greek-speaking believers, the Hellenists, murmured against the Hebrews, because their widows were being neglected in the daily distribution of food, while the Hebrew widows were cared for. It was a small and practical matter on its face—who would see to it that bread reached the table of a poor foreign widow with no man to provide for her—and yet the unity of the body hung on it.`,
`So the twelve summoned the full number of the disciples and said it was not right that they should give up preaching the word of God to wait on tables, and they bade us pick out from among ourselves seven men of good repute, full of the Spirit and of wisdom, whom they would appoint to this duty, that the apostles might devote themselves to prayer and to the ministry of the word. What they said pleased the whole gathering, and we chose Stephen, a man full of faith and of the Holy Spirit, and Philip, and five others, and set them before the apostles, who prayed and laid their hands on them. And the word of God continued to increase, and the number of the disciples multiplied greatly in Jerusalem, and a great many of the priests became obedient to the faith.`,
`It seemed a small thing, the choosing of seven men to carry bread to widows, and yet I have come to see deep wisdom in it. The Lord had girded Himself with a towel and washed our feet to teach us that greatness in His kingdom is service; now the body He was building was learning the same lesson, taking pains that its weakest and most overlooked members—the widows, the foreigners, those with no voice and no advocate—should not be neglected. A church that had been saved by sheer mercy could not help but be marked by mercy, down to the daily bread on a forgotten widow's table. And I noticed something I have seen many times since: that when this care was rightly ordered, when love was given its proper place and the word its proper freedom, the gospel ran all the faster, and even priests who had served in the Temple bowed their heads to the crucified Christ. Mercy makes room for truth to run.`,
`I have noticed, too, how the Lord turns even our complaints and shortfalls into the means of His grace. The neglect of the widows was a real failure, and it might have festered into bitterness and division between the Greek-speaking believers and the Hebrews; instead, in the hands of wise and humble men, it became the occasion for the church to learn to share its burdens and to set apart servants for the care of the weak. And from among those seven servants chosen merely to wait on tables came Stephen, the first martyr, and Philip, the evangelist who would carry the gospel to Samaria and to the Ethiopian. The Lord raised up His mightiest instruments from the lowliest service. I have learned never to despise the small and practical tasks of love, the carrying of bread to a forgotten widow, for it is often in such humble service that He prepares His servants for greater things, and it is always such service that adorns the gospel and lets the word run free.`,
`§§ Acts 6:8-7:60`,
`Stephen, full of grace and power, did great wonders and signs among the people. But some of the men of the synagogue of the Freedmen rose up and disputed with him, and they could not withstand the wisdom and the Spirit with which he spoke. So, as they had done with the Lord, they secretly stirred up men to bring false charges, claiming he spoke blasphemous words against Moses and against God, against the holy place and the law. They seized him and brought him before the council and set up false witnesses, and all who sat in the council, gazing at him, saw that his face was like the face of an angel.`,
`Then the high priest asked if these things were so, and Stephen answered with the whole long story of our people—Abraham called out from his country, Joseph sold by his brothers and yet made their savior, Moses rejected by the very people he came to deliver, the tabernacle and the Temple, the prophets persecuted one after another. And he showed how at every turn our fathers had resisted the Holy Spirit and rejected the deliverers God sent them, until at last they had betrayed and murdered the Righteous One Himself, of whom the prophets had foretold. "You stiff-necked people," he cried at the end, "uncircumcised in heart and ears, you always resist the Holy Spirit. As your fathers did, so do you." They were enraged and ground their teeth at him. But Stephen, full of the Holy Spirit, gazed into heaven and saw the glory of God, and Jesus standing at the right hand of God. "Behold," he said, "I see the heavens opened, and the Son of Man standing at the right hand of God." At that they cried out with a loud voice, stopped their ears, and rushed together at him. They cast him out of the city and stoned him, and the witnesses laid down their garments at the feet of a young man named Saul. And as they were stoning him, Stephen called out, "Lord Jesus, receive my spirit." And falling to his knees, he cried with a loud voice, "Lord, do not hold this sin against them." And when he had said this, he fell asleep.`,
`I was not there to see it with my own eyes, but those who were could not stop telling it, and every detail pierced me to the heart. For I had stood at the foot of another execution and heard another voice pray, "Father, forgive them, for they know not what they do," and then commit His spirit into His Father's hands. And now here was Stephen, the first of us to follow the Master all the way into death, dying in the very shape and image of his Lord—seeing heaven opened, as Nathanael had been promised on the first day he met Jesus that he would; beholding the Son of Man not seated on His throne but standing, as if the risen Christ had risen even from His seat in glory to receive His faithful servant home and to bear witness for him. Stephen committed his spirit to Jesus as Jesus had committed His to the Father, and forgave his murderers as Jesus had forgiven His. I understood then a thing I had not fully grasped before: that salvation does not only rescue us from death and hell; it remakes us into the very likeness of the One who saved us, until even our dying comes to look like His. And I saw, too, that the blood of the Lord, far from being avenged on those who shed it, was still pleading mercy for sinners through the lips of a dying man—for surely among the men who heard Stephen pray for their forgiveness, watching the coats at their feet, stood the very Saul whom that prayer would, before long, lay hold of and make new.`,
`I have thought, too, of the young man at whose feet the witnesses laid their cloaks, watching it all with approval—Saul of Tarsus. He heard every word of Stephen's defense; he saw the face like an angel's; he heard the prayer for the forgiveness of the very men casting the stones, and surely that prayer was offered for him among them. And I have wondered whether the memory of that day did not go down into Saul like a seed buried in hard ground, to lie there through all his raging until the light on the Damascus road brought it to life. Stephen could not have known, as the stones struck him, that he was praying for the man who would carry the gospel to the ends of the earth. But the Lord knew. And it has taught me never to think any testimony wasted, even the testimony of a dying man before his murderers, for we cannot see what seed is taking root in the heart of the one who seems most set against us.`,
`§§ Acts 8:1-25`,
`And Saul approved of his execution. On that day a great persecution broke out against the church in Jerusalem, and we were all scattered throughout the regions of Judea and Samaria, except the apostles, who remained. Devout men buried Stephen and made great lamentation over him. But Saul was ravaging the church, entering house after house, and dragging off men and women alike and committing them to prison. The huddle that the Spirit had set ablaze at Pentecost was now driven out of the city and scattered across the land. And yet, as I watched the believers flee with what little they could carry, I began to see a thing I could not have seen in the moment of grief: that the scattering itself was the sowing. Those who were scattered went about preaching the word wherever they went, so that the very persecution meant to stamp out the gospel only flung its seed into a hundred towns where it had never been heard. What our enemies intended for our destruction, God was bending into the spread of His word, exactly as He had once bent the cross itself.`,
`Philip, one of the seven, went down to a city of Samaria and proclaimed to them the Christ. The crowds with one accord paid attention to what he said, hearing him and seeing the signs he did, for unclean spirits came out of many who were possessed, crying with a loud voice, and many who were paralyzed or lame were healed. And there was much joy in that city. I thought again of the woman at the well in those same Samaritan hills, the first soul to whom the Lord had told plainly who He was, and of how He had said the hour was coming when men would worship the Father neither on this mountain nor in Jerusalem but in spirit and in truth. Now it was coming to pass before our eyes; the well was overflowing, and a people we had been raised to despise was drinking the living water with joy.`,
`There was in that city a man named Simon, who had practiced magic and amazed the people, claiming to be somebody great, so that they called him the great power of God. Even he believed and was baptized and followed Philip, astonished at the signs. When the apostles in Jerusalem heard that Samaria had received the word of God, they sent Peter and John, who came down and prayed for the new believers that they might receive the Holy Spirit, and laid their hands on them, and they received Him—the same gift, the same seal, given to the Samaritans as to us at Pentecost, that none of us might ever boast as though the grace were ours by birthright and not theirs by gift. But when Simon saw that the Spirit was given through the laying on of the apostles' hands, he offered them money, saying, "Give me this power also, that anyone on whom I lay my hands may receive the Holy Spirit." Peter rebuked him sharply: "May your silver perish with you, because you thought you could obtain the gift of God with money! You have neither part nor lot in this matter, for your heart is not right before God. Repent of this wickedness." Here was the same lesson as Ananias's, in another key: the gift of God cannot be bought, nor bent to serve a man's own greatness. Salvation is grace from first to last, and the moment a man tries to purchase it or use it for himself, he shows he has not understood it at all. The thing was free precisely because it was beyond all price.`,
`I have lived long enough to see this pattern repeat in every generation, and it never fails to astonish me: that the enemies of the gospel, in their fury to destroy it, only scatter its seed the wider. They drove us out of Jerusalem to silence us, and we went everywhere preaching the word. They have burned our writings, and the copies multiplied; they have killed our preachers, and the blood of the martyrs has drawn more to the faith than any sermon. It is as though the Lord has so ordered things that the harder the world strikes at His church, the farther it flings the gospel abroad. I do not say we should seek persecution, nor that it is anything but grievous to bear; only that I have ceased to despair when the storm breaks upon us, for I have seen, again and again, that the wind which scatters us is the wind that sows the world.`,
`§§ Acts 8:26-40`,
`Then an angel of the Lord said to Philip, "Rise and go toward the south, to the road that goes down from Jerusalem to Gaza." This is a desert place. And he rose and went, and behold, there was an Ethiopian, a eunuch, a court official of the Candace, queen of the Ethiopians, who was in charge of all her treasure. He had come to Jerusalem to worship and was returning home, seated in his chariot, and he was reading aloud the prophet Isaiah. The Spirit said to Philip, "Go over and join this chariot." So Philip ran to him and heard him reading Isaiah the prophet, and asked, "Do you understand what you are reading?" And the man answered, "How can I, unless someone guides me?" and invited Philip to come up and sit with him.`,
`Now the passage he was reading was this: "Like a sheep he was led to the slaughter, and like a lamb before its shearer is silent, so he opens not his mouth. In his humiliation justice was denied him. Who can describe his generation? For his life is taken away from the earth." The eunuch asked, "About whom does the prophet say this, about himself or about someone else?" Then Philip opened his mouth, and beginning with this very Scripture he told him the good news about Jesus. As they went along the road they came to some water, and the eunuch said, "See, here is water! What prevents me from being baptized?" And he commanded the chariot to stop, and they both went down into the water, and Philip baptized him. And when they came up out of the water, the Spirit carried Philip away, and the eunuch saw him no more, and went on his way rejoicing.`,
`When the account of it reached me, I sat a long while in wonder. A man from the very ends of the earth, a foreigner and an outsider in more ways than one, returning from a Jerusalem that could offer him only the outer courts, sat reading the song of the suffering servant—the very prophecy I had watched fulfilled with my own eyes at Golgotha, the lamb led to the slaughter who opened not his mouth—and asked the one question that mattered: of whom does the prophet speak? And a believer set on that road by the Spirit told him it was Jesus, and before the dust of the desert had settled, the man was carried down into the water and up again into the family of God, and went on his way rejoicing toward Africa. The plan was running far ahead of any of us now. The Lamb who takes away the sin of the world was being preached from an ancient scroll to an African official in a chariot in the wilderness, and the joy of the Samaritan well and the joy of Pentecost was kindled in one more heart, in one more nation. I marveled that nothing was wasted—not the prophet's words written seven centuries before, not the eunuch's long and costly pilgrimage, not even the persecution that had driven Philip out of Jerusalem and set him, of all places, on that lonely southern road at the very hour the chariot passed.`,
`What has always struck me most is the smallness of it, set beside its greatness. There was no crowd here, no thronging city, no thousands cut to the heart—only one man, in a chariot, on an empty desert road, and one believer sent to run alongside him. The Spirit who had filled a whole house at Pentecost now arranged the meeting of two men in the middle of nowhere, because one searching soul was reading a scroll he could not understand. I have learned from it that the God who saves the multitudes does not despise the one; that He will set a whole sequence of providences in motion—an angel, a road, a chariot, a scroll opened to just the right page—for the sake of a single seeking heart. The shepherd who left the ninety-nine to go after the one was at work on that desert road, and is at work still, wherever one lost soul is feeling its way toward Him.`,
`§§ Acts 9:1-31`,
`But the wonder that undid me most in all those days was Saul. He was still breathing threats and murder against the disciples of the Lord, not content to have hounded us out of Jerusalem, and he went to the high priest and asked for letters to the synagogues at Damascus, so that if he found any belonging to the Way, men or women, he might bring them bound to Jerusalem. He was the worst of all our enemies, the one whose very name made the scattered believers tremble and lower their voices, the man who had stood approving while Stephen was stoned and kept the killers' coats. And it was him—him of all men under heaven—that the Lord chose.`,
`As he went on his way and approached Damascus, about noon, suddenly a light from heaven shone around him, brighter than the noonday sun, and he fell to the ground and heard a voice saying, "Saul, Saul, why do you persecute me?" And he said, "Who are you, Lord?" And the answer came: "I am Jesus, whom you are persecuting. Rise and enter the city, and you will be told what you are to do." I have turned that exchange over in my mind more times than I can count. The Lord did not say, "Why do you persecute my people," but "why do you persecute me"—as though every blow Saul struck against the least and lowliest believer fell upon the Master Himself, so closely had He bound us to His own body that to touch us was to touch Him. Saul rose from the ground, and when he opened his eyes he could see nothing, and they led him by the hand into Damascus, and for three days he was without sight and neither ate nor drank.`,
`Then the Lord called a disciple in Damascus named Ananias and sent him to Saul. Ananias was afraid, for he had heard how much evil this man had done to the saints in Jerusalem; but the Lord said, "Go, for he is a chosen instrument of mine to carry my name before the Gentiles and kings and the children of Israel, for I will show him how much he must suffer for the sake of my name." So Ananias went, and entered the house, and laid his hands on him and said, "Brother Saul"—brother, to the man who had come to drag him to prison—"the Lord Jesus, who appeared to you on the road, has sent me that you may regain your sight and be filled with the Holy Spirit." And immediately something like scales fell from his eyes, and he regained his sight and rose and was baptized, and took food and was strengthened. And at once he began to proclaim Jesus in the synagogues, saying, "He is the Son of God," so that all who heard were amazed and said, "Is not this the man who made havoc in Jerusalem of those who called upon this name?"`,
`When the report reached us, we could scarcely believe it, and some among us feared it was a trick, a wolf pretending to be a sheep to root out the flock. But it was true. The chief persecutor, the man who had held the coats at Stephen's stoning, was now preaching with his whole soul the very faith he had set out to destroy, and was soon fleeing for his own life, lowered in a basket through a window in the wall by the disciples he had come to hunt. If grace could lay hold of Saul, then there was no one anywhere beyond its reach, and no sinner whose record was too black and bloody to be washed white as snow. The Lord had said, long before, on the night He sat at table with tax collectors, that He came not to call the righteous but sinners; and here was the proof of it blazing brighter than the Damascus noon. He had reached down out of heaven and seized His fiercest enemy by the throat of his own hatred and made him His most ardent friend and His chosen instrument to the nations. I had thought, after the cross and the empty tomb, that I had finally measured the depth of His mercy. Saul taught me that I had not yet seen the bottom of it, and never would. And so the church throughout all Judea and Galilee and Samaria, no longer hunted by that man, had peace and was built up, and walking in the fear of the Lord and the comfort of the Holy Spirit, it multiplied.`,
`I have pondered, too, the gentleness hidden inside the severity of that noon. The light struck Saul blind and threw him to the ground—but the voice did not destroy him, as it might justly have destroyed the man who was hauling the saints to prison and death. It asked him a question: "Why do you persecute me?" The Lord came to His enemy not with a thunderbolt but with a question, the question of a wounded love seeking to be understood. And He sent, to the blinded persecutor, not an avenging angel but a trembling disciple who called him "Brother Saul." I think the Lord meant from the very first not to crush Saul but to win him, to break him only so far as was needed to make him new. And I have taken great comfort from it, for I have learned that when the Lord lays a man low, it is most often not to destroy him but to save him, and that the same hand which strikes us blind is the hand that means to give us sight we never had before.`,
`§§ Acts 9:32-43`,
`Peter, traveling here and there among all the churches in those days of peace, came down to the saints who lived at Lydda. There he found a man named Aeneas, bedridden for eight years and paralyzed. Peter said to him, "Aeneas, Jesus Christ heals you; rise and make your bed." And immediately he rose, and all who lived in Lydda and the plain of Sharon saw him and turned to the Lord. Near there, at Joppa by the sea, was a disciple named Tabitha, which means Dorcas, a woman full of good works and acts of charity, who had clothed the widows of the city with garments made by her own hands. In those days she fell sick and died, and they washed her body and laid it in an upper room and sent for Peter. When he came, the widows stood beside him weeping and showing the tunics and cloaks she had made while she was with them. Peter put them all outside, and knelt down and prayed, and then, turning to the body, said, "Tabitha, arise." And she opened her eyes, and seeing Peter she sat up. He gave her his hand and raised her up, and called the saints and widows and presented her alive.`,
`I smiled when I heard it, and wept a little too, for it was the old pattern still at work, unchanged. "Tabitha, arise" was so near to "Talitha, cumi"—the very words I had heard the Lord speak by Jairus' bed, when He took a dead girl by the hand and warmed her cold fingers back to life—that I knew at once whose power was moving through Peter's outstretched hand. The Lord who had taken death by the hand in Galilee was taking it by the hand still, through His servants, and the weeping widows of Joppa knew the same joy as that mother long ago. Death was learning, town by town, that it no longer had the last word where the name of Jesus was spoken.`,
`It moved me, when I heard of Tabitha, that the church remembered her not for any great work or office but for the tunics and cloaks she had sewn with her own hands for the poor widows of Joppa. They stood weeping and holding up the garments she had made. Hers was a hidden life of small mercies, the kind the world never records; and yet the Lord raised her by name, and her story is told now in every nation under heaven. I have learned from her that nothing done in love for the least of His people is ever truly small or ever truly lost. The Lord keeps a different account than the world keeps. The widow's two coins, the cup of cold water, the garments sewn for the poor—these are treasured up in heaven, while the monuments of the proud crumble; and on the last day it will be the Tabithas, and not the Herods, who are found to have built something that lasts.`,
`§§ Acts 10:1-48`,
`Then the Lord set in motion a thing that would widen the church beyond anything we in Jerusalem had yet dared to imagine. In Caesarea there was a centurion named Cornelius, of the Italian Cohort, a devout man who feared God with all his household, who gave alms generously and prayed continually—and yet a Gentile, an uncircumcised Roman soldier, a man outside the covenant and the promises. One afternoon an angel of God came to him in a vision and told him his prayers and alms had come up as a memorial before God, and bade him send to Joppa for Peter. And the next day, as his men drew near the city, Peter went up on the roof of Simon the tanner's house to pray, about the sixth hour, and became hungry. While they prepared a meal he fell into a trance, and saw the heavens opened and something like a great sheet descending, let down by its four corners, full of all kinds of animals and reptiles and birds, clean and unclean together. And a voice said, "Rise, Peter; kill and eat." But Peter said, "By no means, Lord; for I have never eaten anything that is common or unclean." And the voice came again: "What God has made clean, do not call common." This happened three times, and then the sheet was taken up to heaven. While Peter puzzled over what it could mean, the men from Cornelius arrived at the gate, and the Spirit told him to go with them without hesitation, making no distinction.`,
`So Peter went, taking some of the brothers from Joppa, and entering the house of a Gentile—a thing no observant Jew would lightly do—he found many gathered, and said, "You yourselves know how unlawful it is for a Jew to associate with or visit anyone of another nation, but God has shown me that I should not call any person common or unclean." And as he preached to them—how God anointed Jesus of Nazareth with the Holy Spirit and power, how He went about doing good and healing all who were oppressed by the devil, how they put Him to death by hanging Him on a tree, and how God raised Him on the third day and made Him to appear, and how everyone who believes in Him receives forgiveness of sins through His name—while Peter was still speaking these words, the Holy Spirit fell on all who heard. The believers from among the circumcised who had come with Peter were amazed, because the gift of the Holy Spirit was poured out even on the Gentiles, for they heard them speaking in tongues and extolling God, just as we had at Pentecost. Then Peter said, "Can anyone withhold water for baptizing these people, who have received the Holy Spirit just as we have?" And he commanded them to be baptized in the name of Jesus Christ.`,
`When the account of this reached me, the words of the Lord at the Samaritan well came rushing back, and the title the townsfolk had given Him there—the Savior of the world. I had believed it, and yet I confess that some narrow, stubborn corner of my heart had still half expected the Gentiles to come in, when they came, by some lesser door, as guests admitted to the edge of the feast rather than as children seated at the table. But God had given them the very same Spirit, in the very same way, with the very same signs, with no thought of partiality at all. Peter said it best, standing in that Roman house: "Truly I understand that God shows no partiality, but in every nation anyone who fears Him and does what is right is acceptable to Him." The cross had torn the veil of the Temple from top to bottom; now it was tearing down the ancient wall between Jew and Gentile as well, the dividing wall of hostility that had stood as long as anyone could remember. And I saw, more clearly than ever before, that the family the Lord had prayed for in the upper room on the night He was betrayed—that they may all be one, as He and the Father are one—was to be gathered out of every nation under heaven, all of them coming in by the one and only door, which is Christ, and none of them by any worthiness of their own.`,
`I have marveled, too, at the patience the Lord showed with Peter's slowness, and through Peter with all of us. It took a vision repeated three times, and the Spirit's plain command, and the men already knocking at the gate, to move Peter to go to a Gentile's house—so deep ran the old walls in even the best of us. We do not lay down our prejudices easily, even when grace has laid hold of us; the heart clings to its old distinctions and its old contempts long after the gospel has, in truth, abolished them. But the Lord was gentle and persistent with Peter, leading him step by step until he could say with his own mouth that God shows no partiality. And I have taken comfort from it, for I too have had to be led, slowly and against my own resistance, into the full breadth of His mercy; and He has never grown weary of leading me, nor cast me off for being slow to learn how wide His love truly is.`,
`§§ Acts 11:1-30`,
`When Peter returned to Jerusalem, those of the circumcision party criticized him, saying, "You went to uncircumcised men and ate with them." But Peter told them the whole story in order—the sheet, the voice, the men at the gate, the Spirit falling on the Gentiles exactly as on us at the beginning—and he ended, "If then God gave the same gift to them as He gave to us when we believed in the Lord Jesus Christ, who was I that I could stand in God's way?" And when they heard these things, they fell silent, and then they glorified God, saying, "Then to the Gentiles also God has granted repentance that leads to life." I have treasured that little phrase ever since—that God granted repentance—for it reminded me again of a truth I am always in danger of forgetting: that even our turning to Him is His gift and not our achievement. He does not stand at a distance waiting for us to manufacture a change of heart and present it to Him as our contribution; He works the change Himself, opening blind eyes and softening hard hearts, in Jew and Gentile alike. The whole of it, from first to last, is grace.`,
`Meanwhile those who had been scattered by the persecution that arose over Stephen traveled as far as Phoenicia and Cyprus and Antioch, speaking the word at first to Jews only. But some of them, men of Cyprus and Cyrene, came to Antioch and began speaking to the Greeks also, preaching the Lord Jesus, and the hand of the Lord was with them, and a great number who believed turned to the Lord. When the report reached the church in Jerusalem, they sent Barnabas to Antioch, and when he came and saw the grace of God, he was glad, and he exhorted them all to remain faithful to the Lord with steadfast purpose, for he was a good man, full of the Holy Spirit and of faith. Then Barnabas went off to Tarsus to look for Saul, and when he had found him he brought him to Antioch, and for a whole year they met with the church and taught a great many people. And it was in Antioch that the disciples were first called Christians—Christ's ones, the people of the Anointed.`,
`I liked that name from the first time I heard it, and I like it still. It put Him at the center, where He belongs, for we were not known by a teaching we held or a rule we kept or a place we worshiped, but by a Person—the One whose name was the only name under heaven by which we are saved, the name into which we were baptized, the name we suffered for and would, many of us, die for. To be a Christian was simply to belong to Christ. And about that time some prophets came down from Jerusalem, and one named Agabus foretold by the Spirit a great famine over all the world, which came to pass in the days of Claudius. So the disciples, each according to his ability, determined to send relief to the brothers living in Judea, and they did so, sending it by the hand of Barnabas and Saul. I noted with quiet joy that the grace lavished on the Gentiles in Antioch was already flowing back as practical love toward the poor Jewish believers in Jerusalem—the two halves of the new family, once divided by a wall of hostility, now sending bread to one another across the miles. The wall was truly down.`,
`I have always thought it fitting that the name was first given to us at Antioch, and not at Jerusalem—that we were first called Christians not in the holy city of our fathers, with its Temple and its law, but in a great pagan crossroads of the Gentile world, where the gospel had broken out among the Greeks. For the name marked the new thing God was doing: gathering a people for Himself not out of one nation by birth but out of every nation by grace, a people defined no longer by descent from Abraham or by the marks of the law, but by belonging to Christ. And it was at Antioch, too, that this new people first sent relief back to the poor of Jerusalem—Gentile believers caring for Jewish believers across every old barrier of race and suspicion. The name and the love went together, and both said the same thing: the wall was down, and we were one in Him.`,
`§§ Acts 12:1-25`,
`About that time—it would have been some fourteen years after the Lord's ascension—Herod the king laid violent hands on some who belonged to the church. He killed James the brother of John with the sword. The news struck me like a physical blow, and I grieve him to this day. James, one of the three the Lord had taken up the high mountain to see His glory; one of the sons of thunder who had once wanted to call down fire on a Samaritan village and had asked, with his brother, to sit at the Lord's right hand and left in His kingdom—now the first of the apostles to drink the cup. And I remembered the Lord's own words to the two brothers that day, when they asked for the thrones: that they did not know what they were asking, but that they would indeed drink the cup that He drank. James had been granted the very thing he once so boldly requested—a place near his Lord—though not at all in the way he had imagined it, for the road to that place ran through a martyr's death by Herod's sword. He had asked for glory and been given the cross first, as his Master had been; and I do not doubt that he understood, at the end, and was content.`,
`When Herod saw that it pleased the Jews, he proceeded to arrest Peter also, during the days of Unleavened Bread, and put him in prison, delivering him to four squads of soldiers to guard him, intending after the Passover to bring him out to the people. So Peter was kept in prison, but earnest prayer for him was made to God by the church. And on the very night before Herod was to bring him out, while Peter slept between two soldiers, bound with two chains, and sentries stood at the door, an angel of the Lord stood beside him and a light shone in the cell, and the chains fell off his hands, and the angel led him past the guards and through the iron gate leading into the city, which opened of its own accord. When Peter came to himself, he went to the house of Mary, the mother of John Mark, where many were gathered together praying for him. He knocked at the door of the gateway, and a servant girl named Rhoda came to answer, and recognizing Peter's voice, in her joy she did not open the gate but ran in and told them that Peter was standing there, and they said to her, "You are out of your mind." I have laughed often at that small and very human detail across the years—that we were gathered praying earnestly for Peter's release, and could not believe it when the answer came knocking at the door. We pray, and still we are astonished when God answers.`,
`Not long after, Herod went down to Caesarea, and when the people of Tyre and Sidon flattered him as a god, crying out that his voice was the voice of a god and not of a man, he accepted their worship and gave God no glory, and immediately an angel of the Lord struck him down, and he was eaten by worms and died. But the word of God increased and multiplied. The contrast was not lost on me, and I have never forgotten it: the king who grasped at glory and let men worship him rotted away in a single dreadful day, while the gospel he had tried to silence by the sword only grew the faster. So it always is. The proud man who exalts himself is brought low, and the humble word he despised goes on conquering the world.`,
`I have set the two deaths of that chapter side by side many times in my mind—James, struck down by the sword of an earthly king, and Herod, struck down by the angel of the King of heaven. To the eye of the world, James was the loser: a leader of the church cut off early, while the tyrant flourished and was praised as a god. But the world's eye sees only the surface of things. James went home to his Lord, to the place near the throne that he had asked for long ago; Herod was carried off by worms in his pride and was no more. I have lived long enough to watch this pattern repeat in many lives, and I no longer envy the Herods their season of glory, nor pity the James their seeming defeat. The Lord's servants may fall to the sword, but they fall upward, into His arms; it is the proud who exalt themselves against Him who truly fall, and there is no rising from that fall.`,
`§§ Acts 13:1-12`,
`In the church at Antioch there were prophets and teachers—Barnabas, and Simeon who was called Niger, and Lucius of Cyrene, and Manaen who had been brought up with Herod the tetrarch, and Saul. While they were worshiping the Lord and fasting, the Holy Spirit said, "Set apart for me Barnabas and Saul for the work to which I have called them." So after fasting and praying, they laid their hands on them and sent them off—and thus, in a quiet room of praying men, began the journeys that would carry the gospel across the world. I have thought it fitting that it started not with a strategy of men but with the Spirit's own word, and not with the famous apostles in Jerusalem but with a mixed company in Antioch, a Cyprian Levite and a converted persecutor and men of Africa and a foster-brother of a king. The Lord chooses His instruments where He will, and sends them out by His own breath.`,
`Being sent by the Holy Spirit, they went down to Seleucia and sailed to Cyprus, Barnabas's home country, and proclaimed the word of God in the synagogues, with John Mark to assist them. When they had gone through the whole island as far as Paphos, they came upon a magician, a Jewish false prophet named Bar-Jesus, who was with the proconsul Sergius Paulus, a man of intelligence who summoned Barnabas and Saul, wishing to hear the word of God. But the magician opposed them, seeking to turn the proconsul away from the faith. Then Saul, who is also called Paul—and from this point I came to know him by that name—filled with the Holy Spirit, looked intently at him and said, "You son of the devil, you enemy of all righteousness, full of all deceit and villainy, will you not stop making crooked the straight paths of the Lord? And now, behold, the hand of the Lord is upon you, and you will be blind." And immediately mist and darkness fell upon him, and he went about seeking someone to lead him by the hand. Then the proconsul believed, astonished at the teaching of the Lord. I marked the strange justice in it: the man who had tried to keep another in spiritual darkness was himself struck blind for a season, as Saul himself once had been on the Damascus road—and I wondered whether Paul, leading words of judgment against a blinded man, remembered the hand that had once led him, blinded, into the city, and the grace that had given him back his sight. Even his severity was the severity of one who had been shown mercy.`,
`It has not escaped me that the great mission to the nations began in a meeting for worship and fasting, with no plan of men at all, only the Spirit's word: "Set apart for me Barnabas and Saul." We did not devise the strategy that carried the gospel across the world; the Spirit did, and He sent out whom He chose, when He chose. I have watched men since try to spread the faith by their own cleverness and their own schemes, and accomplish little; and I have watched the Lord take unlikely men, set apart by His Spirit in some obscure gathering of prayer, and through them shake the nations. The work is His, the sending is His, the increase is His. Our part is to worship and to wait and to obey when He speaks, and to go where He sends, trusting that the One who calls is the One who will accomplish through us far more than we could ever have planned.`,
`§§ Acts 13:13-52`,
`From Paphos Paul and his companions set sail and came to Perga in Pamphylia, where John Mark left them and returned to Jerusalem—a parting that would later cause grief between Paul and Barnabas. They went on to Antioch in Pisidia, and on the Sabbath they entered the synagogue and sat down, and after the reading of the Law and the Prophets the rulers sent word inviting them to speak any word of encouragement they had. So Paul stood up, motioned with his hand, and preached a sermon that, when it was later repeated to us, I recognized at once as the very heart of all that we had learned at the Lord's own feet.`,
`He told the story of Israel—the choosing of the fathers, the deliverance from Egypt, the wilderness, the judges, Samuel, Saul, and then David, a man after God's own heart. And from David's line, Paul said, God had brought to Israel a Savior, Jesus, as He had promised, of whom John the Baptist had gone before preaching. The people of Jerusalem and their rulers, not recognizing Him nor understanding the very prophets they read every Sabbath, had condemned Him and asked Pilate to have Him executed, and when they had carried out all that was written of Him, they took Him down from the tree and laid Him in a tomb. But God raised Him from the dead, and He appeared for many days to those who had come up with Him from Galilee—and I caught my breath when this was repeated to me, for he meant us, the witnesses, the very company I belonged to. Then Paul came to the point of it all, and ended with words I have never been able to forget: "Let it be known to you therefore, brothers, that through this man forgiveness of sins is proclaimed to you, and by him everyone who believes is freed from everything from which you could not be freed by the law of Moses."`,
`Freed from everything that the law could never free us from. I had sat under that law my whole life; I knew its weight in my bones, the way it named every debt and forgave none, the way it could show me my sickness in perfect detail and never once heal it. And here was the whole gospel pressed into a single sentence: what the law could only condemn, Christ had paid; the debts we could never be acquitted of by our own keeping, we were freely acquitted of by simply believing in Him. The next Sabbath nearly the whole city gathered to hear the word of the Lord, and when the jealous leaders contradicted Paul and reviled him, he and Barnabas turned boldly and said, "It was necessary that the word of God be spoken first to you. Since you thrust it aside and judge yourselves unworthy of eternal life, behold, we are turning to the Gentiles." And the Gentiles rejoiced and glorified the word of the Lord, and as many as were appointed to eternal life believed, and the word spread throughout the whole region. The seed the Lord had once sown in His parables, that had seemed to fall on so many hard and thorny hearts, was now bearing its hundredfold harvest among peoples we had been raised to think outside the very reach of God's mercy.`,
`When this sermon was repeated to me, I noticed that Paul preached the same gospel to the synagogue at Pisidian Antioch that Peter had preached at Pentecost and that the Lord Himself had opened to us from Moses and the Prophets: that the Christ must suffer and rise, and that through Him forgiveness of sins is offered freely to all who believe. It was always the same gospel, in every mouth and every city—not Peter's gospel or Paul's gospel or John's, but the one gospel of the grace of God in the crucified and risen Christ. I have learned to be wary of any teacher who brings some new and different message, some deeper secret beyond this; for there is no gospel beyond the cross and the empty tomb, and the moment a man begins to preach himself, or some novelty of his own, he has left the only road that leads home. The treasure was never new or clever; it was always Christ, and Him crucified, and risen, and offered to sinners.`,
`§§ Acts 14:1-28`,
`At Iconium they went together into the Jewish synagogue and spoke in such a way that a great number of both Jews and Greeks believed, and they remained a long time, speaking boldly for the Lord, who bore witness to the word of His grace by granting signs and wonders to be done by their hands. But the city was divided, as it always was where Christ was truly preached, some siding with the Jews and some with the apostles, until an attempt was made to stone them and they fled to Lystra and Derbe.`,
`At Lystra there sat a man crippled in his feet from birth, who had never walked. He listened as Paul spoke, and Paul, looking at him and seeing that he had faith to be made well, said in a loud voice, "Stand upright on your feet." And the man sprang up and began to walk. But the crowds, when they saw it, lifted up their voices in the Lycaonian tongue, crying that the gods had come down to them in the likeness of men, and they called Barnabas Zeus and Paul Hermes, and the priest of Zeus brought oxen and garlands, meaning to offer sacrifice to them. When the apostles heard of it they tore their garments and rushed into the crowd, crying, "Men, why are you doing these things? We also are men, of like nature with you, and we bring you good news, that you should turn from these vain things to a living God." Even so they scarcely restrained the people from sacrificing to them. And then, in the sudden and terrible turning that crowds are so capable of, Jews came from Antioch and Iconium and won the people over, and they stoned Paul and dragged him out of the city, supposing him dead. But the disciples gathered about him, and he rose up and went on—the very man who had once held the coats of those who stoned Stephen now left for dead under a hail of stones for the name he had once tried to destroy.`,
`On their way back they passed again through Lystra and Iconium and Antioch, strengthening the souls of the disciples and encouraging them to continue in the faith, telling them, "Through many tribulations we must enter the kingdom of God." And they appointed elders in every church, and with prayer and fasting committed them to the Lord in whom they had believed. Then they sailed back to Antioch, where they had first been commended to the grace of God for the work they had now fulfilled, and they gathered the church together and declared all that God had done with them, and how He had opened a door of faith to the Gentiles. I drank in every word of their report when it reached us. The Shepherd who had once gone after a single lost sheep in the hills of Galilee was now gathering whole flocks out of the nations—and He was doing it through a man who had once hunted His sheep to the death, so that every city Paul entered was itself a standing proof that the Lord came to seek and to save the lost, and that no sinner is beyond the reach of the grace that had seized him.`,
`That word Paul and Barnabas spoke to the young disciples on their return has stayed with me through all my own afflictions: "Through many tribulations we must enter the kingdom of God." They did not promise the new believers an easy road; they had themselves just been stoned and left for dead, and they hid nothing of the cost. I have seen much harm done by those who promise that the followers of Christ will be spared trouble and given ease and prosperity in this life, for when the trouble comes, as it surely does, such believers are shaken and fall away, thinking God has failed them. But the Lord Himself told us plainly that in the world we would have tribulation, and that whoever would come after Him must take up his cross. The crown is real, and it is coming; but the road to it runs through the tribulation, as the road to the Lord's own crown ran through Gethsemane and Golgotha. The servant is not greater than his Master.`,
`§§ The Letter of James`,
`It was in these years, I believe, before the great question of the Gentiles was settled, that the first of the letters began to pass from hand to hand among us—the letter of James, the Lord's own brother, who had become a pillar of the church in Jerusalem. I knew James well. I had watched him grow from one of the brothers who did not believe, who once thought the Lord beside Himself, into a man so given to prayer that those who knew him said his knees had grown calloused like a camel's from the hours he spent on them in the Temple. His letter was plain and pointed and unadorned, full of the salt and savor of his brother's own teaching, and to read it was like hearing the Sermon on the Mount again in a new and homely voice.`,
`He wrote of counting it all joy when we meet trials of various kinds, knowing that the testing of our faith produces steadfastness, and that the steadfast man will receive the crown of life. He wrote that every good and perfect gift comes down from the Father of lights, with whom there is no shadow of turning, and that of His own will He brought us forth by the word of truth—our new birth, like everything else, His doing and not ours. He charged us to be doers of the word and not hearers only, deceiving ourselves; to be quick to hear and slow to speak and slow to anger; to bridle the tongue, that small fire that can set a whole forest ablaze; to seek the wisdom from above, which is pure, peaceable, gentle, full of mercy and good fruits. He wrote hard, scorching words to the rich who hoarded their wealth and defrauded the laborers in their fields, and tender words about visiting orphans and widows in their affliction, which he called religion pure and undefiled before God. And he insisted, in words that some have thought stood at odds with Paul, that faith without works is dead—that a faith which sees a brother naked and hungry and says only "Go in peace, be warmed and filled" without giving him what he needs cannot save anyone, for it is no more alive than a body without a spirit.`,
`I never heard James and Paul as enemies, though, however much some have tried to set them against each other. Paul told us how a sinner is first set right before God—by faith alone, apart from any works of the law, lest any man should boast. James told us what a living faith looks like once it has been planted in a heart—that it cannot help but put out the fruit of mercy and good works, as surely as a healthy tree cannot help but bear. The one was speaking of the root, the other of the fruit, and both were pointing to the very same tree, planted by the very same grace. For a faith that had truly received the mercy I had watched poured out from the cross could no more sit idle while a brother starved than that forgiven servant in the Lord's parable could rightly seize his fellow by the throat over a hundred denarii. The mercy we received was never meant to be hoarded; it was meant to flow, and a faith through which it does not flow is no faith at all, but a dead and empty profession.`,
`I have heard men set James against Paul as though they were two teachers at war, but I knew them both, and I tell you they were of one mind, like two watchmen guarding the same treasure from thieves who came at it from opposite sides. Paul guarded the gospel from those who would add their works to the finished work of Christ, and so he thundered that we are justified by faith alone, apart from works. James guarded that same gospel from those who would claim a faith that changed nothing, that left the heart cold and the hands closed and the brother still hungry; and so he insisted that a faith which produces no works is dead, no faith at all. The one said: you cannot be saved by your works. The other said: but the faith that saves you will not leave you without works. Both were defending the very same grace—the grace that justifies the ungodly freely, and then so changes them that they cannot help but love.`,
`§§ Galatians`,
`Not long after, another letter came, this one from Paul to the churches of Galatia that he had founded on that first journey, and it carried an urgency, even a holy heat, that startled me when I first heard it read aloud. For certain men had come among those young Gentile believers after Paul had gone, insisting that to be truly saved they must be circumcised and keep the law of Moses—that Christ alone was not enough, that grace needed the law's help to finish the work it had begun. Paul was astonished that they were so quickly deserting the One who had called them in grace and turning to a different gospel, which was no gospel at all; and he wrote that if anyone, even an angel from heaven, should preach a gospel contrary to the one they had received, let him be accursed.`,
`He set the truth down more sharply than I had ever heard it put. "We know," he wrote, "that a person is not justified by works of the law but through faith in Jesus Christ, so we also have believed in Christ Jesus, in order to be justified by faith in Christ and not by works of the law, because by works of the law no one will be justified." And he pressed it to its sharpest edge: "If righteousness were through the law, then Christ died for no purpose." He told the story of his own conversion, the persecutor seized by grace on the Damascus road, and how he had not received his gospel from men but from the Lord Himself; and how he had withstood even Peter to his face at Antioch when Peter, fearing the circumcision party, had drawn back from eating with the Gentile believers, for the very truth of the gospel was at stake in that table—whether we are accepted by God on the ground of our pedigree and our performance, or on the ground of Christ alone. And he wrote the words that I have carried as my own confession ever since, that I have whispered to myself in the dark and that I should like to die saying: "I have been crucified with Christ. It is no longer I who live, but Christ who lives in me. And the life I now live in the flesh I live by faith in the Son of God, who loved me and gave Himself for me."`,
`I read that and wept, for it gathered up everything—the cup He sweated over in the garden, the cross where He bore my forsakenness and cried out into the darkness, the stone rolled away, the breakfast on the beach, the Spirit poured into my heart at Pentecost—into one short sentence that a man could die clinging to. The law, Paul said, had been our guardian, like a stern tutor set over a child, until Christ came; but now that faith had come we were no longer under a guardian, for we were all sons of God through faith, and if sons, then heirs—and an heir does not earn his inheritance by his labor, he receives it because of who his father is. To add anything at all to the finished work of the cross, even the smallest work of our own, was not to improve the gospel but to insult it, to say that the blood of the Son had not been quite enough. Christ had set us free for freedom; and Paul, who had once been the most zealous slave of the law alive, would not stand by and watch anyone slip back into the yoke of trying to save himself, when the saving had already been done.`,
`I have noticed how fiercely Paul wrote in this letter, with none of his usual thanksgiving at the start, only astonishment and warning; and I have come to understand why. It was because the very gospel was at stake. There is a kind of error that is only a stumble, and the Lord is patient with it; but there is a kind that strikes at the root, that adds something of our own to the cross and so empties the cross of its power, and against that Paul would not yield for a moment, no, not even to an angel from heaven. For if we must finish by our own works what Christ began by His grace, then we are thrown back upon ourselves, and the good news is no news at all, only the old despair of trying to save ourselves. I have learned from Paul's holy anger to hold this one thing above all others: that salvation is by grace, through faith, in Christ alone, plus nothing; and that to add to it is not to enrich it but to destroy it.`,
`§§ Acts 15:1-35`,
`The dispute that Paul's letter to the Galatians had answered came at last to a head in Jerusalem, and I was there for it. Men had come down from Judea to Antioch and were teaching the brothers, "Unless you are circumcised according to the custom of Moses, you cannot be saved." Paul and Barnabas had no small disagreement and debate with them, and so the church appointed that they, with some others, should go up to Jerusalem to the apostles and elders about this question. I count that gathering one of the most important I ever witnessed, for the gospel itself was on the scales, and whichever way the council leaned would shape the faith for every generation after us.`,
`There was much debate. Then Peter rose and reminded the assembly how in the early days God had chosen that the Gentiles should hear the word of the gospel through his mouth, in the house of Cornelius, and how God, who knows the heart, had borne witness to them by giving them the Holy Spirit just as He had to us, and had made no distinction between us and them, having cleansed their hearts by faith. "Now, therefore," Peter said, "why are you putting God to the test by placing a yoke on the neck of the disciples that neither our fathers nor we have been able to bear? But we believe that we will be saved through the grace of the Lord Jesus, just as they will." The whole assembly fell silent and listened as Barnabas and Paul related the signs and wonders God had done through them among the Gentiles. And then James—the Lord's brother, whom all of us honored—gave the judgment, citing the prophets who had foretold that the Gentiles would seek the Lord: that we should not trouble those of the Gentiles who turned to God, but write to them only to abstain from a few things that would needlessly wound their Jewish brothers, so that the two might live as one family at one table.`,
`I have pondered Peter's words ever since, for there is a whole world in them. He did not say the Gentiles would be saved in the same way we Jews were; he said the very opposite—that we will be saved through the grace of the Lord Jesus, just as they will. We Jews, with all our law and our covenants and our circumcision and our long inheritance of promises, would be saved by the very same grace, through the very same faith, as the uncircumcised pagans of Antioch and Caesarea. The council might have hung the heavy yoke of the law upon the necks of the Gentiles; instead it confessed before God that there is only one way of salvation for any soul that has ever lived, and that way is grace alone. It was the truth of the parable of the two sons spoken aloud in a council chamber: both sons, the law-keeping elder brother and the wandering younger one, come into the father's feast only by the father's mercy, and neither of them earns his place at the table. The church sent a letter of freedom back to Antioch, and when it was read aloud to the gathered believers, they rejoiced at the encouragement, for the gospel of grace had been guarded, and the door to the nations had been held open.`,
`I have thought since that the whole future of the church hung on the judgment of that council, more than any of us fully knew at the time. Had they decided that the Gentiles must take up the law of Moses to be saved, the gospel of free grace would have been buried under the old yoke, and the good news would have become merely a new and heavier set of rules. But the Spirit guided them to confess the one thing that matters: that there is one way of salvation for all, Jew and Gentile alike, and that way is grace through faith. I have watched the church, in the generations since, tempted again and again to add some new requirement, some mark or work or rule by which men must earn their standing; and every time, the council at Jerusalem stands as a witness against it. The yoke we could not bear has been carried for us. We are saved, every one of us, the same way—by the grace of the Lord Jesus.`,
`§§ Acts 15:36-16:10`,
`After some days Paul said to Barnabas, "Let us return and visit the brothers in every city where we proclaimed the word of the Lord, and see how they are." Barnabas wanted to take John Mark along, but Paul thought it best not to take one who had deserted them in Pamphylia and had not gone with them to the work. The disagreement between them grew so sharp that they parted ways; Barnabas took Mark and sailed to Cyprus, while Paul chose Silas and went through Syria and Cilicia, strengthening the churches. It grieved me to hear of the rift between two such men, who had labored and suffered side by side; yet I have lived long enough to see the Lord weave even our quarrels into His purposes, for now there were two missions where there had been one, and Mark, restored in time, became useful again and gave us, they say, the first written account of the Lord's life. The Lord does not need us to be flawless to use us; He works through cracked and quarreling vessels, as He always has.`,
`At Lystra Paul found a disciple named Timothy, the son of a believing Jewish woman and a Greek father, well spoken of by the brothers, and he took him along, and so the second journey gathered its company. They went through the region of Phrygia and Galatia, having been kept by the Holy Spirit from speaking the word in Asia, and when they attempted to go into Bithynia, the Spirit of Jesus did not allow them. So they passed by Mysia and came down to Troas, on the very edge of the continent, and there in the night Paul saw a vision: a man of Macedonia standing and pleading with him, "Come over to Macedonia and help us." And immediately we sought to go on into Macedonia, concluding that God had called us to preach the gospel to them. So the good news that had begun in a small Galilean village, by a lake among fishermen, crossed the sea into Europe, carried by a handful of men who went where the Spirit drove them, not where they had planned.`,
`§§ Acts 16:11-40`,
`Setting sail from Troas, they came to Philippi, a leading city of that district of Macedonia and a Roman colony. On the Sabbath they went outside the gate to the riverside, where they supposed there was a place of prayer, and sat down and spoke to the women who had gathered there. One of them, named Lydia, a seller of purple goods from the city of Thyatira, a worshiper of God, listened, and the Lord opened her heart to pay attention to what was said by Paul. She was baptized, and her household with her, and she prevailed upon them to come and stay at her house. I have always loved that small phrase—the Lord opened her heart—for it tells the secret of every conversion I have ever witnessed, including my own. Paul spoke the words, but it was the Lord who opened the heart to receive them, as He had once opened my eyes by the Jordan when I thought I was finding Him. The first believer on that whole continent of Europe was a woman won not by clever argument but by the quiet sovereign work of God upon her heart.`,
`There too a slave girl with a spirit of divination, who brought her owners much gain by fortune-telling, followed Paul and us, crying out, "These men are servants of the Most High God, who proclaim to you the way of salvation." When Paul, greatly annoyed, turned and commanded the spirit to come out of her in the name of Jesus Christ, it came out that very hour. But when her owners saw that their hope of gain was gone, they seized Paul and Silas and dragged them before the magistrates, and the crowd joined in attacking them, and the magistrates had them beaten with rods and thrown into prison, their feet fastened in the stocks. About midnight, however, as Paul and Silas were praying and singing hymns to God, and the other prisoners were listening, suddenly there was a great earthquake, so that the foundations of the prison were shaken, and immediately all the doors were opened and everyone's bonds were unfastened. The jailer, waking and seeing the prison doors open, drew his sword and was about to kill himself, supposing the prisoners had escaped. But Paul cried with a loud voice, "Do not harm yourself, for we are all here." And the jailer called for lights and rushed in, and trembling with fear he fell down before Paul and Silas and brought them out and asked the one question that finally matters above every other: "Sirs, what must I do to be saved?" And they said, "Believe in the Lord Jesus, and you will be saved, you and your household." That very night he washed their wounds and was baptized at once, he and all his family, and he set food before them and rejoiced that he had believed in God.`,
`I have always loved that scene, for it holds the whole gospel pressed into a single night. A man with a sword already at his own throat, ready to die in despair, asks how he can be saved, and the answer is not a list of works to perform or a yoke of laws to take up, not years of penance or a lifetime of proving himself—but a Person to trust: believe in the Lord Jesus. The very same answer that had saved the dying thief who could do nothing but ask to be remembered, that had justified the tax collector who beat his breast and begged for mercy, that had caught Peter sinking beneath the waves when he cried, "Lord, save me." Believe, and be saved, you and your household. And the song that the two men had been singing with their backs torn and bleeding in the dark, before ever the earthquake came, told me that they had already found a freedom that no prison and no stocks could touch—the freedom of men who knew themselves saved, and could not stop singing about it even in chains.`,
`I have set the two conversions of Philippi side by side many times—Lydia, the prosperous dealer in purple, whose heart the Lord opened quietly as she listened by the river, and the jailer, a rough man with blood on his hands, brought to his knees by an earthquake in the dead of night. The one came gently, the other violently; the one was a seeking worshiper of God, the other a despairing man with a sword at his own throat. And yet they were saved in precisely the same way, by the same Lord, through the same simple word: believe. There is no single road by which men come to Christ—some He draws softly over long years, some He seizes in a moment of crisis—but there is only one door they all come through, and that door is faith in the Lord Jesus. I have learned never to doubt a conversion because it did not look like my own; the Lord opens hearts as He pleases, but it is always He who opens them.`,
`§§ Acts 17:1-15`,
`When they had passed through Amphipolis and Apollonia, they came to Thessalonica, where there was a synagogue, and for three Sabbaths Paul reasoned with them from the Scriptures, explaining and proving that it was necessary for the Christ to suffer and to rise from the dead—the very thing the Lord Himself had opened to us from Moses and all the Prophets after His resurrection—and saying, "This Jesus, whom I proclaim to you, is the Christ." Some of them were persuaded and joined Paul and Silas, as did a great many of the devout Greeks and not a few of the leading women. But the Jews, moved by jealousy, gathered a mob, set the city in an uproar, and attacked the house of Jason, where the missionaries had lodged, dragging some of the brothers before the city authorities and shouting, "These men who have turned the world upside down have come here also." The accusation was truer than the accusers knew. The brothers sent Paul and Silas away by night to Berea, where the people were more noble than those in Thessalonica, for they received the word with all eagerness, examining the Scriptures daily to see if these things were so. Many of them therefore believed. I have always admired the Bereans, who would not take even Paul's word for it without searching the Scriptures themselves—for the gospel does not fear examination; it was written in the Law and the Prophets long before it was preached in the synagogues, and the more closely a man searches, the more surely he finds the Lord on every page.`,
`I have admired the Bereans above almost any in this whole account, for they did the thing that I have come to think the very mark of an honest soul: they would not take even Paul's word for it, eloquent and Spirit-filled as he was, but searched the Scriptures daily to see whether these things were so. The gospel does not ask to be believed blindly, nor does it fear examination; it was written in the Law and the Prophets centuries before it was preached in the synagogues, and the more closely a man searches them, the more surely he finds the Lord on every page. I have warned many against the teacher who bids you simply trust him and not look too closely; that is the way of the deceiver. The true word of God invites the lamp and the long study, and grows only brighter under it. Search, and you will find Him there, as the Bereans did, and your faith will rest not on a man's eloquence but on the word of God that cannot be broken.`,
`§§ Acts 17:16-34`,
`Now while Paul was waiting for Silas and Timothy at Athens, his spirit was provoked within him as he saw that the city was full of idols. So he reasoned in the synagogue with the Jews, and in the marketplace every day with whoever happened to be there, until some of the Epicurean and Stoic philosophers brought him to the Areopagus, wanting to hear this new teaching. And Paul stood in the midst of the Areopagus, among the altars and the statues and the proud wisdom of the Greeks, and said, "Men of Athens, I perceive that in every way you are very religious. For as I passed along and observed the objects of your worship, I found also an altar with this inscription: To the unknown god. What therefore you worship as unknown, this I proclaim to you." He told them of the God who made the world and everything in it, who does not live in temples made by hands nor is served by human hands as though He needed anything, since He Himself gives to all mankind life and breath and everything; who made from one man every nation, that they should seek Him and perhaps feel their way toward Him and find Him, though He is not far from any one of us, for in Him we live and move and have our being. "The times of ignorance God overlooked," Paul said, "but now He commands all people everywhere to repent, because He has fixed a day on which He will judge the world in righteousness by a man whom He has appointed, and of this He has given assurance to all by raising Him from the dead." Now when they heard of the resurrection of the dead, some mocked, but others said, "We will hear you again about this." Some men joined him and believed, among them Dionysius the Areopagite and a woman named Damaris. I marveled, when the account reached me, at how Paul preached the same crucified and risen Christ to the philosophers of Athens as to the synagogues of Judea, neither flattering their wisdom nor borrowing it, but calling even the cleverest men in the world to do the one thing every soul must do—to repent and turn to the living God, and to reckon with the man God raised from the dead, before whom they would all one day stand.`,
`I have admired how Paul stood among the philosophers of Athens and neither flattered their wisdom nor was cowed by it. He met them on their own ground, quoting their own poets, reasoning from the altar to their unknown god, and yet he bent not one inch on the things that mattered: that God commands all men everywhere to repent, and that He has appointed a day of judgment by the man He raised from the dead. He did not trim the gospel to please the clever, nor leave out the resurrection because he knew they would mock it—and mock it they did. I have learned from him that we are to be wise in how we speak to each kind of hearer, becoming all things to all men that we might win some, but that there is a core we must never surrender to be thought reasonable: the cross, the resurrection, the call to repent. The world will always laugh at a risen Christ; we must preach Him anyway, for some, like Dionysius and Damaris, will believe.`,
`§§ 1 & 2 Thessalonians`,
`From Corinth, soon after, Paul wrote back to the young church in Thessalonica that he had been forced to leave so quickly under cover of night—and these, so far as I have ever known, were the first of his letters to be set down. He wrote to comfort believers under heavy persecution, and to steady them in their grief, for some of their number had died, and the survivors feared that those who had fallen asleep had somehow missed the Lord's return and lost their share in the glory to come.`,
`Paul would not have them grieve as the rest do, who have no hope. "For since we believe that Jesus died and rose again," he wrote, "even so, through Jesus, God will bring with Him those who have fallen asleep. For the Lord Himself will descend from heaven with a cry of command, with the voice of an archangel and with the sound of the trumpet of God. And the dead in Christ will rise first. Then we who are alive, who are left, will be caught up together with them in the clouds to meet the Lord in the air, and so we will always be with the Lord. Therefore encourage one another with these words." And in a second letter, written soon after, he steadied them again, for some, thinking the day of the Lord had already come, had grown idle and alarmed; and he reminded them that the Lord would indeed come, and would grant relief to the afflicted and repay those who troubled them, and bade them stand firm and hold to what they had been taught.`,
`I read those letters as a man who had stood on the Mount of Olives and watched the cloud take Him out of my sight, and heard the two men in white promise that He would come again in the same way as we had seen Him go. The hope that Paul held out to the Thessalonians was the very hope I had carried down that hill and carried still: that the salvation begun at the cross and sealed at the empty tomb was not yet complete, that the Shepherd who had died for the sheep and risen and poured out His Spirit would come once more in glory to gather His own and to right every wrong, and that not even death could rob a single believer of that day. The grave had given Lazarus back at His word; the grave had given the Lord Himself back on the third morning; and the grave would surely give back all who slept in Him. So we did not grieve as the others did. We buried our dead in hope, and laid them down as men lay down seed, and we encouraged one another with these words, and we lifted our eyes and watched the sky.`,
`I have drawn more comfort from these letters in my old age than I can easily say, for I have buried many whom I loved, and I have felt the ache of the question the Thessalonians felt: what of those who fall asleep before He comes? Paul's answer is the answer I rest in as my own end draws near. We do not grieve as those who have no hope. The Lord who died and rose will bring with Him those who have fallen asleep in Him, and the dead in Christ will rise. I have stood at many gravesides now, and laid in the earth faces I shall not see again in this life, and I have learned to lay them down as a farmer lays down seed in the autumn ground—not throwing them away, but planting them, in the sure hope of a spring that is coming. The grave is not a wall but a door. I have watched the sky for Him since the day He ascended, and I watch it still, the more eagerly now that so many I love are waiting on the other side.`,
`§§ Acts 18:1-22`,
`After Athens, Paul went to Corinth, a great and restless port city, rich and crowded and sunk deep in its idols and its pleasures, where two seas met and the sailors of the world passed through. There he found a Jewish tentmaker named Aquila, lately come from Italy with his wife Priscilla, because Claudius had ordered all the Jews to leave Rome; and because Paul was of the same trade, he stayed and worked with them, and reasoned in the synagogue every Sabbath, trying to persuade Jews and Greeks. When Silas and Timothy arrived from Macedonia, Paul was occupied with the word, testifying that the Christ was Jesus; and when the Jews opposed and reviled him, he shook out his garments and turned to the Gentiles.`,
`And the Lord said to Paul one night in a vision, "Do not be afraid, but go on speaking and do not be silent, for I am with you, and no one will attack you to harm you, for I have many in this city who are my people." So he stayed a year and six months, teaching the word of God among them, and many of the Corinthians, hearing him, believed and were baptized—even Crispus, the ruler of the synagogue, with his entire household. I have held that word of the Lord close to my heart ever since I heard it, for it tells me how the gospel goes out into a city and takes root. "I have many in this city who are my people"—spoken before they had yet believed, while they were still pagans in a pagan city, worshiping idols and selling themselves in the temples. The Lord knew His sheep before ever the Shepherd's voice reached their ears; the harvest was already His and already counted before the first seed of preaching was sown. It was the same sovereign grace that had found me at the Jordan when I imagined I was finding Him, that had seen Nathanael under the fig tree before Philip ever called him, that had watched the prodigal's road for the first sign of a returning son. Paul preached, and men believed; and yet beneath all the preaching ran a deeper and quieter current—the Father giving His chosen people to the Son, calling to Himself those who were far off, so that no one who labors in the gospel can ever boast, for the increase is God's alone. When at last the Jews made a united attack on Paul and brought him before the proconsul Gallio, Gallio refused to judge a matter of their own law, and so Paul was left free to finish his work; and then, after some time, he sailed for Syria with Priscilla and Aquila, and came in time back to Antioch where it had all begun.`,
`That word the Lord spoke to Paul in the night at Corinth—"I have many in this city who are my people"—has steadied me whenever the work seemed fruitless and the soil hard. For it told me that the harvest does not depend on the eloquence of the preacher or the openness of the city, but on the prior choice of God, who has His people even in the most corrupt of places, and will surely bring them in. Corinth was a byword for vice, a city given over to every idolatry and every appetite; and yet the Lord had many there who were His, and they came. I have learned not to write off any place or any person as beyond hope, for I do not know whom the Lord has chosen, and I have seen Him gather His own out of the unlikeliest of cities and the hardest of hearts. We sow and we water; but it is God who has the people, and God who gives the increase, and so we labor on in hope even where the ground looks barren.`,
`§§ Acts 18:23-19:7`,
`After spending some time in Antioch, Paul set out again, passing through the region of Galatia and Phrygia in order, strengthening all the disciples—and so the third of his great journeys began. Now in those days there came to Ephesus a Jew named Apollos, a native of Alexandria, an eloquent man, mighty in the Scriptures. He had been instructed in the way of the Lord, and being fervent in spirit he spoke and taught accurately the things concerning Jesus, though he knew only the baptism of John. When Priscilla and Aquila heard him speaking boldly in the synagogue, they took him aside and explained to him the way of God more accurately, and he went on to be a great help to those who through grace had believed, powerfully refuting the Jews in public and showing by the Scriptures that the Christ was Jesus. I have always been moved that it was a tentmaker and his wife, two ordinary believers, who quietly took the brilliant Alexandrian aside and filled out what was lacking in his understanding—for the Lord builds His church not only through apostles and great preachers but through humble saints who know a little more of the truth and share it gently with those who know a little less.`,
`While Apollos was at Corinth, Paul came to Ephesus and found there some disciples who had been baptized into John's baptism but had not so much as heard that there was a Holy Spirit. When Paul had explained the gospel more fully and baptized them in the name of the Lord Jesus, and laid his hands on them, the Holy Spirit came on them, and they spoke in tongues and prophesied—the same gift again, the same seal of the same salvation, given now to a handful of men on the far western edge of the world, just as it had been given at Pentecost in Jerusalem and at Caesarea in the house of Cornelius. I have noticed how the Spirit kept falling in this way, in city after city, on Jew and Samaritan and Gentile alike, always the same gift, as if God meant to leave no room for any people to imagine themselves first or favored. The salvation was one, the Spirit was one, the Lord was one, and all who came, came in the same way and received the same grace.`,
`I have always been moved that it was Priscilla and Aquila, two ordinary tentmakers, who took the brilliant Apollos quietly aside and filled out what was lacking in his understanding. They did not shame him publicly, nor envy his gifts, nor stand off in fear of his learning; they simply, lovingly, in private, showed him the way of God more accurately, and so the church gained one of its greatest preachers. I have learned from them that the Lord builds His church not only through apostles and famous men but through humble saints who know a little more of the truth and share it gently with those who know a little less. Many a soul has been set right not by a sermon from a pulpit but by a quiet word from a faithful friend over a shared meal. We need not be eloquent to be useful; we need only to know the Lord, and to love our brother enough to lead him deeper in.`,
`§§ Acts 19:8-41`,
`Paul entered the synagogue at Ephesus and spoke boldly for three months, and then, when some grew stubborn, he withdrew and reasoned daily in the hall of a man named Tyrannus, and this continued for two years, so that all the residents of Asia, both Jews and Greeks, heard the word of the Lord. And God did extraordinary miracles by the hands of Paul, so that even handkerchiefs and aprons that had touched his skin were carried to the sick, and their diseases left them and the evil spirits came out—the same power that I had once watched flow from the very hem of the Lord's garment in the streets of Galilee, flowing still through the cloth that touched His servant. Some itinerant Jewish exorcists, the seven sons of a man named Sceva, tried to use the name of Jesus as if it were a charm, commanding the spirits "by the Jesus whom Paul proclaims"; but the evil spirit answered, "Jesus I know, and Paul I recognize, but who are you?" and the man in whom the spirit was leaped on them and overpowered them, so that they fled out of the house naked and wounded. Fear fell on all the residents of Ephesus, and the name of the Lord Jesus was extolled. And many who had practiced magic arts brought their costly books together and burned them in the sight of all, and they counted the value of them and found it to be fifty thousand pieces of silver. A great fire of repentance, fifty thousand pieces of silver gone up in smoke and ash, because the men who had paid dearly for those dark arts now counted the freedom Christ gave them as worth more than all of it. I thought of the man freed of his legion in the country of the Gerasenes, and of these books burning in the streets of Ephesus, and I saw the same truth shining in both: that salvation is not only the forgiving of sin but the breaking of every chain, even the chains a man has spent his whole fortune to forge for himself. To be saved is to be set free.`,
`So mightily did the word of the Lord grow and prevail that the silversmiths of Ephesus, who made silver shrines for the goddess Artemis and grew rich by them, began to fear for their trade. A man named Demetrius gathered the craftsmen and stirred them up, crying that this Paul was persuading people that gods made with hands are not gods at all, and that not only their trade but the temple of the great Artemis was in danger. The whole city was filled with confusion, and they rushed into the great theater shouting for some two hours, "Great is Artemis of the Ephesians!" until at last the town clerk quieted them and dismissed the assembly. It did not surprise me, when I heard of it, that the gospel had made enemies of the men who profited from the old darkness. The light always divides; I had seen it from the very beginning, on the Lord's own roads. Where Christ is truly preached, the idols lose their customers, the fortune-tellers lose their gain, and the men who sell darkness grow afraid—for the kingdom of God advances by setting captives free, and there is always someone who was making his living off the chains.`,
`I have often thought of those costly books burning in the streets of Ephesus, fifty thousand pieces of silver curling into smoke and ash, and of what it must have cost those men to set them alight. They were not throwing away worthless things; they were burning a fortune, the very tools of their old trade and the source of their old power. But they had found something worth more, and they would not keep one foot in the old darkness while they walked in the new light. I have seen that this is what true repentance looks like—not merely a sorrow for sin, but a decisive turning away from it, a burning of the bridges back to the old life, however dear and costly those bridges may be. The Lord does not ask us to set down our chains reluctantly, keeping the key in our pocket; He gives us such a sight of His worth that we fling the chains gladly into the fire, counting all that we lose as rubbish compared to gaining Him.`,
`§§ 1 Corinthians`,
`From Ephesus, in those two years, Paul wrote to the church at Corinth, that beloved and deeply troubled flock he had labored a year and a half to gather, for reports had reached him of quarrels and pride and grave sins among them. His letter is long and searching and ranges over many things—divisions and lawsuits, marriage and food offered to idols, the gifts of the Spirit and the ordering of worship—but its foundation is laid in the opening pages, and that foundation has steadied me through many a storm. They were dividing into parties, boasting in their favorite teachers, one saying "I follow Paul," another "I follow Apollos," another "I follow Cephas," and puffing themselves up in their own wisdom and eloquence. Paul cut straight through all of it to the cross. "For the word of the cross is folly to those who are perishing," he wrote, "but to us who are being saved it is the power of God." He had not come to them, he reminded them, with lofty speech or wisdom, but had resolved to know nothing among them except Jesus Christ and Him crucified, lest the cross be emptied of its power by clever words.`,
`I have turned that over many times—that the very thing the world counts foolish and weak, a Savior nailed to a Roman cross like a slave or a criminal, is in truth the wisdom and the power of God. The Greeks wanted clever arguments and the Jews demanded signs from heaven, but God was pleased to save the world through the folly of the cross, choosing what is low and despised in the world, even things that are not, to bring to nothing the things that are, so that no human being might boast in His presence. It was the lesson of the whole gospel set down as doctrine: that we are saved by what we could never have devised and never could deserve, so that all our boasting is emptied out and only one boast remains, that we boast in the Lord. Paul wrote to them of the Lord's Supper, repeating the very words I had heard with my own ears in the upper room—the bread that is His body, the cup of the new covenant in His blood—and warned them to discern that holy table rightly and not to profane it. He wrote that chapter on love that has been read at a thousand gatherings since, that though I speak with the tongues of men and of angels but have not love, I am only a noisy gong; that love is patient and kind, bears all things, believes all things, hopes all things, endures all things, and never ends. And toward the close he laid down the foundation of our whole hope as plainly as it has ever been written: that Christ died for our sins according to the Scriptures, that He was buried, that He was raised on the third day according to the Scriptures, and that He appeared to Cephas, then to the twelve, then to more than five hundred brothers at once, most of whom were still alive, and then to James, and last of all to Paul himself. I read his roll of witnesses and remembered, with a catch in my throat, that I was among that number—one of the company that had seen the risen Lord with our own eyes and touched Him with our own hands. And so I gave thanks that the hope on which I had staked my whole life was not a tale that we had spun out of our grief, but a thing that we had seen. For if Christ has not been raised, Paul wrote, our preaching is in vain and our faith is in vain, and we are still in our sins, and those who have fallen asleep have perished, and we are of all people most to be pitied. But in fact Christ has been raised from the dead, the firstfruits of those who have fallen asleep; and as in Adam all die, so also in Christ shall all be made alive; and the last enemy to be destroyed is death.`,
`I have read this letter many times, and what strikes me most is that Paul answered a whole tangle of problems—divisions and pride and lawsuits and disorder at the Lord's table and confusion about the resurrection—by leading them back, again and again, to the cross. The Corinthians were enamored of wisdom and eloquence and spiritual showmanship, and Paul kept setting before them a crucified Savior, foolishness to the Greeks, a stumbling block to the Jews, but the wisdom and power of God to those being saved. I have come to see that nearly every sickness in a church can be traced to a forgetting of the cross, and nearly every cure is found in returning to it. Where men glory in their own wisdom or gifts or standing, the cross humbles them, for it says that we were saved by what we could never have devised and never could deserve. There is no boasting at the foot of the cross. And a people who truly remember how they were saved cannot long remain proud, or divided, or cold.`,
`§§ 2 Corinthians`,
`Paul's next letter to Corinth that reached us was the most tender and the most wounded of all his writings, poured out of a heart that had been through great affliction in Asia, where, as he said, he had been so utterly burdened beyond his strength that he despaired even of life itself, and had learned through it to rely not on himself but on God who raises the dead. He wrote of the comfort with which God comforts us in all our affliction, so that we may comfort others with the comfort we ourselves have received. He wrote of the surpassing treasure we carry in jars of clay—the light of the knowledge of the glory of God in the face of Jesus Christ, held in vessels as frail and breakable as our own mortal bodies, so that it might be plain to all that the surpassing power belongs to God and not to us. He wrote of being afflicted but not crushed, perplexed but not driven to despair, struck down but not destroyed; of an eternal weight of glory beyond all comparison being prepared for us through what he called our light and momentary affliction, while we look not to the things that are seen but to the things that are unseen.`,
`But the words of his that have lodged the deepest in me, that I return to again and again, are those in which he laid bare the very mechanism of our rescue, the holy exchange at the heart of it all: "For our sake He made Him to be sin who knew no sin, so that in Him we might become the righteousness of God." I read that and I saw Golgotha again as if I stood beneath the cross once more. The One who knew no sin, the spotless Lamb, was made to be sin—my sin, and the sin of the world, laid upon His innocent and undeserving shoulders, until the forsakenness I had earned tore out of His lips in that cry into the darkness, My God, my God, why have you forsaken me. And in that same dread exchange, His righteousness was reckoned to me, so that I now stand before God clothed not in my own filthy rags but in the spotless robe of the Son. It is the greatest trade ever made in all the history of the world: He took what was mine, that I might receive what was His; He became what I was, that I might become what He is. Paul called this the ministry of reconciliation, and said that God was in Christ reconciling the world to Himself, not counting our trespasses against us, and that He had committed to us the message of this reconciliation, making us ambassadors for Christ, God making His appeal through us: "We implore you on behalf of Christ, be reconciled to God." And when Paul three times begged the Lord to take away a thorn in his flesh, a messenger of Satan sent to harass him, the answer he received has become my own comfort in every weakness and every grief: "My grace is sufficient for you, for my power is made perfect in weakness." So that even our frailty, our wounds, our thorns, become the very places where the grace of God is most clearly displayed, and we learn at last to boast not in our strength but in our weakness, that the power of Christ may rest upon us.`,
`I have grown to love this wounded letter most of all Paul's writings, for in it he hides nothing of his weakness—his afflictions, his despair, his tears, the thorn that would not leave him, the burden that pressed him beyond his strength. And it is precisely there, in his weakness laid bare, that the grace of God shines brightest. "My grace is sufficient for you, for my power is made perfect in weakness." I have found this to be the very pattern of the Christian life, and never more than in my old age, as my strength fails and my body breaks down like the jar of clay it always was. The treasure was never in the vessel; it was always in the One who fills it. And so I have learned to glory not in what I can do but in what I cannot, that the power may plainly be His and not mine. The world seeks to be strong; the Lord teaches His people to be content in weakness, that they may lean wholly on Him, who raises the dead.`,
`§§ Romans`,
`It was from Corinth, in the few months before he turned his face toward Jerusalem with the collection for the poor, that Paul wrote the longest and weightiest of all his letters, sending it ahead to the believers in Rome whom he had never yet met, to prepare them for his coming and to set before them the gospel in full. When a copy of it at last reached us, I read it slowly, again and again, late into the nights, for it set out in good order the whole of the gospel I had lived—from the very depth of our ruin to the very height of our hope. I had walked the story on the roads of Galilee and Judea; Paul had written it down as doctrine, link by link, and the two fit together like a hand into a glove made for it.`,
`He began with the bad news that alone makes the good news good: that the wrath of God is revealed against all ungodliness, that the Gentiles are without excuse for suppressing the truth and worshiping the creature rather than the Creator, and that the Jews, for all their law, are no better, for they break the very law in which they boast. All men, Jew and Greek alike, are under sin; none is righteous, no, not one; no one seeks for God; every mouth is stopped and the whole world is held accountable before Him; and by works of the law no human being will be justified in His sight, for through the law comes only the knowledge of sin. I knew the truth of every word of it in my own bones, for I had learned at the paralytic's torn-open roof in Capernaum that my deepest need was never my circumstances but my guilt, the sickness that no washing could reach. And then Paul turned the great corner into the heart of the matter, and I have never in all my life read words that gave me more joy than these: "But now the righteousness of God has been manifested apart from the law, although the Law and the Prophets bear witness to it—the righteousness of God through faith in Jesus Christ for all who believe. For there is no distinction: for all have sinned and fall short of the glory of God, and are justified by His grace as a gift, through the redemption that is in Christ Jesus, whom God put forward as a propitiation by His blood, to be received by faith."`,
`Justified by His grace as a gift. Not earned, not deserved, not paid for by anything in us, but given—the very thing I had watched happen with my own eyes a hundred times over: to the woman who wept on His feet and was told her sins were forgiven; to Zacchaeus, down out of his tree; to the thief at the last gasp, promised paradise that very day; to Saul, struck down on the Damascus road; and to me. Paul showed how Abraham himself was counted righteous not by working but by believing God, so that the promise might rest entirely on grace and be guaranteed to all his offspring. And he drew the blessed conclusion: that since we have been justified by faith, we have peace with God through our Lord Jesus Christ—peace, where once there had been only enmity and that great fixed chasm I had heard the Lord describe. He set Adam beside Christ, the one man whose disobedience made the many sinners against the one man whose obedience makes the many righteous, the trespass against the free gift, and showed that where sin increased, grace abounded all the more.`,
`I have read no letter more carefully than this one, nor returned to any more often, for in it Paul lays the whole foundation bare, stone by stone, and a man may build his soul upon it and not be moved. He begins where we least like to begin, with our ruin—that none is righteous, no, not one, that every mouth is stopped and the whole world stands guilty before God. I have known men who would not hear this, who thought themselves good enough, and they could find no joy in the gospel, for they did not know they needed it; the good news is only good to those who have first heard the bad. But once a man has truly seen that he is undone, that he cannot save himself, that the law can only condemn him—then the word "justified by His grace as a gift" falls on him like rain on cracked ground, and he understands at last why we call it good news. I had to be brought low at the paralytic's torn roof before I could be lifted up; and so must every soul that is saved.`,
`§§ Romans (continued)`,
`And then Paul rose, in the eighth chapter of that letter, to a height that I can still scarcely read aloud without trembling. "There is therefore now no condemnation for those who are in Christ Jesus." No condemnation—for the very ones who deserved nothing but condemnation, set free because the law of the Spirit of life had set them free from the law of sin and death, and because God had done what the law, weakened by our flesh, could never do, sending His own Son in the likeness of sinful flesh and condemning sin in the flesh, that the righteous requirement of the law might be fulfilled in us. He wrote that the Spirit Himself bears witness with our spirit that we are children of God, and if children, then heirs—heirs of God and fellow heirs with Christ; that the sufferings of this present time are not worth comparing with the glory that is to be revealed; that the whole creation groans as in the pains of childbirth, waiting for its redemption; and that the Spirit helps us in our weakness, interceding for us with groanings too deep for words. And he gave us the great chain that cannot be broken: that those whom God foreknew He predestined, and those whom He predestined He called, and those whom He called He justified, and those whom He justified He also glorified—every link forged by God, not one of them by us. "If God is for us," Paul asked, "who can be against us? He who did not spare His own Son but gave Him up for us all, how will He not also with Him graciously give us all things?" And he ended in a torrent of triumph that I have clung to in the darkest hours, declaring that neither death nor life, nor angels nor rulers, nor things present nor things to come, nor powers, nor height nor depth, nor anything else in all creation, will be able to separate us from the love of God in Christ Jesus our Lord.`,
`I had walked down from the Mount of Olives on the day He ascended, wondering in my heart how the rescue would ever hold, how a salvation entrusted to men as weak and faithless as we had proved ourselves to be could possibly endure to the end. And here in Paul's letter was the answer, written out in full and in order: it holds because it never rested upon us at all. It rested, from before the foundation of the world, upon the Father who chose us, and the Son who died for us, and the Spirit who seals us; and the God who did not spare His own Son will surely, having given us the greater gift, not fail to give us every lesser one along the way home. I had thought the chain might break at the weakest link, which was me. Paul showed me there was no human link in the chain at all.`,
`When I am afraid—and I have been afraid often in my long life, of men and of prisons and of death and most of all of my own weakness—I go back to the end of the eighth chapter and read it aloud to myself in the dark. Who shall separate us from the love of Christ? Neither death nor life, nor things present nor things to come, nor anything in all creation. I had wondered, on the day He ascended, how the rescue could ever hold, entrusted as it was to men as faithless as we had shown ourselves to be. And here is the answer that has held me through everything: it holds because it does not rest on us at all. The chain that Paul forges—foreknown, predestined, called, justified, glorified—has not a single human link in it; every link is the work of God, and God does not begin a work He will not finish. My salvation is as secure as the faithfulness of God Himself, and no power in heaven or earth or hell can pluck me from the hand that bought me. I had thought the weak link was me. There is no human link at all.`,
`§§ Acts 20:1-16`,
`After the uproar in Ephesus had ceased, Paul called the disciples together, encouraged them, and said farewell, and set out for Macedonia, giving the believers there much encouragement as he went, and so came to Greece, where he stayed three months. When a plot against him was discovered as he was about to sail for Syria, he decided to go back through Macedonia instead, and a company of brothers from many of the churches traveled with him, the firstfruits of the very nations he had labored to gather. At Troas we gathered on the first day of the week to break bread, and Paul, intending to depart the next day, talked on and on until midnight, the room warm and close with the smoke of many lamps. A young man named Eutychus, seated in an open window, sank into a deep sleep as Paul talked still longer, and overcome by sleep he fell down from the third story and was taken up dead. But Paul went down, bent over him, and taking him in his arms said, "Do not be alarmed, for his life is in him." And they took the lad away alive and were not a little comforted, and Paul went on talking until dawn and then departed. I smiled when I heard it, for it was the old pattern still at work, unchanged across all the years and miles—life called back through the arms of a servant, just as it had once come through the hands of the Lord at the bedside of Jairus' little girl. Death still let go its hold wherever the name of Jesus and the power of His Spirit were at work.`,
`When I heard of the boy Eutychus, taken up dead from beneath the window and given back alive, I smiled through my tears, for it was so plainly the old pattern still at work. “Tabitha, arise”; “Talitha, cumi”; “Lazarus, come out”; and now a boy in Troas, fallen in the long midnight of Paul's preaching, restored to his weeping friends. Death had been losing its grip, town by town and year by year, ever since the morning the stone was rolled away from the Lord's own tomb. Each of these raisings was but a small token, a firstfruit, a promise in miniature of the great resurrection to come, when the trumpet shall sound and all who sleep in Him shall rise. I have buried many; I shall soon be buried myself; but I lay down my dead, and shall lie down myself, in the certain hope of hearing that same voice say, at the last, “Arise.”`,
`§§ Acts 20:17-38`,
`From Miletus Paul sent to Ephesus and called the elders of the church to come to him, for he was hurrying to be at Jerusalem if possible by the day of Pentecost, and he would not take the time to go up to Ephesus itself. When they came, he gave them a farewell that those who heard it never forgot, and that moves me still whenever I think on it. He reminded them how he had served the Lord among them with all humility and with tears and with trials, how he had not shrunk from declaring anything that was profitable, teaching them in public and from house to house, testifying both to Jews and to Greeks of repentance toward God and of faith in our Lord Jesus Christ. And now, he told them, he was going to Jerusalem, bound in the Spirit, not knowing what would happen to him there, except that the Holy Spirit testified in every city that imprisonment and afflictions awaited him. "But I do not account my life of any value nor as precious to myself," he said, "if only I may finish my course and the ministry that I received from the Lord Jesus, to testify to the gospel of the grace of God."`,
`He charged the elders to pay careful attention to themselves and to all the flock, in which the Holy Spirit had made them overseers, to care for the church of God, which He obtained with His own blood. With His own blood—the price of the whole company of the saved, the ransom paid not in silver or gold but in the very life of God's own Son. He warned them that fierce wolves would come in among them, not sparing the flock, and that even from among their own number men would arise speaking twisted things to draw away disciples after them, and he commended them to God and to the word of His grace, which is able to build them up and to give them the inheritance among all those who are sanctified. Then he knelt down and prayed with them all, and there was much weeping, and they fell on Paul's neck and kissed him, grieving most of all over the word he had spoken, that they would not see his face again. And they accompanied him to the ship. I have thought often that there was much of the Lord Himself in that parting—a shepherd warning his sheep against the wolves, loving them to the very end, setting his face toward suffering for the sake of the gospel, exactly as the Master had set His face toward Jerusalem long before, knowing what waited for Him there and going anyway.`,
`I have wept over this farewell more than once, for there is in it the very heart of a true shepherd, and the very shadow of the Good Shepherd Himself. Paul knew he would not see their faces again; he was going up to chains and afflictions; and yet his concern was not for himself but for the flock, for the wolves that would come in among them, for the truth they must guard. He commended them not to his own care, which would soon be taken from them, but to God and to the word of His grace. I have learned from it how a faithful servant of Christ takes his leave: not clinging to his people as though they were his own, but giving them back to the One who bought them with His blood, trusting that the Lord who began the good work in them will keep it to the end, long after every earthly shepherd has been taken away.`,
`§§ Acts 21:1-16`,
`So we sailed away from them and came by Cos and Rhodes to Patara, and finding a ship crossing to Phoenicia we went aboard and set sail, sighting Cyprus and passing it on our left, and landed at Tyre. There we found the disciples and stayed seven days, and through the Spirit they kept telling Paul not to go up to Jerusalem; and when our days there were ended, we departed, and they all, with wives and children, accompanied us until we were outside the city, and we knelt down on the beach and prayed and said farewell to one another. We came at last to Caesarea, and stayed in the house of Philip the evangelist, the same Philip who had once preached in Samaria and baptized the Ethiopian on the desert road, now an old companion in the faith. While we were there, a prophet named Agabus came down from Judea, and taking Paul's belt he bound his own feet and hands with it and said, "Thus says the Holy Spirit, 'This is how the Jews at Jerusalem will bind the man who owns this belt and deliver him into the hands of the Gentiles.'"`,
`When we heard this, both we and the people there begged Paul with tears not to go up to Jerusalem. But Paul answered, "What are you doing, weeping and breaking my heart? For I am ready not only to be imprisoned but even to die in Jerusalem for the name of the Lord Jesus." And since he would not be persuaded, we ceased and said, "Let the will of the Lord be done." I recognized that surrender the moment the words were repeated to me, for I had heard its like before, in a garden among the olive trees on the night He was betrayed—not my will, but yours be done. The servant was walking the very path of the Master, his face set toward the city that kills the prophets, choosing the cup that was held out to him rather than the safety his friends pressed upon him. I had learned long since that this kind of obedience is not the absence of fear but the conquest of it by love, and that a man who can say "Let the will of the Lord be done" over his own chains has already won a freedom that no prison can take.`,
`§§ Acts 21:17-36`,
`When we came to Jerusalem, the brothers received us gladly, and the next day Paul went in with us to James, and all the elders were present, and Paul related one by one the things that God had done among the Gentiles through his ministry, and they glorified God when they heard it. But there were many thousands of Jewish believers, all zealous for the law, who had been told that Paul taught the Jews among the Gentiles to forsake Moses; and so, to show that there was no truth in it, Paul agreed to join four men in a vow and to pay their expenses in the Temple. But when the seven days were almost completed, the Jews from Asia, seeing Paul in the Temple, stirred up the whole crowd and laid hands on him, crying out, "Men of Israel, help! This is the man who is teaching everyone everywhere against the people and the law and this place"—for they supposed, falsely, that he had brought a Gentile into the Temple. The whole city was aroused, and the people ran together and seized Paul and dragged him out of the Temple, and at once the gates were shut, and they were beating him and trying to kill him when word came up to the Roman tribune that all Jerusalem was in confusion. He came down at once with soldiers and centurions, and the crowd, seeing the soldiers, stopped beating Paul; and the tribune arrested him and ordered him bound with two chains. The mob followed, crying out, "Away with him!"`,
`That cry was so like the one I had heard outside Pilate's hall, the very words the crowd had screamed against the Lord, that a cold shudder ran through me when the report reached me. The disciple was being handed over as his Lord had been—bound, beaten, shouted against by the very people he had come to bless, dragged out of the Temple by the guardians of the Temple. And yet, even as I grieved for him, I knew in my heart that this was not the end of the gospel but its road to Rome. The Lord had already told Paul plainly that he must bear witness also in Rome, and I had learned long before, at the foot of a cross that looked like the death of every hope, that what seems to the eye like the defeat of the word of God is so often the very means by which He sends it farther than it had ever gone. The chains the tribune fastened on Paul's wrists that day were, though none of us could yet see it, the gospel's passage to the heart of the empire.`,
`When the report of his arrest reached me, and I heard that the crowd had dragged him from the Temple crying "Away with him," I sat a long while in grief, for the words were the very words I had heard screamed against the Lord outside Pilate's hall. And I thought: it is happening again, the servant treading the path of the Master. But I have come to see that this is not the tragedy it appears, but the very shape of a life given wholly to Christ. The Lord told us that the servant is not greater than his Master, and that if they persecuted Him they would persecute us. To be handed over, bound, shouted against for His name is not to fall outside His care but to be conformed to His image, to share in His sufferings that we may share in His glory. I no longer pity those of us who are made to walk that road; I have learned to count it, as the apostles did, a thing to rejoice in.`,
`§§ Acts 21:37-22:29`,
`As Paul was about to be brought into the barracks, he asked the tribune leave to speak to the people, and standing on the steps he motioned with his hand, and when there was a great hush he addressed them in the Hebrew tongue. He told them the story of his own life—how he had been brought up in Jerusalem at the feet of Gamaliel, educated strictly in the law, zealous for God as they all were that day; how he had persecuted this Way to the death, binding and delivering to prison both men and women, as the high priest and the whole council of elders could testify. And then he told them of the road to Damascus, the light from heaven brighter than the noonday sun, the voice that named him, "Saul, Saul, why do you persecute me," and the answer, "I am Jesus of Nazareth, whom you are persecuting." He told of Ananias, a devout man according to the law, who had restored his sight and bidden him be baptized and wash away his sins, calling on the name of the Lord. They listened to him until he came to the word the Lord had spoken to him in a trance in the Temple: "Go, for I will send you far away to the Gentiles." At that word they lifted up their voices and cried, "Away with such a fellow from the earth! For he should not be allowed to live," and they shouted and threw off their cloaks and flung dust into the air. The tribune ordered him brought into the barracks and examined by flogging, to learn why they shouted so against him; but as they stretched him out, Paul said to the centurion standing by, "Is it lawful for you to flog a man who is a Roman citizen and uncondemned?" and they drew back in fear, and the tribune was alarmed to find that he had bound a citizen. I marked the strange providence in it: the very citizenship Paul had been born into, in a Gentile city far from Jerusalem, became the shield that preserved his life again and again on the road the Lord had appointed to Rome.`,
`I have noticed how Paul, given the chance to speak for his life, did not argue his innocence or plead for mercy, but told the story of how the Lord had laid hold of him on the Damascus road. Again and again, before mobs and governors and kings, his defense was simply his testimony, what he had been and what the grace of God had made of him. And I have come to see that there is no argument more powerful than a changed life honestly told. Men may dispute our doctrines and resist our reasonings, but they cannot easily explain away a man who once breathed murder against the saints and now would die for them. Paul's whole defense was the gospel written in his own flesh: I was the chief of sinners, and the Lord Jesus had mercy on me. I have learned that when we do not know what else to say for our faith, we may always say that, what we were, and what He has done.`,
`§§ Acts 22:30-23:11`,
`The next day, desiring to know the real reason the Jews accused him, the tribune brought Paul down and set him before the chief priests and all the council—the same Sanhedrin that had condemned the Lord to death. Paul, looking intently at them, said, "Brothers, I have lived my life before God in all good conscience up to this day." At that the high priest Ananias commanded those standing by to strike him on the mouth, and Paul answered, "God is going to strike you, you whitewashed wall! Are you sitting to judge me according to the law, and yet contrary to the law you order me to be struck?" But when he learned it was the high priest, he yielded, citing the Scripture that one must not speak evil of a ruler of the people. Then, perceiving that one part of the council were Sadducees and the other Pharisees, Paul cried out, "Brothers, I am a Pharisee, a son of Pharisees. It is with respect to the hope and the resurrection of the dead that I am on trial." At this a dissension arose between the two parties, for the Sadducees say there is no resurrection, nor angel, nor spirit, but the Pharisees acknowledge them all; and the assembly was divided, and the dispute became so violent that the tribune, fearing Paul would be torn to pieces, ordered the soldiers to take him by force out of their midst and bring him into the barracks. The following night the Lord stood by Paul and said, "Take courage, for as you have testified to the facts about me in Jerusalem, so you must testify also in Rome." I have held to those words ever since, for I had feared greatly for Paul's life in those days; but the Lord Himself stood by His servant in the dark of the cell and gave him not a promise of comfort but a promise of purpose—Rome—and no plot of men, however cunning, could turn Him from it. The bonds that looked to every eye like the end of Paul's work were the very means by which the gospel would be carried, under guard and in chains, to the capital of the world.`,
`§§ Acts 23:12-35`,
`When it was day, more than forty of the Jews bound themselves under an oath neither to eat nor drink until they had killed Paul, and they laid their plan before the chief priests, asking that Paul be brought down to the council again, so they might ambush and kill him on the way. But the son of Paul's sister heard of the ambush, and went and told Paul, who sent him to the tribune; and the tribune, learning of the plot, called two centurions and ordered a great guard prepared—two hundred soldiers, seventy horsemen, and two hundred spearmen—to take Paul safely down to Felix the governor at Caesarea by night, with a letter explaining the case. I marveled, when I heard the whole of it, at how the Lord wove even the malice of murderers into the carrying out of His own word. He had said Paul must reach Rome; and so the very plot meant to end his life became the reason he was hurried out of Jerusalem under the protection of nearly five hundred armed men, safe and surrounded, one long stage farther down the road the Lord had appointed for him. I have seen it again and again across my long life, and I never cease to wonder at it: what men intend for the death of the gospel, God bends into its advance. The cross itself had taught me that lesson once and for all—that the worst the enemies of God can do becomes, in His hands, the very instrument of the salvation they meant to stop.`,
`I have seen the hand of God in nothing more clearly than in the way He wove the murderous plot of those forty men into the safe carrying of Paul to Caesarea, surrounded by an army, one stage farther down the very road to Rome that the Lord had appointed. The men who swore not to eat until they had killed him became, all unwilling, the reason he was hurried out of their reach under guard. I have lived long enough to see this a hundred times: that what the enemies of God devise for the ruin of His servants, He turns to their preservation and to the advance of His purpose. Joseph's brothers meant evil and God meant it for good; the council meant to silence the Lord by the cross and instead set the whole world free. I have learned, therefore, not to despair at the schemes of wicked men, for there is no plot so cunning that the Lord cannot bend it, even against the schemers' will, into the doing of His own good pleasure.`,
`§§ Acts 24:1-27`,
`Five days later the high priest Ananias came down to Caesarea with some elders and a hired orator named Tertullus, who flattered the governor and laid out a smooth and lying accusation against Paul, calling him a plague, a stirrer-up of riots, a ringleader of the sect of the Nazarenes. But Paul answered for himself plainly, denying that he had stirred up any crowd, and confessing freely the one thing they truly held against him: "But this I confess to you, that according to the Way, which they call a sect, I worship the God of our fathers, believing everything laid down by the Law and written in the Prophets, having a hope in God, which these men themselves accept, that there will be a resurrection of both the just and the unjust." Felix, who had a rather accurate knowledge of the Way, put them off, saying he would decide when the tribune came down. Some days later he sent for Paul and listened to him speak about faith in Christ Jesus; and as Paul reasoned about righteousness and self-control and the coming judgment, Felix was alarmed and said, "Go away for the present. When I get an opportunity I will summon you." He hoped, besides, that money might be given him by Paul, and so he sent for him often and conversed with him. But that opportunity, so far as I ever learned, never came, and after two years, wishing to do the Jews a favor, Felix left Paul in prison.`,
`That trembling governor has stayed in my memory across all the years as a solemn warning. He felt the truth; he was alarmed by it; he saw plainly enough, when Paul spoke of righteousness and self-control and the judgment to come, that he himself stood condemned before God and needed the very mercy that his prisoner was offering him. And he sent the messenger away to a more convenient season that, so far as any of us ever knew, never came. I thought of the rich young ruler who went away sorrowful because he had great possessions, and of the seed that hears the word but is choked by the cares and pleasures of this life. It is a terrible thing to come so near the open door of salvation, to feel its very pull upon the heart, and then to turn back to one's silver and one's comfort and the next convenient hour. The gospel had reached Felix; the gospel had stood bound before him for two long years; and Felix would not come in. For not all who hear are saved—only those who, hearing, come, and Felix kept saying, soon, soon, until the door was shut.`,
`Felix has haunted me across the years as the very picture of the almost-saved, who are in the end the wholly lost. He trembled; he understood; he sent for Paul again and again; and he never once bent his knee. He was waiting, he said, for a convenient season—and I have learned that the convenient season is the great lie by which more souls are lost than by open unbelief. The man who flatly denies the gospel at least knows where he stands; but the man who means to repent later, who feels the truth and puts it off for a more convenient hour, is lulled to sleep at the very door of life and wakes to find it shut. The Spirit does not always strive; the door does not always stand open; the conviction that is stifled today may not return tomorrow. I have begged many a Felix, in my long life, not to wait—for now is the accepted time, and now is the day of salvation, and the soul that says "tomorrow" to God has no promise of a tomorrow.`,
`§§ Acts 25:1-22`,
`When Festus succeeded Felix as governor, the Jews of Jerusalem pressed their case against Paul afresh, asking as a favor that he be summoned to Jerusalem—planning, in truth, another ambush on the road. But Festus told them to come down to Caesarea, and there, when they brought many serious charges they could not prove, and Paul answered that he had committed no offense against the law of the Jews, the Temple, or Caesar, Festus, wishing to do the Jews a favor, asked whether Paul would go up to Jerusalem to be tried. Then Paul, knowing well what waited for him on that road, said the words that settled his course: "I appeal to Caesar." And Festus, after conferring with his council, answered, "To Caesar you have appealed; to Caesar you shall go." A few days later King Agrippa and his sister Bernice arrived to greet the new governor, and Festus laid Paul's case before the king, confessing that the dispute was about certain points of the Jews' own religion and about a certain Jesus, who was dead, but whom Paul asserted to be alive. Agrippa said he would like to hear the man himself, and Festus answered, "Tomorrow you shall hear him."`,
`§§ Acts 25:23-26:32`,
`So on the next day Agrippa and Bernice came with great pomp into the audience hall, with the military tribunes and the prominent men of the city, and Paul was brought in—a prisoner in chains before all the splendor of an earthly court. And being given leave to speak, he stretched out his hand and told once more the whole story of his life: his strict upbringing as a Pharisee, his rage against the name of Jesus, how he had shut up many of the saints in prison and cast his vote against them and tried to make them blaspheme, and how, on the road to Damascus at midday, a light brighter than the sun had shone around him, and the voice had said, "Saul, Saul, why do you persecute me? It is hard for you to kick against the goads." He told how the Lord had appointed him a servant and witness, to open the eyes of the Gentiles, to turn them from darkness to light and from the power of Satan to God, that they might receive forgiveness of sins and a place among those who are sanctified by faith in Him. He had not been disobedient to the heavenly vision, Paul said, but had declared everywhere that men should repent and turn to God; and he said nothing but what the prophets and Moses had foretold would come to pass—that the Christ must suffer and that, by being the first to rise from the dead, He would proclaim light both to our people and to the Gentiles.`,
`At this Festus cried out with a loud voice, "Paul, you are out of your mind; your great learning is driving you out of your mind." But Paul answered, "I am not out of my mind, most excellent Festus, but I am speaking true and rational words." And then he turned to the king: "King Agrippa, do you believe the prophets? I know that you believe." And Agrippa said to Paul, "In a short time would you persuade me to be a Christian?" And Paul's reply has never left me, and I think it never will: "Whether short or long, I would to God that not only you but also all who hear me this day might become such as I am—except for these chains." A prisoner in irons, standing before a king and a governor and the great men of the province, wished them every one the freedom that he himself possessed; for he knew, as I have come to know, that the only true bondage in all the world is sin, and the only true liberty is Christ, and that a man may be chained at the wrist and yet be freer in his soul than the king who sits in judgment over him. Agrippa concluded, when they had withdrawn, that this man had done nothing deserving death or imprisonment, and said to Festus, "This man could have been set free if he had not appealed to Caesar." But the appeal was no mistake; it was the Lord's own appointed road to Rome, and the chains Agrippa pitied were the very means by which the gospel would stand at last before the throne of the empire.`,
`I have set that scene before my mind many times—the prisoner in his chains and the king in his pomp—and asked which of the two was truly free. Agrippa sat in purple, with the governor at his side and the great men of the province gathered to do him honor; Paul stood bound, an old man worn by beatings and shipwreck and prison. And yet it was Paul who pitied Agrippa, and not the other way around. "I would to God," he said, "that you were such as I am, except for these chains." He knew, as I have come to know, that the only chains that finally matter are the chains of sin, and the only freedom worth the name is to belong to Christ. A man may sit on a throne and be a slave; a man may stand in irons and be the freest soul in the room. I have envied many things in my life, but I have never since envied a king his crown, having seen a prisoner pity one.`,
`§§ Acts 27:1-44`,
`And so they put Paul and some other prisoners aboard a ship under a centurion named Julius, of the Augustan Cohort, and set sail at last for Italy. I have heard the account of that voyage many times from those who made it, for it was a tale of terror and of the Lord's faithfulness woven so tightly together that one could not tell them apart. The season for sailing was already dangerous, and Paul warned them that the voyage would bring injury and much loss, not only of the cargo and the ship but of their lives; yet the centurion listened to the captain and the owner rather than to the prisoner, and they put out to sea. Soon a tempestuous wind, the one they call the northeaster, rushed down and struck the ship and drove it before the gale, so that they could not face it and gave way and were driven along. They threw the cargo overboard with their own hands, and then the ship's tackle, and when neither sun nor stars appeared for many days, and no small tempest lay on them, at last all hope of being saved was abandoned.`,
`Then Paul stood up among them, in the howling dark of the hold, and said, "Take heart, for there will be no loss of life among you, but only of the ship. For this very night there stood before me an angel of the God to whom I belong and whom I worship, and he said, 'Do not be afraid, Paul; you must stand before Caesar. And behold, God has granted you all those who sail with you.' So take heart, men, for I have faith in God that it will be exactly as I have been told." After fourteen nights of being driven across the sea, when they sensed land was near, and the sailors tried to abandon ship in the lifeboat, Paul told the centurion, "Unless these men stay in the ship, you cannot be saved," and the soldiers cut the boat away. As day was about to dawn, Paul urged them all to take food, for they had eaten nothing in their long ordeal, and taking bread he gave thanks to God in the presence of all, and broke it and began to eat, and they were all encouraged and took food themselves. Then they ran the ship aground, and the bow stuck fast while the stern was broken up by the surf, and the soldiers planned to kill the prisoners lest any escape; but the centurion, wishing to save Paul, kept them from it, and ordered those who could swim to make for land, and the rest on planks and pieces of the ship. And so it was that all were brought safely to land—two hundred and seventy-six souls, and not one of them lost, exactly as the Lord had promised.`,
`I have loved that voyage, and one small mercy hidden inside the great one above all. For the sake of one man whom the Lord was carrying to Rome to bear witness, He spared the whole shipload—soldiers and sailors and prisoners and all, men who knew nothing of Jesus and had not asked to be saved. So it has always been with those whom God saves: they become a blessing to all who are near them, and the very presence of one of the Lord's servants in the storm becomes the rescue of many who never knew to ask for it. The world little knows how much it owes to the praying few in its midst, the handful for whose sake the ship is spared, as Sodom would have been spared for ten righteous men. Paul broke bread and gave thanks in the teeth of the gale, as the Lord had broken bread on the green hillside and in the upper room, and the breaking of that bread put courage into despairing men—a small foretaste, in the dark of a sinking ship, of the feast at the end of all storms.`,
`I have loved to think on the bread Paul broke in the howling dark of that sinking ship, when all hope had been abandoned and men had not eaten for fourteen days. He took bread, and gave thanks to God in the presence of them all, and broke it, and began to eat—and they were all encouraged. It was such a small thing, a man giving thanks over bread in a storm; and yet it put courage into two hundred and seventy-six despairing souls, because it was the act of a man who plainly believed that God would do what He had promised. I have come to see that this is much of what the Lord's people are for in this storm-tossed world: to give thanks in the dark, to break bread in the teeth of the gale, to act as those who trust the promise when everyone around them has given up hope. The world little knows how much it owes to the quiet, thankful faith of the few in its midst, for whose sake, like Paul's shipmates, it is so often spared.`,
`§§ Acts 28:1-16`,
`When they had been brought safely through, they learned that the island was called Malta, and the native people showed them unusual kindness, kindling a fire and welcoming them all because of the rain and the cold. As Paul gathered a bundle of sticks and put them on the fire, a viper came out because of the heat and fastened on his hand. When the natives saw the creature hanging from his hand, they said to one another that this man must be a murderer, who though he had escaped the sea, Justice had not allowed to live. But Paul shook off the creature into the fire and suffered no harm, and when they waited a long time and saw no misfortune come to him, they changed their minds and said he was a god. Nearby were the lands of the chief man of the island, named Publius, whose father lay sick with fever and dysentery; and Paul visited him and prayed and laid his hands on him and healed him. After that the rest of the sick on the island came and were cured, and they honored the company greatly and provided for their needs. After three months they set sail in a ship that had wintered in the island, and came by Syracuse and Rhegium to Puteoli, where they found brothers and stayed seven days; and so at last they came toward Rome. And the believers there, hearing of their coming, came out as far as the Forum of Appius and the Three Taverns to meet them, and when Paul saw them, he thanked God and took courage. I have always been touched by that—that after the trial and the storm and the long captivity, what put fresh heart into Paul was simply the sight of his brothers walking out along the road to meet him. The fellowship of the saints is itself a mercy from God, a foretaste of the great gathering to come.`,
`I have always been touched by the small detail that what put fresh heart into Paul, after the trial and the storm and the long captivity, was simply the sight of his brothers coming out along the road to meet him at the Forum of Appius and the Three Taverns. He had faced down mobs and councils and the open sea without flinching; and it was the faces of fellow believers walking out to greet him that made him thank God and take courage. I have learned from it not to despise the simple fellowship of the saints, nor to think it a small thing. We were made to bear one another up, to be Christ's hands and face to one another in a hard world; and the company of those who love the same Lord is itself one of His sweetest mercies, a foretaste even now of the great gathering to come, when we shall all meet on the far shore and never part again.`,
`§§ Acts 28:17-31`,
`And when Paul came to Rome, he was allowed to stay by himself, with a soldier to guard him. After three days he called together the local leaders of the Jews and explained that he had done nothing against his people, and that it was because of the hope of Israel that he wore this chain. They came to him in great numbers at his lodging, and from morning till evening he expounded to them, testifying to the kingdom of God and trying to convince them about Jesus from the Law of Moses and from the Prophets. Some were convinced, and some disbelieved, and when they disagreed among themselves and departed, Paul declared that this salvation of God had been sent to the Gentiles, and they would listen. And he lived there two whole years at his own expense, and welcomed all who came to him, proclaiming the kingdom of God and teaching about the Lord Jesus Christ with all boldness and without hindrance.`,
`There, with those words, the account of all these journeys, as it has been written down for us, comes to its rest—not at Paul's death, not at his release, not at any tidy ending, but with the gospel sounding out freely in the very heart of the empire, from a prisoner's rented lodging, through chains that could bind the man but could not bind the word of God. I have thought there could be no fitter place to pause the story. The Lord had told us on the Mount of Olives, before He was taken up, that we would be His witnesses in Jerusalem, in all Judea and Samaria, and to the end of the earth; and here, in one generation, the word had traveled from a manger in Bethlehem and a carpenter's bench in Nazareth, through Galilee and Jerusalem and Samaria, through Antioch and Cyprus and Athens and Corinth and Ephesus, across the sea and through the shipwreck, all the way to Rome itself, the head and heart of the whole world—and still it was going out, still saving, still unhindered, still unstoppable. What had begun with one Man calling fishermen by the shore of a Galilean lake had become a fire spreading across the nations, and no power of Rome or of hell had been able to put it out.`,
`I have always thought it fitting that the great account of the church's birth ends not with a triumph or a tragedy but with an open door and an unhindered word—the gospel sounding freely from a prisoner's lodging in the heart of the empire. For the story was not finished, and is not finished still. The book breaks off, but the word goes on. The Lord told us we would be His witnesses to the end of the earth, and the end had not yet come, nor has it now; the gospel that reached Rome in chains has reached lands the apostles never dreamed of, and is reaching them yet. I am one of the last of those who saw Him; soon I too will be gone. But the witness will not be gone, for the word of the Lord endures forever, and the door He has opened no man can shut, and the salvation that began by a Galilean lake will not cease until the last of His people, from every nation, is gathered home.`,
`§§ Ephesians`,
`From his imprisonment in Rome, Paul wrote letters that have become among the most precious treasures of the whole church, and the first of them I would name is the one to the Ephesians, though I think he meant it to be shared among many of the churches of Asia. In it he soared higher into the wonders of our salvation than I had ever heard any man go, and reading it is like standing at the foot of a mountain and craning one's neck to find the summit. He began not with us at all but with God, blessing the God and Father of our Lord Jesus Christ who has blessed us in Christ with every spiritual blessing in the heavenly places, even as He chose us in Him before the foundation of the world, that we should be holy and blameless before Him; who in love predestined us for adoption as sons through Jesus Christ, according to the purpose of His will, to the praise of His glorious grace, with which He has blessed us in the Beloved. In Him we have redemption through His blood, the forgiveness of our trespasses, according to the riches of His grace. I marveled, reading it, that the rescue I had watched unfold in time and dust and blood on the roads of Galilee and on the hill of Golgotha had in truth been settled in the very heart of God before ever the world was made—that I had been chosen in Christ before there was a sun to rise or a sea to roar.`,
`But the words of his that I have read most often, until I know them as well as my own breath, are these: "For by grace you have been saved through faith. And this is not your own doing; it is the gift of God, not a result of works, so that no one may boast." There it was again, the truth that the whole gospel had been pressing into me from the very first day by the Jordan—that we are saved by a gift we did not earn and never could earn, lest any man stand before God and claim even the smallest portion of the credit for his own rescue. Paul wrote that we were once dead in the trespasses and sins in which we walked, children of wrath like the rest of mankind; but God, being rich in mercy, because of the great love with which He loved us, even when we were dead in our trespasses, made us alive together with Christ, and raised us up with Him, and seated us with Him in the heavenly places. Dead men do not raise themselves; a corpse contributes nothing whatever to its own resurrection. I thought of Lazarus called out of the tomb, of Jairus' daughter, of the widow's son at Nain, of Tabitha in the upper room at Joppa, and I saw that what the Lord had done to their bodies, He does to every dead soul that He saves: He speaks, and the dead hear, and live. And Paul wrote of the wall thrown down—that Christ Himself is our peace, who has made Jew and Gentile both into one, breaking down in His flesh the dividing wall of hostility, that He might create in Himself one new man in place of the two, so making peace, and reconcile us both to God in one body through the cross. The veil of the Temple torn from top to bottom, the wall of hostility thrown down, the far-off brought near by the blood of Christ—it was all of one piece, the gathering of the one new family of God by the blood of the one Lamb.`,
`I have stood at the foot of this letter as at the foot of a great mountain, straining to see the summit, and I have never reached it. That God should have chosen me in Christ before the foundation of the world, before there was a sea to roar or a star to shine—that the rescue I watched unfold in dust and blood was settled in the heart of God before time began—this is too high for me, and I can only worship. And yet Paul does not leave it in the heavens; he brings it down to the dust where I live, and tells me that I, who was dead, have been made alive, and raised, and seated with Christ in the heavenly places already, even now. I am a poor old man with shaking hands; and I am seated with Christ in glory. Both are true at once, and the second is the truer, for it rests not on what I am but on where He is, and I am in Him. Grace from first to last, that no man may boast, and that every mouth, in the end, may be filled only with His praise.`,
`§§ Philippians`,
`To the church at Philippi—the church that had begun with Lydia by the riverside and the jailer who fell trembling and asked how to be saved—Paul wrote a letter overflowing with joy, though he wrote it in chains, not knowing whether he would live to see them again or die under Caesar's sword. "For to me to live is Christ," he wrote, "and to die is gain," and I knew he meant every word of it, for I had heard how he and Silas had sung hymns in that very city's prison with their backs torn open. He told them that what had happened to him had really served to advance the gospel, so that his imprisonment for Christ had become known throughout the whole imperial guard, and that he rejoiced, and would rejoice, whether by life or by death Christ was honored in his body.`,
`At the very heart of the letter he set down words that have shaped my own soul more than I can say—a hymn, it seemed, of the church, telling how Christ Jesus, though He was in the form of God, did not count equality with God a thing to be grasped, but emptied Himself, taking the form of a servant, being born in the likeness of men; and being found in human form, He humbled Himself by becoming obedient to the point of death, even death on a cross; and therefore God has highly exalted Him and bestowed on Him the name that is above every name, that at the name of Jesus every knee should bow, in heaven and on earth and under the earth, and every tongue confess that Jesus Christ is Lord, to the glory of God the Father. I read it and saw the whole arc of the One I had followed laid out in a few short lines: the glory laid aside, the basin and the towel and the washing of our feet, the cup in the garden, the cross, the empty tomb, the cloud on the mountain, and the throne. The road down and the road up; the descent into the depths of our death and the ascent to the highest place in heaven—it was all there, and Paul sang it not only as the story of our salvation but as the very pattern for our own humility, bidding us count others more significant than ourselves. And Paul, the former Pharisee who had once trusted in his blameless pedigree and his zeal for the law, counted all of it as loss, as rubbish, that he might gain Christ and be found in Him, not having a righteousness of his own that comes from the law, but the righteousness from God that depends on faith—that he might know Him and the power of His resurrection and share in His sufferings. The man who had once trusted everything in himself now boasted in nothing at all but Christ, and pressed on toward the prize of the upward call of God, and bade us rejoice in the Lord always, and again he said, rejoice, for the Lord is at hand.`,
`I have made that hymn my own meditation through many years—the road down and the road up, the descent of the Son into our death and His ascent to the throne. And I have seen that Paul set it before us not only to be adored but to be imitated: have this mind among yourselves, he said, which is yours in Christ Jesus. The Lord who was in the form of God did not grasp at His rights but emptied Himself and took the form of a servant and humbled Himself to death; and we who follow Him are to walk that same downward road, counting others more significant than ourselves, seeking not our own. The world climbs; the Christian descends, as his Master descended, and finds at the bottom that this is the only road that leads, at the last, to being lifted up. I have watched proud men ruin themselves grasping at greatness, and humble men shine like the Lord Himself; and I have learned that in His kingdom the way up is always down.`,
`§§ Colossians`,
`To the Colossians, a church he had not himself founded but loved nonetheless, Paul wrote to guard them against teachers who would diminish Christ—who would add to Him their philosophies and their rules about food and festivals and their worship of angels, as though He alone were not sufficient, as though a believer needed something more than the Lord Himself to be complete. And so Paul lifted up the Lord in words of such surpassing majesty that I have never tired of them. He is the image of the invisible God, Paul wrote, the firstborn of all creation, for by Him all things were created, in heaven and on earth, visible and invisible, whether thrones or dominions or rulers or authorities—all things were created through Him and for Him. And He is before all things, and in Him all things hold together. And He is the head of the body, the church; He is the beginning, the firstborn from the dead, that in everything He might be preeminent. For in Him all the fullness of God was pleased to dwell, and through Him to reconcile to Himself all things, whether on earth or in heaven, making peace by the blood of His cross.`,
`I drank in those words, for they set my Lord where He belongs—above all, before all, holding all things together by the word of His power, the very One I had watched still a storm with a word, and break five loaves to feed a multitude, and weep at the tomb of His friend. Paul wrote that we who were once alienated and hostile in mind, doing evil deeds, have now been reconciled in His body of flesh by His death, to be presented holy and blameless and above reproach before God. He spoke of the record of debt that stood against us with its legal demands, which God set aside, nailing it to the cross—and I thought of the charge that had been fixed above the Lord's head at Golgotha, naming the crime for which He was condemned, and I saw that the true charge nailed there was not His crime at all but mine, the long and damning list of my own sins, cancelled forever in His dying, the handwriting against me blotted out. And Paul named the mystery hidden for ages and generations but now revealed to the saints, which is "Christ in you, the hope of glory." The Savior who had once walked beside me on the dusty roads of Galilee now dwelt within His people by His Spirit, the very Helper poured out at Pentecost, and that indwelling was the surest pledge and down payment that the glory begun in us would surely be brought to its appointed end.`,
`It has been my comfort in old age that Paul names the great mystery of the gospel as "Christ in you, the hope of glory." For the Lord whom I once walked beside on the roads of Galilee, whose face I saw and whose voice I heard and whose feet I watched gather the dust, is no longer beside me in the body; the cloud took Him long ago. And yet He is nearer to me now than He was even then, for by His Spirit He dwells within me, and within all His people, the very Helper He promised in the upper room. The disciples who walked with Him in the flesh had Him beside them; we who believe now have Him within us, which the Lord Himself said was the better thing. And this indwelling is the down payment, the first installment, the sure pledge of the glory to come; for the One who has joined Himself to me so closely will not let me go, but will bring the work He has begun in me to its appointed end, when I shall see Him face to face.`,
`§§ Philemon`,
`The shortest of all the letters that Paul sent out from his Roman chains was to a man named Philemon, and it concerned a runaway slave named Onesimus, who had wronged his master and fled, and who had somehow found his way to Paul in prison, and there found Christ. Paul was sending him back to his master, no longer as a slave only but as a beloved brother, and he pleaded for him with the tenderest words, offering even to pay out of his own pocket whatever the slave had stolen or owed: "If he has wronged you at all, or owes you anything, charge that to my account. I, Paul, write this with my own hand: I will repay it."`,
`I have always thought that this little letter holds the whole gospel within it, like a single drop of water that carries the taste of the whole sea. For here was a debtor who could not pay his debt, received back not on the ground of his own worth—he had none to plead—but for the sake of another who stood as surety for him, with all that he owed charged to an innocent account and paid by an innocent hand. And is this not exactly, precisely, what the Lord Jesus has done for me? I had run from God as Onesimus ran from his master; I had wronged Him and robbed Him and owed Him a debt I could never in ten thousand years repay. And the Lord Jesus had stepped between me and the reckoning, and said to the Father, in effect, "Charge that to my account—I will repay it," and had paid it not with silver or gold but with His own blood, that I might be received back no longer as a runaway slave but as a beloved son and brother. Paul asked Philemon to welcome Onesimus exactly as he would welcome Paul himself; and the Father welcomes us exactly as He welcomes His own beloved Son, because we come to Him clothed in that Son, and counted in Him. The mercy I had watched poured out from the cross was now, in a small house in Asia, teaching a slaveowner and his runaway to live as brothers in Christ; and in the reconciling of those two men, I saw the picture of my own.`,
`§§ 1 Timothy`,
`Paul, it seems, was released from that first Roman imprisonment, and for a season he traveled again among the churches before the end came upon him. From those years came his letters to Timothy and to Titus, the young men he had left to shepherd the flocks at Ephesus and on Crete—letters of a father to his sons in the faith, full of charges to guard sound doctrine, to appoint faithful elders, to rebuke error gently and to keep themselves pure. To Timothy at Ephesus he wrote first, warning him against those who wanted to be teachers of the law without understanding it, and reminding him that the law is good if one uses it lawfully, that it is laid down not for the righteous but for the lawless and the ungodly, to drive sinners to the grace that alone can save them.`,
`And then Paul set down what he called a trustworthy saying, deserving of full acceptance, and I have copied it out and kept it where my eyes fall on it often: "Christ Jesus came into the world to save sinners, of whom I am the foremost." I loved Paul for that confession when I first heard it, and I love him for it still. The apostle who had carried the gospel across the world, who had been caught up to the third heaven, who had written the great letter to the Romans setting out the whole counsel of God—he still called himself the foremost of sinners, the chief of them, and counted his own salvation the prime exhibit of the Lord's perfect patience, a pattern set up for all who would afterward believe. The man who had held the coats at Stephen's stoning never once got over the wonder of having been forgiven. And neither, I have found, do any of us who have truly grasped what we were and what we have been given. Grace does not make a man forget his debt; it makes him treasure forever the One who paid it. He charged Timothy to wage the good warfare, to fight the good fight of the faith, to take hold of the eternal life to which he was called, and to keep the commandment unstained until the appearing of our Lord Jesus Christ, whom God will display at the proper time—He who alone has immortality, who dwells in unapproachable light, to whom be honor and eternal dominion.`,
`I have copied out that trustworthy saying and kept it where my eyes fall on it daily, for it is the whole gospel in eight words: Christ Jesus came into the world to save sinners. And I have loved that Paul added, "of whom I am the foremost"—not "of whom I was," in his persecuting days, but "of whom I am," still, now, in his old age and apostleship. He never graduated from being a saved sinner; none of us does. I have known men who, having walked with Christ many years, came to think themselves advanced beyond the simple gospel, as though grace were the doorway one passes through and then leaves behind. But the saints who shone brightest, in all my long life, were always those who, like Paul, never got over being forgiven, who lived and died as chief of sinners saved by grace. The gospel is not the porch of the house; it is the house itself, and we never move out of it, but only deeper in.`,
`§§ Titus`,
`To Titus, whom he had left on the island of Crete to set in order what remained and to appoint elders in every town, Paul wrote much the same charge, that an overseer must be above reproach, holding firm to the trustworthy word as taught, that the people might be sound in the faith and adorn the doctrine of God our Savior in all they did. But woven through the practical counsel are two passages that gather up the whole of the gospel, and I have treasured them as much as anything Paul ever wrote. "For the grace of God has appeared," he wrote, "bringing salvation for all people, training us to renounce ungodliness and worldly passions, and to live self-controlled, upright, and godly lives in the present age, waiting for our blessed hope, the appearing of the glory of our great God and Savior Jesus Christ, who gave Himself for us to redeem us from all lawlessness and to purify for Himself a people for His own possession, who are zealous for good works."`,
`The grace of God has appeared. It had appeared to me first by the muddy Jordan, in the form of a man with dust on His sandals, and John crying, "Behold, the Lamb of God"; and that same grace had given itself, on a cross, to redeem a people for God's own possession. And Paul wrote, as if he could not help returning to it again and again, of how God our Savior saved us—"not because of works done by us in righteousness, but according to His own mercy, by the washing of regeneration and renewal of the Holy Spirit, whom He poured out on us richly through Jesus Christ our Savior, so that being justified by His grace we might become heirs according to the hope of eternal life." Not because of works done by us in righteousness, but according to His own mercy. I never tired of hearing it, in whatever words it came, from whatever mouth, for it was the one note that the whole gospel sounded from beginning to end, and the note my own soul most needed to hear: that I was saved by His mercy, and not by my own goodness, and could rest there forever.`,
`I have returned often to that word Paul wrote to Titus: that we are saved not because of works done by us in righteousness, but according to His own mercy. There is no sweeter sentence for a dying man to rest his soul upon. For when I come to the end and look back over my long life, I can find no righteousness of my own fit to set before God, only failures forgiven, and grace upon grace. If my salvation rested on my works, I should despair; but it rests on His mercy, and His mercy does not fail. I have watched men try to build their hope on their own goodness, and I have watched that hope crumble at the hour of death when they most needed it; for our best works are stained, and we know it in our hearts. But the man who has learned to say that he is saved not by his own righteousness but by His mercy has a hope that the grave cannot shake, for it rests on the unshakable mercy of God.`,
`§§ 1 Peter`,
`In those same years, before the great fire and the persecution that followed, a letter came from Peter, written to the believers scattered as exiles through the provinces of Asia, who were suffering grief and reproach for the name of Christ. I knew Peter's voice in every line of it—the same voice that had once denied the Lord by a fire of coals and then, restored on a beach by another fire, had stood up at Pentecost and preached three thousand into the kingdom. He wrote to people under fire, and he did the very thing a true shepherd does: he lifted their eyes off their pain and onto their hope.`,
`"Blessed be the God and Father of our Lord Jesus Christ," he began, "who according to His great mercy has caused us to be born again to a living hope through the resurrection of Jesus Christ from the dead, to an inheritance that is imperishable, undefiled, and unfading, kept in heaven for you, who by God's power are being guarded through faith for a salvation ready to be revealed in the last time." Born again to a living hope—the very thing the Lord had spoken of to Nicodemus by night, that a man must be born again to see the kingdom, now the settled and guarded possession of suffering exiles, secured forever by the empty tomb and kept for them in heaven where no enemy could reach it. Peter reminded them that they had been ransomed from the futile ways inherited from their fathers not with perishable things such as silver or gold, but with the precious blood of Christ, like that of a lamb without blemish or spot. And he wrote of the Lord's suffering in words that I am sure he learned by standing near the cross and never being able to forget what he saw: "He Himself bore our sins in His body on the tree, that we might die to sin and live to righteousness. By His wounds you have been healed. For you were straying like sheep, but have now returned to the Shepherd and Overseer of your souls." I thought of the Lamb of God whom John had pointed out at the Jordan on the first day, of the lamb led to slaughter in Isaiah's song that the Ethiopian had read in his chariot, and I saw the whole long testimony come together in Peter's lines: that Jesus is the spotless Lamb, and His blood the only ransom, and by His wounds we who were straying sheep are healed and brought home to the Shepherd. Peter told the exiles not to be surprised at the fiery trial that came upon them, as though something strange were happening, but to rejoice insofar as they shared Christ's sufferings, and to cast all their anxieties upon Him, because He cares for them.`,
`I knew Peter, and I tell you that the man who wrote "He Himself bore our sins in His body on the tree" had stood near enough to that tree to see the body and the blood, and had never been able to forget it. There is a weight in his words that comes only from having been there. And there is a tenderness, too, that comes from having been forgiven much; for this was the man who had denied the Lord with curses and been restored by a look of love and a breakfast on the shore. When he calls the Lord "the Shepherd and Overseer of your souls," and reminds the scattered, suffering exiles that they were straying sheep now returned, he is speaking out of his own straying and his own return. I have always trusted the comfort of a wounded comforter more than the counsel of one who has never fallen; and Peter, who had fallen so far and been raised so gently, knew exactly how to bind up the wounds of a frightened and failing flock.`,
`§§ Hebrews`,
`About this time there came among us a letter—I never learned with certainty whose hand had written it—addressed to Hebrew believers who, under the weight of persecution, were sorely tempted to slip back into the old religion of priests and sacrifices and the Temple, as though the shadows were safer than the substance that had cast them. It is a letter of surpassing richness, and it does for the whole old covenant what the Lord Himself did on the road to Emmaus: it opens the Scriptures and shows that every part of them was pointing forward to Him.`,
`It declares that God, who long ago at many times and in many ways spoke to our fathers by the prophets, has in these last days spoken to us by His Son, whom He appointed the heir of all things, through whom also He created the world; who is the radiance of the glory of God and the exact imprint of His nature, who upholds the universe by the word of His power; and who, after making purification for sins, sat down at the right hand of the Majesty on high. He sat down—and that one detail, when I first marked it, went through me like light. For the priests of the old covenant stood daily at their service, offering again and again the same sacrifices, which could never take away sins; the work was never finished, and so the priest could never sit. But Christ, having offered for all time a single sacrifice for sins, sat down at the right hand of God, because there was nothing left to do, nothing left to add, the work complete and the debt paid in full and forever. The letter calls Him our great high priest, who is also Himself the offering—the one who passed through the heavens, who entered the holy places not by means of the blood of goats and calves but by means of His own blood, thus securing an eternal redemption. He has no need, like the old high priests, to offer sacrifices day after day, first for His own sins and then for the people's, for this He did once for all when He offered up Himself.`,
`The word in this letter that has steadied me most is that small one: He sat down. The priests of old never sat, for their work was never done; they stood day after day, year after year, offering the same sacrifices that could never take away sins, an endless labor that could never reach an end. But Christ, having offered Himself once for all, sat down at the right hand of God, and in that sitting down is all my rest. The work of my salvation is finished; there is nothing left for me to add, nothing left to be done, no sacrifice to repeat, no debt outstanding. I do not labor to be saved; I rest in a salvation already accomplished and a Savior already seated. I have known restless souls who could never believe the work was truly done, who kept trying to add their own offerings to His, and they never found peace. But He is seated; the work is finished; and the weary soul that believes it may at last sit down too, and rest.`,
`§§ Hebrews (continued)`,
`And the letter speaks of the curtain, and of this I have pondered most of all. I had been told, by those who were near, how at the very moment the Lord cried out and gave up His spirit on the cross, the great veil of the Temple, the heavy curtain that shut off the Most Holy Place where the presence of God dwelt, was torn in two from top to bottom, by no human hand, so that what no man had ever been allowed to enter save the high priest once a year, and that not without blood, stood suddenly open. And here, at last, this letter named what that tearing meant: "Therefore, brothers, since we have confidence to enter the holy places by the blood of Jesus, by the new and living way that He opened for us through the curtain, that is, through His flesh, let us draw near with a true heart in full assurance of faith." The veil was His flesh, torn open on the cross, that we might come in—come all the way in, into the very presence of God, not trembling at a distance as our fathers had, but with confidence and full assurance, washed clean and welcomed.`,
`I sat a long while with that when I first understood it, and I weep over it still. Every sacrifice I had ever watched smoke up from the altar in my childhood, every Passover lamb whose blood was smeared on the doorposts, every drop of blood the high priest carried behind the veil on the great day of atonement—every one of them had been a finger pointing forward across the centuries to the one true Lamb, whose single offering of Himself would never need to be repeated, and whose torn flesh would open the way to God that all the rivers of animal blood could never open. "It is finished," the Lord had cried from the cross, and this whole letter is but the long and glorious unfolding of those three words. The shadows had served their purpose and were passing away; the substance had come; and the way into the holiest place of all stood open, watered with the blood of the Son of God, for any sinner who would draw near through Him. The letter urged the wavering Hebrews, therefore, not to go back to the shadows, not to shrink back to destruction, but to hold fast their confession and to run with endurance the race set before them, looking to Jesus, the founder and perfecter of our faith, who for the joy set before Him endured the cross, despising the shame, and is seated at the right hand of the throne of God.`,
`I cannot write of that torn veil without my old eyes filling, for I have lived my whole life on the far side of a thing my fathers could only long for. They worshiped at a distance, behind a curtain none might pass; the high priest went in but once a year, trembling, and not without blood, and the people waited outside. And now, because His flesh was torn, the curtain is torn, and the way stands open—not for a priest once a year, but for any sinner, any day, at any hour, to come all the way in, into the very presence of God, with confidence and full assurance. I have come boldly to that throne ten thousand times in my long life, in joy and in grief and in fear, and have never once been turned away, because I do not come in my own name or my own righteousness but in the blood of Jesus, by the new and living way He opened for me. The veil that shut us out is the flesh that was torn to let us in.`,
`§§ 2 Peter`,
`Before the end came upon him, Peter wrote a second letter, sent out, he said, as one who knew that the putting off of his body would be soon, as the Lord Jesus had made clear to him—a last word from a dying shepherd to the flock he loved. He wrote to stir up their minds by way of reminder, that they might remember the words of the prophets and the commandment of the Lord given through the apostles, and that they might grow in the grace and knowledge of our Lord and Savior Jesus Christ. He testified that he and the others had not followed cleverly devised myths when they made known the power and coming of the Lord, but had been eyewitnesses of His majesty—and here he spoke of the mountain, of the day he and James and John and I had heard the voice from the cloud and seen the Lord's face shine like the sun, and I knew the truth of every word, for I had been at its foot. He warned sharply against false teachers who would secretly bring in destructive heresies, even denying the Master who bought them, and who would entice unsteady souls with sensuality and greed.`,
`But the deepest comfort of that letter, the part I have leaned on most as the years stretched long and the Lord did not yet return, was Peter's answer to the scoffers. For there were those, even then, who mocked, saying, "Where is the promise of His coming? For ever since the fathers fell asleep, all things are continuing as they were from the beginning of creation." And Peter answered that the Lord is not slow to fulfill His promise as some count slowness, but is patient toward us, not wishing that any should perish, but that all should reach repentance. I have held that close, for I am very old now, and many long years have passed since the cloud took Him from the Mount of Olives, and still we wait, and still the scoffers ask their question. But I have learned, through all the waiting, that the long delay is not forgetfulness, nor weakness, nor any failure of His promise; it is mercy, and nothing else but mercy. Every day that He tarries is another day given for one more sinner to come home—another prodigal still on the long road back, another Saul still riding toward Damascus, another dying thief who has not yet turned his head to ask to be remembered. The very patience that has so tried our waiting is the patience that is saving the world, soul by soul. And Peter promised that the day of the Lord will come like a thief, and the heavens will pass away with a roar, and that we, according to His promise, are waiting for new heavens and a new earth in which righteousness dwells.`,
`I have leaned on Peter's word about the Lord's patience more and more as the years have lengthened and the scoffers' question has grown louder: where is the promise of His coming? I do not have all the answers to that question, but I have the one that matters: He is not slow, He is patient; the delay is not forgetfulness but mercy. Every year He tarries is a year in which more of the lost are gathered in, more prodigals coming up the road, more Sauls struck down on the way to Damascus, more dying thieves turning their heads at the last to ask to be remembered. If He had come in my youth, how many now in the kingdom would have been shut out? The waiting that so tries our patience is the very mercy that is saving the world, soul by soul. And so I have learned to be glad even in the delay, and to use the days He gives in telling what I have seen, that one more might come home before the door is shut.`,
`§§ Jude`,
`About the same time there came a short and fiery letter from Jude, another of the Lord's own brothers, who like James had once not believed and now called himself a servant of Jesus Christ. He had set out, he said, to write to us about our common salvation, but had found it needful instead to write urging the believers to contend for the faith that was once for all delivered to the saints, for certain ungodly men had crept in unnoticed, perverting the grace of our God into sensuality and denying our only Master and Lord, Jesus Christ. He warned them with stern examples from of old—of the people saved out of Egypt and afterward destroyed for their unbelief, of the angels who did not keep their proper place, of Sodom and Gomorrah—that grace is never a license to sin, and that those who turn the mercy of God into an excuse for wickedness show that they never understood it at all. But he ended not in warning but in glory, with a doxology I have prayed over more times than I can count, and that I would have spoken over me at my own end: "Now to Him who is able to keep you from stumbling and to present you blameless before the presence of His glory with great joy, to the only God, our Savior, through Jesus Christ our Lord, be glory, majesty, dominion, and authority, before all time and now and forever. Amen." He is able to keep us. Not we ourselves, who stumble and deny and fall asleep in the garden and flee in the dark—but He is able to keep us, and to present us at the last blameless and with great joy. The keeping, like the saving, is His.`,
`I have prayed Jude's closing words over myself more times than I can number, especially now as my strength fails: that He is able to keep me from stumbling, and to present me blameless before the presence of His glory with great joy. For I know my own weakness too well to trust in my own keeping. I have stumbled often in my long life; I fled in the garden; I have feared and failed and doubted. If my standing before God depended on my holding fast to Him, I should have been lost long ago. But the gospel does not say that I must keep myself; it says that He is able to keep me, and to present me at the last not merely acquitted but blameless, and not grudgingly but with great joy. The keeping is His, as the saving was His; and the same hand that laid hold of me by the Jordan will hold me fast through the valley of the shadow and set me at last, faultless and glad, before His glory.`,
`§§ 2 Timothy`,
`The last letter of Paul's that ever reached us was his second to Timothy, written from a cold Roman cell in the time of Nero's cruelty, when to be a Christian had become a crime punished by fire and the sword and the wild beasts. Paul knew the end was upon him now; this was no imprisonment from which he expected release. "For I am already being poured out as a drink offering," he wrote, "and the time of my departure has come. I have fought the good fight, I have finished the race, I have kept the faith. Henceforth there is laid up for me the crown of righteousness, which the Lord, the righteous judge, will award to me on that day, and not only to me but also to all who have loved His appearing."`,
`I have never been able to read those words without weeping. The man who counted himself the foremost of sinners, saved by nothing but sheer grace, came to the very end of his course boasting not in his own righteousness, which he had long ago counted rubbish, but in the crown that the righteous Judge would give him—a crown of righteousness that was itself, like everything else from first to last, a gift and never a wage. He told Timothy that he was lonely, that all had deserted him at his first defense, that only Luke was with him, and he asked him to come before winter and to bring the cloak he had left at Troas, and the books, and above all the parchments—an old man, cold and alone in a dungeon, still wanting his books and his Scriptures. He charged Timothy to preach the word in season and out of season, to endure suffering, to do the work of an evangelist, to fulfill his ministry. And then, so far as we ever heard, the executioner came, and Paul, a Roman citizen, was taken outside the walls of the city and beheaded with the sword.`,
`This last letter of Paul's is, to me, the most moving of them all—not for its doctrine, though that is glorious, but for the man I see in it: old, cold, abandoned by nearly all, alone in a dungeon with the executioner's sword soon to fall, asking only for his cloak and his books and his friend before winter. And yet there is no whining in it, no bitterness, no doubt—only "I have fought the good fight, I have finished the race, I have kept the faith," and the certain hope of a crown laid up by the righteous Judge. I have thought often that a man's faith is most truly shown not in his vigor but in his dying, and that Paul's quiet, unshaken peace in that cell preached the gospel as loudly as any of his sermons. He was being poured out as a drink offering, and he poured gladly, for he knew the One in whom he had believed, and was persuaded that He was able to keep what had been entrusted to Him until that day. I should like to die as Paul died.`,
`§§ The Martyrdoms of Peter and Paul`,
`In those same burning years, Peter too was put to death in Rome, and they say he was crucified, and that he asked to be hung head downward upon the cross, counting himself unworthy to die in the same manner as his Lord had died. And so the two great pillars of the church, the apostle to the Jews and the apostle to the Gentiles, went home to the Lord within a little while of one another, and the church wept for them as for fathers. Yet we did not weep as those who have no hope, for we knew well where they had gone, and to whom. The Lord had told Peter long before, by the charcoal fire on the beach where He had restored him, the kind of death by which he would glorify God; and Peter, who had once fled and denied and wept bitterly, now followed the Shepherd all the way through the valley of the shadow, as James the brother of John had before him, as Stephen had before them all, as so many were doing in those terrible years. I had watched, over a long life, the frightened men of the upper room become men who could sing in prison and rejoice to suffer dishonor for the name and walk steadily to their own crosses, and I knew that nothing on earth had wrought such a change but the salvation that had laid hold of them—for no man dies like that for a tale he knows to be false, or for a hope he is not sure of. They had seen the risen Lord, and death had lost its terror, because they knew, as I knew, the One who had already conquered it.`,
`When the news of their deaths reached me, I grieved as a man grieves for his own brothers, for so they were; and yet, even in the grief, there was a strange and steadying joy, for I knew they had not lost their lives but found them, exactly as the Lord had said. I remembered Peter as I had first known him—the rough, impulsive fisherman who blurted out whatever came to him, who sank in the waves the moment he looked away from the Lord, who swore by the fire that he never knew the man. And I marveled at what grace had made of him: a rock indeed, who followed his Master to a cross and asked to hang head downward, unworthy, he said, to die as his Lord had died. No man is born able to die like that. It is the work of the salvation that lays hold of cowards and makes them martyrs, that takes a man who fled in the garden and gives him, at the last, the courage to glorify God with his death. I had watched it happen in Peter over a lifetime, and it was the surest proof I ever saw that the gospel is true.`,
`§§ The Fall of Jerusalem`,
`Then came the war, and the thing the Lord had wept over from the Mount of Olives came at last to pass. The armies of Rome surrounded Jerusalem, and after a long and terrible siege, with horrors within the walls that I cannot bring myself to write, the city fell, and the Temple was burned to the ground, and not one stone was left standing upon another, exactly as He had foretold to us as we sat looking down upon its golden splendor. When the news reached me I wept, as He had wept—for the city I had loved, for the holy house in whose courts I had worshiped since I was a child, for the people who had not known the time of their visitation nor the things that made for their peace.`,
`But I understood, in a way that I could not have understood in my younger years, what the destruction truly meant. The true Temple had already been destroyed and raised—His body, torn down at Golgotha and raised on the third morning, the temple He had spoken of in the very first days when He said, "Destroy this temple, and in three days I will raise it up." The dwelling place of God was no longer a house of stone upon a hill, to which the nations must come up; it was the Lord Himself, and His people, in whom His Spirit had come to dwell, scattered now through every city of the world. The veil had been torn; the sacrifices had found their end in the one true Sacrifice; and so the shadow passed away, in fire and ruin, precisely because the substance had come and would remain forever. The old order, which had served its purpose and pointed faithfully to Christ, was folded up like a worn-out garment; and the new and living way stood open, no longer in one city only, but to the ends of the earth.`,
`I was old when the city fell, and the news of it broke something in me, for I had loved Jerusalem—had gone up to her feasts as a boy, had worshiped in the Temple courts, had walked her streets with the Lord Himself. And now not one stone stood upon another, just as He had foretold with tears as we sat looking down upon her golden splendor. But in my grief I came to understand what the ruin meant. The Temple had been a great signpost, pointing all our history toward the One who was to come; and now that He had come, and died, and risen, the signpost had done its work, and the sacrifices that pointed to His sacrifice had found their end in Him. The veil was already torn; the true Lamb had already been slain; the dwelling place of God was no longer a house of stone but the risen Christ and His people. The signpost was taken down because the travelers had reached the destination. I wept for the stones, but I worshiped the One the stones had always been pointing to, who can never be thrown down.`,
`§§ The Writing of the Gospels`,
`In those same years, as the war scattered us and the eyewitnesses began one by one to die, the Lord moved His servants to set down in writing what we had seen and heard, lest the testimony be lost when the last of us was gone. Mark, who had once turned back from the work in Pamphylia and been restored, set down the account he had heard from Peter's own lips, plain and swift and vivid. Matthew, who had risen from his tax booth at a single word and left everything to follow, wrote for our own people, showing how Jesus fulfilled all the law and the prophets, how He was the son of David and the son of Abraham, the King of the Jews. And Luke, the beloved physician who had traveled with Paul and stayed with him to the end, made careful inquiry of those of us who had been eyewitnesses from the beginning, and set down an orderly account, that his reader might have certainty concerning the things he had been taught; and then he wrote a second book, of the acts of the apostles, that the story might be carried on past the empty tomb into the spreading of the word through all the world.`,
`I gave my own testimony where and when I could, for I had promised myself, on the day the cloud took Him from our sight, that I would spend whatever was left of my life telling what I had seen. And it is a strange and holy thing, in my old age, to watch the story that I lived become Scripture—to know that the very words a frightened band of fishermen and tax collectors had spoken in fear, behind locked doors, would be read aloud in every nation under heaven, in every tongue, as the very word of God, exactly as the Lord had said the gospel would be proclaimed to all the world before the end should come. The witnesses were passing, one after another, into glory; but the witness would remain. The seed we had been given to scatter had become a tree in whose branches the nations of the earth were finding their rest.`,
`It has been a strange grace of my old age to watch the very things I lived become Scripture, to know that words spoken by frightened fishermen behind locked doors are now read aloud as the word of God in lands whose names we never knew. I gave my testimony where I could, and others wrote, each as the Lord moved him, and I have marveled at how the same Lord shines through them all, each telling truly what he saw and heard. I think often that the Lord arranged it so on purpose: that the gospel should rest not on the word of one man, who might be doubted, but on a cloud of witnesses who saw the same risen Christ and could not be silenced. We did not invent Him; we could not have. Frightened men do not die for tales they have made up. We told what we had seen, and the telling has outlived us all, and will outlive every empire, for the word of the Lord endures forever.`,
`§§ The Gospel of John`,
`The years went on, and one by one the old companions went home to the Lord, until at last, of those who had walked with Him by the lake of Galilee, only a very few remained, and then fewer still. John lived longest of the twelve, an old man at Ephesus, gentle and unwearied, returning always to his one great theme, which was love. And in his old age he wrote his own account of the Lord—not as the others had written, beginning with the manger or the baptism, but reaching back behind the beginning of all things: "In the beginning was the Word, and the Word was with God, and the Word was God. He was in the beginning with God. All things were made through Him, and without Him was not anything made that was made. In Him was life, and the life was the light of men. And the Word became flesh and dwelt among us, and we have seen His glory, glory as of the only Son from the Father, full of grace and truth."`,
`When I first heard those words read, I wept for joy, for it was the deepest and truest thing that any of us had ever managed to say about the Man we had followed through the dust of Galilee. That the carpenter from Nazareth, whose tired feet I had watched gather the dust of the roads, who had slept in our boats and eaten at our tables and wept at our friend's tomb, was the eternal Word, God Himself, the maker of all things, come down and made flesh to dwell among us—that the glory we had beheld on the mountain and at the empty tomb was the glory of the only Son of the Father. John gathered up the signs we had seen, the water made wine and the bread multiplied and Lazarus called from the grave, and the great sayings of the Lord, the bread of life, the light of the world, the good shepherd, the resurrection and the life, the way and the truth and the life, the true vine. And he told plainly why he had set it all down: "These are written so that you may believe that Jesus is the Christ, the Son of God, and that by believing you may have life in His name." Life in His name—the very thing Peter had cried before the council long ago, that there is salvation in no other name under heaven. John had written the whole of his Gospel to one end: that whoever read it might believe and live.`,
`When I first heard John's opening words read aloud—"In the beginning was the Word, and the Word was God... and the Word became flesh and dwelt among us"—I had to stop and weep, for he had said the thing the rest of us had circled around for years without quite daring to say so plainly. We had followed a man; we had watched Him eat and sleep and weep and bleed; and that man was God, the eternal Word, the maker of all things, come down and clothed in our flesh. I had touched Him; John had leaned on His breast at the supper; and He was the One by whom the worlds were made. It is a thing too great for the mind to hold, and yet it is the very center of everything: that God did not save us from a distance, sending help down from heaven, but came Himself, in person, into our dust and our death, to seek and to save us with His own hands and His own blood. The Word became flesh. There is no deeper truth than that, and no greater comfort.`,
`§§ The Letters of John`,
`John wrote letters too in those last long years, and they returned again and again, like a tide, to two things: the love of God, and the blood of His Son. He had stood at the foot of the cross, the one disciple who had not fled, and had watched with his own eyes the blood and water flow from the Lord's pierced side; and to the very end of his long life he could not stop telling sinners that there was cleansing in that blood for all who would come. "If we walk in the light, as He is in the light," he wrote, "we have fellowship with one another, and the blood of Jesus His Son cleanses us from all sin." And he would not let any soul despair: "If we confess our sins, He is faithful and just to forgive us our sins and to cleanse us from all unrighteousness." And again, for those who stumbled: "If anyone does sin, we have an advocate with the Father, Jesus Christ the righteous; and He is the propitiation for our sins, and not for ours only but also for the sins of the whole world."`,
`But the word that rang through all his letters, the word he had learned, I think, by leaning on the Lord's own breast at the supper and by standing beneath His cross, was love. "See what kind of love the Father has given to us," he wrote, "that we should be called children of God; and so we are." And: "In this is love, not that we have loved God but that He loved us and sent His Son to be the propitiation for our sins." And, simplest of all, the deepest thing the old man could say after a lifetime of following: "God is love." He had seen the proof of it with his own eyes, lifted up and bleeding on a Roman cross, and he spent the last of his days telling the world that the love which made the stars had come down and given itself for sinners, and that whoever would believe in the Son had eternal life, and that we love because He first loved us.`,
`That John, after a whole long lifetime of following the Lord, came at the last to so simple a word, that God is love, has always seemed to me the ripe fruit of everything. He had begun, like the rest of us, full of fire and ambition, a son of thunder who wanted to call down flame on a village that would not receive the Lord. And he ended an old man whose every letter circled back to love: love one another; God is love; we love because He first loved us. That is what a lifetime in the presence of Christ does to a man, if he will let it: it burns away the thunder and leaves the love. And John had seen the proof of that love with his own eyes, for he was the one disciple who did not flee, who stood beneath the cross and watched the blood and water flow from the pierced side. He knew what the love of God looked like, for he had seen it lifted up and bleeding, and he spent his last breath telling the world.`,
`§§ Revelation: The Lamb on the Throne`,
`Last of all came the great vision. In the days of a new persecution under Caesar, John, now very old, was exiled to a small and rocky island called Patmos in the sea, for the word of God and the testimony of Jesus. And there, on the Lord's day, the Lord met His aged servant once more, and gave him to see the end of the whole story. When his account of it at last reached the churches, I read it as a man reads the final page of a book that he has lived inside his whole life long, and found that the ending was greater than anything I had dared to hope.`,
`John saw the Lord again—no longer the bound and bleeding prisoner of Pilate's hall, nor the broken figure on the cross, but the risen and glorified Christ in blazing majesty, His face like the sun shining in full strength, His voice like the roar of many waters, holding in His hand the keys of Death and Hades, and saying, "Fear not, I am the first and the last, and the living one. I died, and behold I am alive forevermore, and I have the keys of Death and Hades." And John was caught up in the Spirit into heaven itself, and saw the throne of God, and around it the worship of all the living creatures and the elders; and then, in the midst of the throne, he saw a Lamb standing, as though it had been slain. A Lamb—still bearing the marks of slaughter, the wounds upon it—standing alive at the very center of all the glory of heaven; and the whole of creation fell down before it and sang a new song: "Worthy is the Lamb who was slain, to receive power and wealth and wisdom and might and honor and glory and blessing!" I have wept over that page more than any other in all the Scriptures, for it gathered up into one everything I had seen and everything I had hoped. The Lamb of God whom John the Baptist had pointed out by the muddy waters of the Jordan on the very first day, the Lamb led silently to the slaughter, the Passover Lamb whose blood was the ransom of the world—there He was at the last, at the center of the universe, slain and yet standing, His wounds now His eternal glory, worshiped forever by all that lives as the One who alone was worthy, and worthy precisely because He was slain.`,
`And John saw why the Lamb was counted worthy. They sang to Him a new song: "Worthy are you to take the scroll and to open its seals, for you were slain, and by your blood you ransomed people for God from every tribe and language and people and nation, and you have made them a kingdom and priests to our God, and they shall reign on the earth." From every nation. The thing I had first glimpsed at a Samaritan well, that had broken open at Cornelius' house, that had run through Antioch and Athens and Corinth and Rome and out to the very ends of the earth—here it was at the end, complete and gathered home: a great multitude that no one could number, from every nation and tribe and people and tongue, standing before the throne and before the Lamb, clothed in white robes and with palm branches in their hands, crying out with a loud voice, "Salvation belongs to our God who sits on the throne, and to the Lamb!" And one of the elders explained to John who they were and how their robes were made white: "These are the ones coming out of the great tribulation. They have washed their robes and made them white in the blood of the Lamb." White robes, washed clean in blood—the great exchange that Paul had written of, the sinner clothed in a righteousness not his own, shown now as the eternal dress of the whole company of the redeemed, in the light of the throne.`,
`I have read that vision until I know it by heart, and the part that undoes me every time is this: that when John looked to see the Lion of Judah who had conquered, he saw instead a Lamb, standing as though it had been slain. The conqueror is a slain Lamb. The marks of the slaughter are not hidden in heaven, nor healed away as though they had never been; they are His eternal glory, the very reason all creation falls down and sings that He is worthy. I had stood beneath the cross and seen those wounds made; I had touched the print of them in the risen body; and now I learn that they will shine at the center of heaven forever, the trophies of the victory by which He bought us. We shall not forget, through all eternity, what it cost Him to save us, for we shall see the wounds and worship. The Lamb who was slain is worthy—worthy precisely because He was slain—and that song will never end.`,
`§§ Revelation: All Things New`,
`John saw the long judgment of all the enemies of God, the defeat of the dragon and the beast and the false prophet, and the last judgment before the great white throne, where the dead were judged by what was written in the books, and death and Hades themselves were thrown at last into the lake of fire. And then he saw the thing toward which the whole story had been straining since the gates of Eden were shut behind our first parents: a new heaven and a new earth, for the first heaven and the first earth had passed away, and the sea was no more; and the holy city, the new Jerusalem, coming down out of heaven from God, prepared as a bride adorned for her husband. And he heard a loud voice from the throne saying the words that are the end and the crown of all the gospel: "Behold, the dwelling place of God is with man. He will dwell with them, and they will be His people, and God Himself will be with them as their God. He will wipe away every tear from their eyes, and death shall be no more, neither shall there be mourning nor crying nor pain anymore, for the former things have passed away."`,
`No more death. The last enemy, the one I had watched the Lord defeat at the gate of Nain and at the bedside of Jairus' little girl and at the tomb of Lazarus, and at last and forever in His own rising on the third morning—abolished now, swallowed up in victory, cast into the lake of fire, never to touch the redeemed again. No more tears, no more mourning, no more pain; the curse undone, the long exile ended, the Father's house thrown open, and God dwelling with His people face to face. And He who was seated on the throne said, "Behold, I am making all things new." John saw the city need no temple, for its temple is the Lord God Almighty and the Lamb; and it had no need of sun or moon, for the glory of God gave it light, and its lamp was the Lamb; and the leaves of the tree of life were for the healing of the nations, and there would be no more anything accursed, and His servants would see His face, and reign forever and ever.`,
`No more death. I have read those words over the graves of nearly everyone I loved, and I read them now as my own end draws near, and they are the sweetest words outside the gospel itself, for they are the gospel's final fruit. The last enemy, the one I watched the Lord defeat at Nain and at Jairus' bedside and at the tomb of Lazarus and at last in His own rising, is to be abolished forever, cast into the lake of fire, never again to touch one of the redeemed. And with it go the tears and the mourning and the pain and the whole long curse that has lain upon the world since Eden. God Himself will dwell with His people and wipe every tear from their eyes with His own hand. This is what the whole story was always moving toward, from the first promise in the garden to the Lamb upon the throne: not merely that our sins should be forgiven, great as that is, but that God should dwell with man, face to face, forever, and make all things new. I have watched the sky a long time for that day. It is nearer now than it has ever been.`,
`§§ The Last Word`,
`I am very old now, older than I ever thought to be, and I have outlived nearly all who walked those dusty roads with me. My hands shake as I set these final things down, and my eyes are dim, but the memory is as bright in me as the morning each thing happened. I have told it all, from the first day to the last—from the day John the Baptist pointed across the Jordan and cried, "Behold, the Lamb of God, who takes away the sin of the world," to the day John on Patmos saw that same Lamb standing slain and glorious at the very center of heaven, worshiped by every nation that He had bought with His own blood. And I have come to see, looking back across the whole long sweep of it, that it is all one story, and that it has only ever been about one thing: how God came down to seek and to save the lost, and would not stop—not at the manger, not at the cross, not at the empty tomb, not at Pentecost, not at the ends of the earth, not at the fall of the holy city, and not until He has gathered in His own from every nation, and wiped away every tear, and made all things new.`,
`I think now of the men I knew. I think of Peter, who denied Him by the fire and was restored by another fire, and who followed Him at last to a cross of his own. I think of Stephen, the first of us to fall, his face like an angel's, praying for his murderers as the stones fell. I think of Saul, the worst of our enemies, struck blind on the Damascus road and made the greatest of the heralds, the chief of sinners who never got over being forgiven. I think of Lydia by the river, and the jailer trembling with his sword, and the Ethiopian rejoicing in his chariot, and the multitude no man can number. And in every one of them I see the same hand at work that found me by the Jordan when I was young and thought that I was finding Him—the seeking, saving, sovereign mercy of God, that lays hold of the unworthy and the unwilling and the dead, and will not let them go.`,
`The vision John received closes with a promise and a prayer, and I can find no better words to end my own account than the ones the Spirit gave him. The Lord says, "Surely I am coming soon." And the church, the whole great company of the ransomed—the prodigals brought home, the dead raised to life, the sinners clothed in white, the nations gathered around the throne—answers Him with one voice across all the years and all the lands: "Amen. Come, Lord Jesus." So I lay down my pen, and I wait, and I watch the sky, as I have watched it since the cloud first took Him from the Mount of Olives. The light that drew me to Him by the river on the first day has never once gone out in all the years since; it burns brighter than ever now, at the very end of my days, and soon—soon—I shall see His face again, and the wounds in His hands, and I shall know fully, even as I have been fully known. Until that day, I tell what I have seen, that whoever reads it may believe; and that believing, he may have life in His name.`,
`And if any who read this should ask what they must do to share in all that I have told, how they too may be washed in the blood of the Lamb and stand at last among that countless multitude before the throne, I will give them the same answer the apostles gave the trembling jailer, the same answer that saved the dying thief, the same that found me by the Jordan when I was young: believe in the Lord Jesus, and you will be saved. Not by your works, not by your worthiness, not by any goodness of your own, for you have none that will stand, and neither had I. Only come to Him as you are, a sinner with empty hands, and trust the One who came to seek and to save the lost. He turned away no one who came to Him in all the years I walked beside Him, and He turns away no one still. The door He opened with His own torn flesh stands open yet. Come in, before the night falls, and you too shall see His face, and know, as I am soon to know, that He is faithful who promised.`];
